      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>BRIDGE PARTY DRESS.</head>
        <p>A BRIDGE PARTY DRESS.</p>
        <p>IBridge party dresses form a part of every woman of fashion's outfit. These, says the Globe, are hardly distingurdiable from smart dinner gowns. Them may be a little suggestion of neglige about them, but it must be very little. ; One for a lady who plays often is of Gobelins I blue miroir velvet. The skirt is full and em- broidered round (the hem with chenille and silver ; tissue, with a suggestion of blue in it. The !sleeves are rucked and reach to below the elbow, ; where are cuffs of chenille and silver chiffon, !turning up from fine narrow ruchings of soft creamy- tinted Valenciennes lace. The bodice is ; open in a heart shape,' and has a little double f quilling of lace all round; bordered, outside, ! with a scalloped embroidery in silver tissue and ; chenille.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SMART EARLY SPRING COSTUME.</head>
        <p>SMART EARLY SPRING COSTUME.</p>
        <p>A smart little costume for early spring wear is here depicted. With the help of a good pat- tern the design would not present many difficul- ties to the home-worker. It would look par- ticularly well carried out in a light grey tweed. The skirt is arranged with a narrow panel in front, otherwise it fits closely round the hips, and flows out well towards the hem, where it is trimmed with two rows of braid, which finishes on each side of the panel with looped ends through a steel buckle. The same idea is car- ried out on the shoulders, the material being tucked between the two rows of braid. The slight fulness is drawn in at the wait, where</p>
        <p>-':•. ' • • ' ';'. ,•;‘,. ta• ( ( i , ~I.!e •,..._, .t.\ "ii , • ~.,,,...: ‘ ..\\\,, \\.\,,,,,,,,,. ,i„lit /,, ‘ 0 •,.,,,. , , • ~•,. , .......- • L.„, ..7',!1•:-.,,,,,,,,..,,,,',',,k'.,, - .:\ • I / _ I, 1!):. .A\ .</p>
        <p>the coat is attached to . deep stitched belt of the tweed. A short' basque finishes the coat . behind, the joining line being hidden beneath a belt of braid. The smart little waistcoat is of no white cloth, adorned with a double row of troall steel buttons. The sleeves are of the three-quarter length, and are trimmed with braid to correspond with the rest of the cos- tume. GIRLS IN SOCIETY. Girls, writes "A Society Butterfly" in M.A.P., will be again to the fore in nefi. It is curious to note how often the affairs of life go, as it"-were, by contraries. At the time of King Edward's accession everyone predicted a Court and a society marked by its Modern tone and its extreme brilliance. But somehow events havo shaped themselves in an opposite direc- tion. and the London world seems to have steadied down to a dead level of respectability. The rikhts of girlhood aro carefully respected, chaperons have returned to favour, smart mar- ried women take a. back seat, and the "new ~ hostess, with her cotillons and fancy frisks, has given place to the old-world matron with her boy-and-girl dinners and dances. " time rt- -11038 " is rather at a eFscount in the twentieth century.</p>
        <p>(</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>TO-DAY-'4 RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY-'4 RECIPE.</p>
        <p>PIGEONS WITH VEAL FORCEMEAT. Prepare four pigeons for cooking. Stuff them with a forcemeat of breadcrumbs, parsley, and finely minced real moistened with the yolk of an egg. Bind some thin slices of fat bacon over the breast of each pigeon, and brown in a saucepan itryery fipt butter. Pour off the butter, and fill up the pan with strong stock, so as to completely cover the birds. Add a few button mushrooms, half-a. dozen small onions, a little salt, pepper, a piece of thin lemon•rind, and finally a tumblerful of Burszundv. Let the viveons stew slowly for about halt-an-nour, reduce the sauce, thicken with a tittle cornflour, and add a spoonful of crab-apple jelly. Pour the sauce over the pigeons, and serve en s bed of savoury rice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>CRUEL V_llPTaTiOik\,,k,.</head>
        <p>CRUEL V_llPTaTiOik\,,k,.</p>
        <p>Murderer would not carry away with him such evidence of his crime. Both Police Constable Dobbs and the gamekeeper had searched the grounds but Maria felt they could not have done it effectually ; the fence which the Park on the west, side was no great distance from the spot where Norton's body had been found; just within the fence was a row of thickly-planted spruce trees, and Maria'a theory was that the murderer clambered over the fence at a spot just before these trees began and flung the revolver from hint; if so, it would have fallen in the narrow space between them and the fence. ' Miss Mackenzie was not in the least above' trespassing when it suited her purpose, and she decided te search the space at .the roots of the spruee trees thoroughly ; but there were two other points on -which she could by no means make up her mind—should she go alone to the search and if she found the sought-after weapon should she take it direct to Lord Alandale's laWyer, or keep it in her own custody till events had gone a little further? She did not want a companion, because the latter might betray her to her father, but Would the prosecution believe in her unsupported testimony ? At length she decided to-risk it. It was. not as though she were a relation or close friend of the prisoner.; she might be called, in a manner, one of the opposite' side, so that her evidence must be considered disinterested. '</p>
        <p>She had to wait several days before the chance came of carrying out her plans, fox though Rockmount was only a short walk from Alandale, she could not count on the expedition taking less than an hour, and for many reasons it seemed wisest to go' after dark, when there would be less chance of her meeting any of the villagers. As Mrs. Mackenzie hated to be alone, and loved her daughter's company above all at-hers, it was hard indeed to manage, what Maria wanted, but the opportunity came at last. Her father and mother were going to a dinner party at a little distance given by friends, who, having no young people Of their' own, invited only married couples and did not include grown-up sons and daughters. Mr. and Mrs, Mackenzie started before seven and would not be home till past eleven. Here was the chance for Maria. Miss Mackenzie had not the least intention of being discovered and recognised by anyone she chanced to meet, and took her own pre- ' cautions accordingly. The previous Christ- mas she had taken part in some theatricals, M which she represented a very precocious Eton boy, and the suit she had worn on that oc- casion was still in her possession. Her hair was a difficulty as the wig had only been hired, but by dint of combing it up to the top of her head and then securing it with many hairpins, it would go comfortably under a large-sized sailor hay.and not betray her sex. Miss Mackenzie took' • a look at herself in the glass and decided she Made quite a presentable boy. "It's a deal of trouble to take for a man who's almost a stranger to !lie and is never likely to be anything else," she told herself, but all the same she was bent on her Under- taking. Maria Mackenzie, had ' an intense love of fair play, and from her father's comments on the Alandale murder it seemed to her that the young Earl was not likely to get it without her help. Maria would have liked nothing better than to have 'called on Mr. Fielding and give him her opinion as to the lines the defence of Lord Alandale ought to take, but even she did not see her way to do that, considering she had never spoken to the young lawyer in her life, and she cOuld hardly approach him through his father-in-law -since, as the Mackenzies were rigid dissenters, and the ironmaster would never have suffered the kindly old Rector to enter his house, Maria 'had no acquaintance with Mr. Amot. _</p>
        <p>, The 'handsome young . Etonian (Maria looked the part remarkably well) found, as was to be expected at that hour, the lodge gates closed, but he had no difficulty in vaulting over tIA fence close to the spruce trees, and, lighting a small lantern thought- fully brought from Rockmount, he began the search.</p>
        <p>It took longer than Maria expected, for she was so anxious to succeed she would not leav an inch of ground unexamined, but at last her efforts were reward&amp;l ; she found not only a revolver, which had been skilfully hidden between two trees, but a large handkerchief, which, though discoloured now by exposure and rain, her quick eye saw at once to be of good quality and texture. The girl was utter- ly devoid of fear and what are called by ,fine ladies "nerves," but even she trembled just a little as she examined her spoils ; the hand- kerchief was too large to have belonged to anyone but a man; it had a hand-embroidered monogram in one corner, but owing ,to the in- tricacies of the design Maria could not decipher the letters. " It's not A or K," she said jubilantly, "so as the monogram does not stand either for Lord Alandale's title or his Christian name I don't see that anyone could pretend that it was. his."</p>
        <p>There was the revolver, but Maria .dared not waste more time now. She rolled it up hurriedly in the handkerchief and started for home at her fastest speed, only reaching' the sanctuary of her ,own room five minutes before the round of carriage wheels announced the return of her parents.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mack:2nzie came into her daughter's room presently to say good-night, but by that time Maria had discarded her masculine get- up and Put oia a soft white dressing-gown ; the mother's anxious eye thought that she looked tired and careworn. " Is anything the matter, dear ? "</p>
        <p>"Nothing. Did you have a nice evening ?" " Rather a .painful one. /No • one talked of anything but the Alandale murder, and somehow, Maria, I can't bcar to think of that. Maria, the more I hear abOut it the more troubled I4cel about Lord Alandale."</p>
        <p>" You mean the people to-night thought him guilty ? " "My dear, everyone thinks so. Someone said that even the Rector who had known the Earl all his life was so sure of his guilt that he signed the warrant tor his arrest,"</p>
        <p>That's rubbish," said 'Maria briskly, " I beg your pardon, mother, but INir. Arnot's signing that doesn't mean that he thought Lord Alandale guilty ; a magistr;ittt can't allow himself to nave itelimo. Why. if I robbed' a house ad the pee applied to pa, he'd be bound to give .a wumiut for my arrest."</p>
        <p>s " 'Well," said poor Airs. Mackenzie, "it seems a bad business. They say Mis Jardine is seriously ill with grief and frOit." " I suppose she feels it's her fault," said Maria simply ; " she must, know perlectly that if she hadn't met poer Norton by stealth Lord 'Alandale could never iftive been accused of his murder."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Florence Flodgkinson,</head>
        <p>Florence Flodgkinson,</p>
        <p>Author of " TINSEL AND GOLD," E,c4</p>
        <p>CHAPTER Xv.</p>
        <p>IF ever fate were against a man in every single particular it seemed against the Earl of Mandale now. Of the seven magistrates who composed .the local Bench four were known to be keen radicalS, of the type whb preach the doctrine one law for rich and poor, and who, so far from taking a man'l birth, ances- try and position as favourable circumstances likely to deter him from the crime, have a: sort of positive eatisfaction when a ..better-class prisoner is brought before them in treating him more harshly than if he were a humbler - offender. Weil, of the seven magistrates, the three who represented the other side: were Mr. Artiot, Sir John Leigh, and Lord Alandale himself; the latter, being perforce absent it left only two voices on the Bench favourably disposed towards Kenneth, while the four "Progressives." were there in full force. They-were all moneyed men, One was a retired manufaCturer, the &amp;her three had risen from the ranks, and .one of -,them, Mr. Mackenzie, had an extra prejudice against Lord Alandale 'unknown to his colleagues. He was a rich iron master with one only child, a daughter, and it was the ambition. of his life to marry her into the aristocracy. Though he affected to despise titles, to see his Maria a real live "my lady" was the supreme ambition of his life, and it had seemed to Mr. Mackenzie that Kenneth Jardine was cut out for Maria. He had birth and .a prospective peerage; she had money enough to buy a new estate when Alandale was 'separated from the title and to provide a revenue not unworthy .of an Earl. Just before the old lord's death Mr. Mackenzie employed a friend to " sound " Kenneth Jardine on the subject of Maria, without exactly 'offering Miss Mackenzie for the young man's acceptance, the friend being empowered to state the amount of her dowry and to ex- plain that her father would not object to a needy son-in-law provided he was heir to a title. Kenneth understood perfectly all that was implied but left unsaid,; he told_ the " friend cheerfully he was not a marrying man, he should not take a Wify till -he hap- pened to fall in ;love ; when he did that he shouldn't consider whether the young lady was penniless or an heiress so that he could love her?' After that the new Lord Alandale was cordially detested by Peter Mackenzie, though they so seldom met except on magisterial was quite unaware oi cited. Mr. Arnot bthuesinaenssuntohasittyKleiennehtand e however, Was perfectly aware of it, and he knew that in Mr. Mackenzie the young .Earl had a judge bound to take an unfavourable view of things. The evidence was purely formal. The witnesses summoned to attend the inquest were again called, and gave much the same testimony, ...only that Mr. Fielding elicited</p>
        <p>from Dr. Graliam a positive statement that the shot must have been fired 'before mid- night ; the prisoner on his lawyer's advice, reserved his defence .and was formally re- manded for a fortnight. Two things followed. that mud' excited the inhabitants of Alandale and Thornby ; two bills were placarded every- where, one offering a reward of a hundred pounds for the weapon with which Norton had been murdered, the other promising five hundred for information concerning any. stranger visiting Alandale village and grounds on the night of the murder, provided that it was sufficient to accomplish his appre- hension. "That's a faille Move," said Peter Macken- zie in ,the bosom of his family; "there may • have been a dozen strangers about on that evening who had nothing whatever to do with the murder." "Pa," said Maria suddenly, believe Lord Alandale did it ? " " I trust I know my duty better than to .express an opinion while the case isstill being • tried," said Mackenzie pompously," but it is impossible to deny that appearances are very much against him. Maria subsided then, for she was just a little afraid of the ironmaster, but alone with her mother she made up for her temporary silence. " never believe he did it, ma, was her frank outburst. " I've known Lord Alandale ever since we came to Rockinount, three years ,ago, and I don't believe he'd hurt a fly." Mrs. Mackenzie was limp and spiritless ; .her husband had early crushed her into 'out- ward submission, but he had never been able to alter her nature and way of thinking. Mrs. Mackenzie was never heard to express her opinion publicly, but in her heart she held the old-fashioned doctrine of "looking up to her betters," Church and Queen were very dear .:to her, and she had such a reverent admira- tion for Lady Mary Jardine as always to order her own clothes in humble imitation of her ladyship's. ' Depend upon it, Maria, there's a terrible mistake somewhere," poor Mrs. Mackenzie confessed to her daughter. "But your pa would be precious angry 11 he heard me say</p>
        <p>"do you</p>
        <p>"Lord Alandale never did it;" repeated Maria with an air of conviction. Why, you've only to read his cousin's evidence ' (there had been a special edition of the Thont- by Times devoted almost exclusively to the Alandale murder); " he was going to help the miserable wretch, had actually promised him a hundred pounds to make a fri:ge start .some- where else; he wouldn't have troubled to do that if he mealft to kill him." Mrs. Mackenzie shook her head. " I daresay you are right, Maria; you .had always a wonderful head, even as a little tiling. But my dear, your father's a clever real and never fails in anything- he's set his mind on. Ho says he means to see Lord Alan- :tale convicted, that it will do the aristocracy good to have one of their members dragged into the mire, and, depend upon it, he'll do it,</p>
        <p>Maria; I never knew him to 'fail in anything yet." ! Miss Mackenzie did Mit • argue with her mother, but she made up her mind her father 'slainkl not have things all his own way. This Wrl had a wonderful fund of 'determination and a strangely keen sense oi justice ; if Mr. Mackenzie had been content to do his j duty s a magistrate without bringing personal ! spite into ,the case his daughter might have left vell alone; but, as it was, Maria decided that if her .father was lighting against the ! prisoner it was clearly her duty to strike a blow in his service; so she said nothing to anyone, but made up her mind to do " some- thing" to help Lord Alandale. A High' School education, plenty of Mother wit, and natural shrewdness had, made Maria Mackenzie a very able practical young woman; if she had needed to earn her living she would have succeeded wonderf4ly well as a lady-detective; not requiring to work for money she was now about to undertake an amateur detective job on her own account. She had read every word in .the. papers— London and local—about the Alandale murder ; she had enjoyed the advantage of I hearing Mrs. Martin's sentiments an opin- ipns, the old woman being One of ' Maria's *2ecial protegees, and she jumped to a con- Jusion which was as shrewd as it was con- • :Ausive—namely, that the best way to serve Lord Alandale was to bring the guilt, home to someone else, and the first step towards that end was to find the weapon that had killed Norton.</p>
        <p>Maria knew the scene of the murder per- fectly, and her belief (after thinking ove, filings long and earnestly) was that the tevulver would be found near the spot, for she felt certain in her own mind ,that the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHAPTER ',VI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER ',VI.</p>
        <p>IT was not till her mother had left her and silence had settled down on Rockmount that Maria Mackenzie dared to examine her discovery. She locked the door, lighted two extra candles, and placed them on a small table, then with hands that trembled just a little M spite of herself, she unrolled the handkerchief and disclosed the revolver. Peter Mackenzie's• fortune had been ac- quired so late in life that he had never taken to such sports as shooting: no gun, revolver or pistol had ever been possessed by him. But since leaving school Maria had paid visits</p>
        <p>to prosperous families where such weapons formed part of the sons' Aquipment and she had seen quite enough to know that the revolver She had found behind the spruce trees was neither an expensive .one nor up to date in its character. It was, in fact, rather antiquated and could have been purchased anywhere seccindhand for a few shillings. , she turned it over and over in her long capable hands quite suddenly she saw the name of a firm engraved , close to tlle handle with one other word beneath it, and then the truth flashed upon Maria, the name indicated not .the Makers of the revolker but only the firm who had sold it, and the N•orc.l below,, hourne,',' meant that they carried on business three. -</p>
        <p>Norton had been .mnrdered in cold blood with arevolver ; Maria Mackenzie had picked up this revolver near the scene of the crime and it bore. the 'name of a MeAbourne firm, To the quick-witted girl there was, but one explanation; Helen Jardiae, the beautiful heiress of Alaridale, was ,the only creature in the neighbourhood known, to have lived in Melbourne; she, it was- openly admitted, had held several meetings with the dead Man ; to Maria it was clear aS daylight Helen was the murderer, '</p>
        <p>I Maria Mackenzie Was what people ca 1 strong-minded," but the horror of her discovery almost broke down her composure. She had often seen Miss Jardine walking or driving. about the country lanes, she had , admired her with *the open-hearted admiration of a generous mind, she had fancied, more than once that the heiress looked sad and troubled and had wondered what grief could assail the mistress of Alandale, now it seem.ecl she had . the clue to all. Miss Jardine had been in Norton's power ; alarmed at his repeated demands on her, stung pm haps to frenzy lw his threats, she had taken his" life.</p>
        <p>"Poor girl," Ond Maria's pity was deep and true. "I reckon she's' sorry enough now ; .I've been told that when the ‘verdict a 4 the inquest was given she looked ready to faint. I've seen her before now walking with Lord -Mondale and looking as though she minded nothing when he was by her side-----and now he's suspected of ' her crime; she must wish she'd killed herself rather than have btought the suspicion on him. " I meant to take the revolver to the police to-morrow and tell them just where and how I found it, but 1 can't do it now,, it will be like putting a rope round Helen Jardine's neck. Lord Mondale is innocent and per- haps 'they'll find it out and not send him for trial ; anyway, I must wait till things have gone further before I do anything. . I don't say but what if the Earl is condemned to death I'd not tell the truth, but I can't speak it now for if once suspicion falls on Helen 'Jardine nothing. in the world can save- her: I almost wish now I had left well alone and not meddled in what didn't concern me."</p>
        <p>Methodical in many ways, the ironmaster's daughter took from her wardrobe a half-sheet of brown paper and placed on it the hand- kerchief and revolver, making a neat parcel of the whole, which she secured with string and sealing wax, finally writinr, oh the outside the date of the month and her Own name ; the 'parcel she locked up in one of her drawers, feeling that there it was impossible for it to be tampered with in any way. But even then, though one o'clock had struck, Maria Mackenzie did not attempt to go to bed. Amon,' the many expensive presents lavished on her by her father was a typewriter, which she had taught herself to manipulate with care and correctness; she put‘ in a sheet of paper and sat down now to type a letter, or something whDch resembled one, F though it had neither beginning nor end.' "Lord Alandal9 is innocent, and the writer of these hnes can prove ,that he is so. To do this now would be to bring about a terrible catastrophe, so silence will be maintained for the present. It is waste of time for you to offer a reward for the revolver with which the crime was committed or to search for it; it has been found already, and will only be produced when there remains no other way of saving the Earl from a death of shame."</p>
        <p>If I post that note to-morrow to Mr. Fielding," reflected , Maria, " I shall do two things: make him understand it is of no use for him to hunt for the reVolver and convince him that it has been found—the date written on the parcel being the same as that on this note he will be dmvinced, when -I produce the former, that the revolver was really discovered at the time I say."</p>
        <p>She put the communication into an envelope and typed the address, then she got into bed and slept as 'soundly and peacefully as though she had had no exciting experiences to trouble her. _ _ - .</p>
        <p>The next day at breakfast Miss Mackenzie announced her desire to go to York shopping, and her father, who could deny her nothing, offered his company. He would drive her to the station and accompany her to the city ; there he could find plenty to amuse himself while she bought her finery. " I'll give you a ten-pound note to spend," he sal&amp; graciously. " I daresay you haven't much left of your allowance, though it's only a month after quarter day." Maria took the money—to refuse it would have given displeasure—and she spent a whole hour buying trifles at a large draper's in Coney Street, but the real object of her trip to York was accomplished beipre she left the station since she had slipped into the pillar-post there her letter to Mr. Fielding. He was at his own home in York when it reached the Rectory, but Mr. Arnot forwarded it with scrupulous care, and Joe opened it when he was alone in his own study, thinking rather gloomily of Lord Alandale's second appearance before ,the magistrates—now only two days distant. The ,fortnight's respite had nearly expired,' and though Mr. Fielding had worked his hardest he had discovered nothing; he was not one whit nearer proving his client's innocence than he had been before the remand.</p>
        <p>And what was worse,-- his • partner, Mr. Maitland, a lawyer of great experience ( Joe was only the junior in the firm) was dead against the prisoner. "Of course, as he is an Old friend of Mr. Arnot's you couldn't refuse, to undertake the Earl's defence, but I wish to goodness the business had never been offered us," said Mr. Maitland testily. "You are bound to fail and we never have taken up criminal cases, they are not in our line." _ _ .</p>
        <p>NNW/ do you think Lord Alandale guilty ? " " Because it's impossible to hit upon anyone else likely to have murdered Norton. You'd do much better service, to the Earl and his friends if you persuaded hut to plead guilty ; it could be put that Norton said something insulting ,about I\liss Jardine, and the Earl, stung to fury, in a moment's passion fireci the shot. They'd bring it down to man- slaughter, and he'd get off with a light • sentence."</p>
        <p>" I can't do that, Maitland. I'ye known Alandale, man and boy, for years, and I'd otake my life he is innocent." " Well, I suppose you must have things your own way," grumbled his partner, " but you'll never get him off, even if you brief all the best counsels of the day." And with those wcrds ringing in his ears Mr. Fielding •went home, to find Maria Mackenzie's letter. There was a ring of truth about it which impressed him strangely, used as he was to receive anonymous communi- -1 cations. . _</p>
        <p>A woman wrote that," he decided, "and got it typed lest her writing should betray her, Of course, I have no proof, but to my mind there's only one woman who could have r written it—Helen Jardine." He sat for a moment lost in 'thought, then he brought down his clenched fist on the table with a bang. . I"l've got it at last. Miss Jardine fired .the shot herself and this letter means that, sooner than her cpusin should suffer for her crime, I she will conibss her guilt, but that if I can get •Alandale off without her help she'd prefer to retain her freedom. I distrusted that woman the moment• I saw her, but I didn't think she was as bad as this ; it's easy to see with half ?an eye that Alandale's in love with tier—hp love with a murderess, poor fellow 1" .</p>
        <p>- . (To be continued.)</p>
