      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Royal Movements.—The King will stay at Biarritz for about three weeks, and from there will visit the King of Spain. There are to he great celebrations in Madrid in honour of King Edward's visit, after which, WS Majesty will join the Royal yacht at Cadiz or Lisbon, for a cruise in the Mediterrans.n. It is expected that the Queen rod Princess Victoria will apcomparty the King on this cruise, paying a visit to the King and Queen of Greece at Athens. In the mean- while the Queen will spend most of the time quietly at her beloved Sandringham. Of all places that is the one for which the Queen has most affection. Nor is this surprising seeing how nearly it is associated with her married life. during which her Majesty has seen not only the building of the house stables, and kennels, &amp;c., but the making of the whole Of the terraces, gardens, and landscape. Almost daily, the Queen goes her rounds visiting the kennels, the model farmyards,the hothouses and the rose gardens. All the garden walks have been most carefully laid down at Sandringham and afford the cleanest walking, even after very wet weather. Biarritz.—The King's choice of Biarritz for his stay in the Mediterranean has attracted a large number of people to that popular resort. It is modern and , fashionable, and has always been a favourite resort for English visitors. The King is staying at the Hotel de Paris, which commands very fine views, and another fashionable establishment is the Hotel Victoria, where the Duchess of Abercorn,. Lady Alexandra Hamilton, I and many other distinguished visitors, have, for the time being, taken Up their abode. Biarritz is famous as the place where Napoleon first met the Empress 'Eugenie, witlla resided their with her mother—the Countess de Montijoy—until Far marrirge in 1853 Since then the place has changed from an insignifican. village into a town of splendid mansions and hotelst but it is still keeping up its reputation as the courting placq of monarchs, for, if the King of Spain did not first meet Princess Ena of Battenberg at Biarritz, it was there that the arrangements for theirmarriage were completed. The Ladies' Gallery.—The many new members in the House of Cammons are naturally anxious to entertain their lady friends and acquaintances at Westminster, and seats for the Ladies' Gallery are eagerly sought , after. The suggestion that the grille,' which shuts off that gallery from the House should be removed,has been revived,but nothing ever comes of these attempts, beyond providing members with a subject for humorous sallies. The new Parliament is full of reforming zeal, but it has already decided that it can do nothing with that "grille." The wife of the Speaker—who comes from an old Parliamentary family—is privileged to invite her friends to the Speaker's Gallery from which a much better view of the House may be obtained. Her invitations are therefore much more prized than a seat in the Ladies Gallery, which one member has said reminded him of a lion's cage, and to another it sug- gested a " chamber of horrors." The Women Suffragists and the Premier.—The women suffragists are determined not to be put down, although Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman was ill in bed with a severe cold, about a dozen of these ladies called at Downing-street the other day, to present their demands. The circumstances were explained to them, but this did not satisfy the deputation, and after insinuating that the Premier was only pretending, one of the ladies stated that sa soon as Sir Henry was better, " they would call and call upon him until he was compelled to. satisfy their claims." The Prime Minister has there- fore a lively prospect in view, with the possibility of having to abandon his official residence, or be compelled to go in and out disguised as a charwoman. There is a good deal to be said for Woman's Suffrage, especially for the class who are ratepaying, householding, or property-owning women, but this method of presenting the case is not likely to appeal to the sympathetic con- sideration of Sir Henry Campbell-Bannerman. The Lenten Season.—Although Lent has put a stop to some of the balls and gaieties, it is a fact that the season is being far less strictly observed in London than has been the case for several years past. The people who were accustomed to clothe themselves in sombre black, or at best in half-mourning hues, have grown fewer and fewer, and, by the great majority it is no longer deemed wrong to go to the theatre, or play cards during the Lenten weeks. But although there is less outward show among Society women, there are many evidences.of practical denial this Lent. Several Leagues have been formed, the members of which are bound over to deny themselves something—new frocks, jewellery, holidays, or something they would like, yet is not absolutely necessary to their well-being, the pro- ceeds of these" denials " going to the hospitals, or some good cause, The Elephant Charm.—While the Prince and Princess of Wales have been in India we have heard much about, and seen divers pictures of the elephants connected with the Royal tour, which probably accounts for the vast array of elephant charms, to be seen in the shops, and dangling from bracelets and nezkche ins, at the present time. The animals may be bad in ivory, bog oak, china, silver and gold, and are supposed—by believers in fetishes of this description—to bring good fortune. .</p>
        <p>Veils.—Keen spring winds work havoc with our complexions, and good and becoming veils are really an excellent investment. When buying a hat the fashion- able milliner invariably brings forward a veil to match. which by the way may be all one could desire, but is always an expensive item. The more economical plan is to purchase at drapery establishments some extremely fine cobweb net, which should match exactly the hat with which it is to be worn. Then any good hair- dresser will supply black velvet "beauty patches," two of which should be affixed to the net when the hair is arranged—one under the left eye and the other at the left corner of the month. Whatever colour the veil may be, the" beauty patches must be black, and about the size of a threepenny piece. These veils are very fashionable and most becoming, and, purchased in the manner described, have much to recommend them on the score of economy. Dressing on the Conti act System.—The women who habitually anticipate their dress allowance might con- sider the advisability of adopting the new contract system. The idea was started in America, by the proprietor of a large drapery establishment vvho,instead of making his wife a dress allowance, agreed to provide her with an inclusive y.nrly outfit, according to a cer- tain style, and standard of quality. This arrangement. proving satithctory, the idea was carried further, and extended to the gereral customers. For a fixed stim paid quarterly they can dress according to the class they pay for much the mine as one can purchsse a com- plete wedding tronssenu, or have a flat furnished upon inclusive terms, according to style and quality. Some of the London houses are now offering faculties for this contract system in dress expenditure. .7iVo doubt they prefer it to giving long credit and it will be interesting to see how it works out for the customer, who,however should benefit by obtaining better value for the money expended. Miss Hnrraden's New Book.—We have learned to expect great things of the author of " Ships that pass in the Night. " &amp;c.. rind in " The Scholars Daughter " (Methuen) Miss Harmden has not disappointed us. The scholar—whose life is :devoted to the making of a new dictionary—is Professor Grant, a grim learned man, so grim and learned indeed that his young wife speedily discovered that it was impossible to live with him. The daughter is Geraldine, a charming girl, who is brought up to belieire her mother dead, and is allowed no girl friends, indeed no woman is permitted to cross the threshold the "staff " consisting of elderly men servants. There is much of humour, and much of pathos in this touching story and it is no exaggeration to say that few authors could have handled the subject In so ablea manner. MADGE</p>
        <p>Three wagonloads of clothing and provisions,supposed to be the proceeds of robber:es, were discovered during a police raid on Wednesday night in the for( ign quarters of Brownlow-hill and Ainsworth-street, Liverpool,</p>
      </div>
