      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION CIIIILLENGE CUP.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CIIIILLENGE CUP.</p>
        <p>FIRST ROUND.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. *Broughton R. 1 414 Barrow 1 1 5 *Batley Salford Leigh 2 0 4 *Swinton 1 0 2 *Tinll *Victoria R (11'fd) 0 0 0 Widnes 0 A 0 *York 1 1 5 Wigan *Brighouse R'gers 2 3 13 Morecambe 0 0 0 *Egerton Oldham *Hull Kingston R. 0 14 12 Dewsbury 1 1 5 *Halifax *Leeds *Featherstone R... 2 416 Brookland li'v's 1 1 5 *Keighley 2 3 13 Eeremont Bradford</p>
        <p>Warrington Beaten by a Point. On Saturday Warrington were drawn to play Batley at Batley in the firat round of the Northern Union Cep, and they made the journey in high hopes of evengina fvor at i°ttingndeelfeats at the hind.; of the" gallant, youths." able, Y. anything but the beet team was avail- Fish, Barrie. Lees, Boardman and Shugars all being on the injured list. A change was made at the last minute owing to TAOS being :unit Brooks going half- back and Lloyd wing. During the week the weather bad been very wet, but on Saturday it cleared up splendidly, and Warrington with their 500 suporters made the journey in brilliant sunshine. The Ratleir around proved to he in very fair order, and a crowd of 7,000 people assembled. Batley were at frill strength. Mr. J. Priestley was the referee and the teams were as follows : Batley.—White. back ; W. P. Davies, F. Loosemore, y• T. Davies, and W. L. Turton, three-quarter backs ; I; Oakland and Cookson. half hacks; Settle, Fryer. Ozzard, James Wade, Hodgson, Beaumont, Tebbutt,and efts forwards. n'arrington.—Berry, back; Lloyd Isherwood, Dickin- pin, and Taylor. three-quarter backs: Brooks and 'ocken hall, half backs ; Preston. Belton. Heath, "armer, Swift, Jolley, Thomas, and Morrison forwards. Touch judges, Messrs. J. 11. Holdsworth, Halifax, and G. Gibson, Keighley. Riatley won the toss and Preston started operations gainst wind and slope. The opening exchanges were 411 favour of the home side, and Taylor had to save the situation near the Warrington line. A short forward cleared to the quarter where Warrington were ,ienalised. Wattle Davies took the kick and landed a tine goal from a wide angle amid frantic cheering. War- rington rallied gamely, and Hockenball made a char- acteristic burst down the centre of the field. Batley had to defend and smart passing by the Warrington !Alves and centres nearly brought about the home !Ram's downfall. Preston took a Penalty shot at goal Vld was very neat the mark. Warrington kept up 1 .p Bro ;_'ne pressure and another fine round of passing Dickinson and Isherwood left 4401"doks, Hockenhall, in the rear and his centre had hatleP"nt to the line where White saved. morolZern completely overplayed at this stage and a dashed between later fonnd themselves in arrears. Isherwood Lo Hockenh 11 osemore and wno eluded the defence very trickily and scored a'fine try Davies and handed to near the astiTis.eaThet scorer took the goal-kick and missed adding an y sce points. The home side now took up the running w and Oakland punted across to Davies who was pulled up smartly by Taylor. Warrington endeavoured to clear bat Thomas was off-side and Davies kicked a second goal which gave Batley the lead again. Quickly the game Was taken to the other end and Preston tried at goal but Was a yard short. White returned with n lofty kick and Lloyd obstructing Berry the Warrington line had a narrow escape. Taylor was tackled near the line and from the ensuing scrummage Oakland picked up and dropped a goal. Half-time arrived a minute later with there: Batler,3 goals (6 points) ; Warrington, a try (3 points.) Oakland resumed and play was hotly contested. Twice zrooke passed out to Hockenhall who knocked on unaccountably, and then for a long time the Warring- ill backs never got a chance to combine. The home mR kept the ball close and wasted much time by loose Aking, while at every opportunity the ball was sent e touch. Berry played a great game for Warrington liatl saved several intended touch kicks. Once he tined to the corner, where Dickinson lias just beaten Possession by Tutor). Some keen scrummaging iwed but Batley got the ball and by use of the touch. reached centre again. Warrington returned and a i .rt passing bout close in resulted n Isherwood dash- :g headlong over the line. Warrington's rejoicings pere premature for the referee refused to allow the try. ~reaton had two more penalties sod at last recorded a Roal. The game now became desperate and Warrington .ttlere very near wiping off the odd point. Batley i'ended stubbornly and were penalised again for off Rlde in the last minute. Theangle was not a bad one 111 Rd Preston took the kick amidst dead silence. The all soared hi gh,towards the goal but passed just outside t.the Posts and a great shout proclaimed the fact that lalitley had won. Final : Batley 3 goals (0 points), Warrington 1 goal 1 try (5 points). Runcorn Lose at Hull. Hull entered the cup-ties iu rare form, and they met nunoorn on the Boulevard before a crowd of about /3,000 spectators. It was a slow and unexciting game in</p>
        <p>One of the greatest factors for comfort in the house is to have the needful work done in the best manner and in the shortest time. _</p>
        <p>There is no help to compare with Fels-Naptha soap towards furthering these ends.</p>
        <p>That is if rightly used: not as other soaps are used, but in an easier way fully 'xplained in the simple directions with every bar. With Fels-Naptha soap</p>
        <p>don't use any clothes-rotting -chemicals nor skin destroying Powders. Let Fels-Naptha cleanse</p>
        <p>and purify in the comfort- able, easy, rapid and absolutely safe way directed. Fels-Naptha 3; Wilson street London EC _</p>
        <p>its early stages, but all the points were made before the interval. Capital work was done by Jolley and Lewis, and then came the opening score as a result of ,smart plavlby Freer, the ball being grounded over the line by Major. The attempt at goal failed. Within ten minutes Burchlll by a grand, well-judged shot landed the ball over Harrison's head, and Pullen being first in the race for possession was able to score a try in a fairly good position, but Goddard again failed to improve it. During the second half Runcorn made a better show, and were often dangerous. They were greatly weakened by the withdrawal of Harrison,the full back, who had injured his shoulder, Result 2 tries (6 points), Runcorn nil.</p>
