      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>EX-POLICEMAN'S STORY ON OATH.</head>
        <p>EX-POLICEMAN'S STORY ON OATH.</p>
        <p>Eczema For Twenty-Five Years—Cured at Last by Zam-Buk.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. Denton, of 64, Albion Road, Great Yarmouth, a retired metropolitan police constable (St. Luke's Sub- division), testifies to the astounding effect of Zam-Bnk in oases of chronic skin disease. The full facts were obtained by a "Yarmouth Mer- cury " reporter, and Mr. Denton afterwards felt so- strongly that his remarkable experience should be received as absolute fact by the publics that he made the following Sworn Declaration before a Commissioner for Oaths :</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>"SWORN DECLARATION.</head>
        <p>"SWORN DECLARATION.</p>
        <p>"I, Charles Denton, of 64, Albion-road, Great : Yarmouth, a retired metropolitan police constable, solemnly and sincerely declare on oath as follows "Twenty-five years ago I commenced to suffer frOm eczema, for which I was treated by private doctors and 1 by the physicians at a well-known hospital. One of the divisional surgeons declared that this form of eczema was incurable. I endured great agony from the itching , and pain, notwithstanding the ordinary ointments Which I were given to me to use. "Three months ago I heard of Zam-Buk, and, getting some, tried several dressings of the balm. Perseverance 1 with Zam-Buk, to my surprise, effected a complete cure. I My skin is now perfectly healthy, every trace of ths disease, thanks to Zam-Buk, having disappeared. "I give the Zam-Buk Company full permission to publish my statement in the hope' that it will benefit others, and I make this solemn declaration' conscienti- I onsly believing the same to be true and by virtue of ' the provisons of the Statutory Declaration Act, 1835. I</p>
        <p>"DECLARED at Great Yar- mouth in the County •of CHARLES DENTON Norfolk this 2nd day of February, 1206, before me, A. TAYLOR HARRARD, Comntissioaer for Oaths."</p>
        <p>"At first," said Mr. Denton to the pressman previ- onsly, " it was thought I had caught some loathsome disease from the dirty people I had to handle, and by whom I was often bitten. "My hands swelled up to a great size, and sometimes got so hard that I have rasped the lumps down with a small file. I couldn't open them, and all the creases of my palms would burst open into wide cracks, which bled very much. The irritation was intense. "I used to show my hands to the Divisional Surgeons and other doctors whom I was constantly meeting in my duties." The disease was called "filthy eczema" and was authoratively pronounced incurable by many. One police surgeon showed me marks of the same disease upon himself, and said : "If I could cure this, don't you think I would? It isn't to be cured." I went to I St. Bartholomew's, but the verdict there, too, was 'No cure.' My hands got so bad that I couldn't hold a prisoner properly, and as it defied all kinds of treat- ment, I had eventually to leave the force, unfit for further service. "I lived in enforced retirement for nineteen years, and found no way of escape from my sufferings during all that time. About ten weeks ago I tried Zam-Buk, the eczema being so bad just then that I would have tried anything that came my way. Two or three dress- ings with Zarn-Buic made them quite easy, so I got a large box. Perseverance vajth the balm effected s com- plete cure, and my hands ate now natural in appearance, soft, and strong." Zam-Buk is indispensable in work- shop and home.</p>
        <p>Warrington Rural District Council The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers, on Wed- nesday when there Were present Mr. Brooks (presiding), Dr. Murray, Messrs. 0-. Mather, R. Forshaw, W. Houghton, J. Backhouse, P, Longton, J. W. Knowles J. C. Sutton (clerk), S. Sutton (assistant clerk), F. W. Kerfoot (inspector), B, S. Newcombe (surveyor), and W. Saphton (medical officer). Mr. Sutton said it was proposed that calls should be made noon the parishes for special expenses for the coming half-year in the various parishes : Buttonwood £6O, as compared with £BO, the amount of the last call; Penketh £l4O agailist £180; Poulton £5O against £4O; Rixtom £4O againt £2O Great Sankey £3O against £35. The general call for the district had never been below sd. in the £ but, owing to the considerable balance which they had in hand, the Council could if they liked reduce the call to 4d. (Hear, hear.) The only difficulty in regard to such reduction was that when they had to go back to the normal call there was a great outcry about increas- ing the rates. The call"for the Union would be reduced tby id. in the £. It was decided to levy a rate of 4d. in the £ for the district, with the addition of the special expenses in the various parishes.—The Medical Officer reported that daring the month there had been 20 births and 33 deaths. The death- rate was equal to 35 per 1,000 as against 40.0 the average of the past three months and 38.9 for the corresponding period of last year. Twenty-two of the deaths occurred at the County Asylum exclusive of which the death-rate for the district was equal to 119 per I,ooo.—Mr. Mather remarked that he had had a conversation with the Chairman of the County Council Highways Committee who stated that when- ever a discussion arose as to the state of the roads, the Warrington District Council was always set up as an example. Their roads being the best in the 1 county. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BREACH OE THE FACTORY ACT.</head>
        <p>BREACH OE THE FACTORY ACT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday John Roberts 35, Pinner's-brow,was summoned for employing a child under the age of twelve years contrary to the provisions of the Factory Acts.—Mr. Augustus Lewis, H.M. Inspector of Factories, prosecuted, and stated that in February his colleague called at the defendant's boot repairing shop and there found a lad eleven years of age at work. It appeared that the lad had been•cmployed after school since Christmas to run errands and finish off the boots.—John Mather, Pinner's-brow, stated that his mother cooked meals for the defendant and he was in the habit of taking them. He was interested in the work and asked to be allowed to do something. Defen- dant sometimes gave him a penny for running errands. —Mr. Steel, on behalf of the defendant, admitted a technical offence. The lad only worked because he liked to help a little but in the strict sense of the word there was no doubt that be was employed.—Here the magistrates intervened and said that as the offence was purely a technical one they had decided to dismiss the car on the payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
