      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>EFFECTS OF . COLD ON SEEDS. Some important experiments on the effect of cold on seed ht are described by Mr. J. Adams in the Scientific Proceedings of the Royal Society of Dublin. The seeds experimented upon were peas, barley, flax, swede, red clover, mea- dow fescue, and timothy grass. It was found that 65 to 96 per cent, of dry seeds germinated after twenty-four hours' immersion in liquid air. Moist seeds, after a similar immersion, utterly failed to grow. It is suggested as an explana- tion that in the latter case the formation of ice crystals in the seed ruptures the tissues, and thus causes the death of the protoplasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>INSLCTg AND PAIN.</head>
        <p>INSLCTg AND PAIN.</p>
        <p>Th. following remarkable observation of the great naturalist Fabre seems to indicate that insects are not very sensitive to pain. A species of solitary wasp store S up the bodies of hive bees to nourish its larva. It catches the bee on the flower, and if it finds its captive full of honey, squeezes out the sweet liquid, and drinks it before storing up the body for its young. This bee-catching wasp, however, sometimes falls a prey to another inseot, the mantis. Now, on one occasion, Fabre saw a wasp which had just seized a bee, and was squeezing out the honey, itself —seized and• held fast in •the saw-like fore- legs of a mantis. And as the latter held the wasp transfixed, and was gnawing its under- parts, the wasp went on licking up the honey squeezed from the beef</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>• LOWEST TEMPERATURE KNOWN.</head>
        <p>• LOWEST TEMPERATURE KNOWN.</p>
        <p>In the northern hemisphere the greatest cold seems to 'Ave been observed at Werchojansk, in Siberia, wheie it is stated 'that the thermometer gpea down as low as —698 deg. C. However, according to the information which has been brought by the Russian artist Borrissoff, certain parts of Nova Zembla seem to shew at least as low a temperature as the above. The Bulletin of the Societe Astronomique states that in an excursion which M. Borrissoff made in the Strait of Matotchkin, he discovered underneath a case a box containing two thermometers, one a maximum and the other a minimum-recording thermometer. It is supposed that these instru- ments belonged to Hofer, an Austrian geologist, who made an expedition to this spot in 1872. One of the. thermometers was found to have registered the temperature of +l5 as a maxi- mum, while the second instrument &amp;hewed that the greatest cold had been —7odeg. C. This value seems to be the extreme cold which has been reached in this region for thirty years past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>VALUE OF MICRO-BIOLOGY</head>
        <p>VALUE OF MICRO-BIOLOGY</p>
        <p>The bacteria and other microscopic forms of I plant and animal life, all of which are conveni- ently included under the term microbes, have so lately begun to be understood and appreciated, remarks the Scientific American, that we must still emphasise their extreme importance. The discoveries of the botanists and zoologists and revelations of the microscopists in this domain are comparable, in their importance to public health science, with nothing less than the reve- lations of the telescope to astronomy. Astro- nomy had indeed existed long before the inven- tion of the telescope, and public health science had its beginnings nearly a century before any/ ecinsiderable progress had been made in micro- biology. But it, .is .not too. much to say that the developments in rilicro-biology since Pasteur I began his work have not only revolutionised our ideas of the nature of the infectious' diseases, but have also placed in our hands the key of their complete control.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>FLIGHT OF THE 'FLYING FISH.</head>
        <p>FLIGHT OF THE 'FLYING FISH.</p>
        <p>Does the flying fsh 'really fly, or is the so- called flight a' mere extended leap, in which the fins are used on the principle of the aeroplane to float or rest on the air, and so afford support to the body? The question has been much discussed, and many naturalists have denied it the power of true flight. Mr., Frank Bullen, who, in his many voyages, has had unique opportunities for observing the flying fish, is emphatic in the asser- tion ihrsi it does really fly in the proper sense of i the word. He has seen t, for example, change its course at a sharp angle when an obstacle in- tervened, and when about to re-enter the water he has seen it rise and continue its course on see- ing an enemy below. Some observations contri- buted by Lieutenant-Colonel Durnford to the "Annals and Magazine of Natural History" confirm this view. The writer contends that the ordinary aeroplane theory involves a mechanical impossibility. The true explanation is an in- tensely rapid vibration of the wing-like fins--a 'vibration which becomes apparent to the eye as it slows down when the fish reaches the water.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>CYCLE OF 'METON.</head>
        <p>THE CYCLE OF 'METON.</p>
        <p>There is a period, known as the Cycle of Meton, says "The Story of the Sun," which plays a very important part in the several re- lations between the sun and the moon. It can be shewn that the exact length of 235 lunations is 6,93969 days. It also appears, by simple mul- tiplication, that nineteen years, reckoned each at 365.25 days, amount to 6,93975 days. As the former of these periods is only abouta an hour and a-half shorter than the latter, we are justi- fied in saying that nineteen years is almost exactly equal to 235 inflations. From the al- rnanack-maker'spoint of view the Cycle of Meton has a. distinct importance. It tells him that in nineteen years from any date the moon will have made a number of complete revolutions around the sun, and therefore the age of the moon, on any particular day of the year, will ho repro- duced on that day nineteen years, or, more pre- cisely, after an interval of 6,939•6 days. Thus, for instance, on the day that these lines are the being l m moon 28rteer back hilB93 penned—that is, old. March 28th. 6.9396 days we come to March 28th, 1874. In this interval the exact number of 235 lunations have been performed. The age of the moon will therefore have been the same as on the same day of the year nineteen years later.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>INSECT PESTS AND THEIR ENEMIES.</head>
        <p>INSECT PESTS AND THEIR ENEMIES.</p>
        <p>IThe protection of fruit trees from their insect ; enemies has been in recent years practised on I scientific lines in the Hawaiian Islands and Cali- fornia. Lady birds imported from Australia and ' New Zealand proved efficient in destroying the ; aphides and scale insects which were ruining the fruit crops. ' Mr. Compere, who is continuing this work for California, holds the view that every insect pest has its destroyer Ly which it might be kept in check. This has led to investi- gations in various parts of the world with the view of finding the natural destroyers of portion- Jar pests. The codling-moth, for example, had done much damage to the Californian apple trees, and in the course of his travels Mr. Com- pere found a district in Spain where the codling- , moth occurred, and yet did only slight damage. , Examination shewed that it was-kept in check by an ichneumon-fly. Pupas of this fly were, there- fore, obtained and sent over to California. They ' were allowed to hatch out on the infected trees, ' and they at once set to work on the caterpillars. ' The method sometimes adopted to introduce the desirable alien is to send a young infected tree to. the country where the enemy has been dis-/ , covered. It returns with a stock of the de- I stroyer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>HISTORY OF HELIUM.</head>
        <p>THE HISTORY OF HELIUM.</p>
        <p>Helium, the supposed product of the disinte• gration of the atoms of radium, has had an inte- resting history, says a Globe writer. Its existence was first made known by the appearance of cer- tain new lines in the solar spectrum. As these lines did not correspond to those of any known substance, the presence rf a new element in the sun was inferred, and it was appropriately named Helium. And after having been known as a solar element only for some twenty-five years, helium was found on earth as the constituent of a rare mineral. Tt is now known as a rare but rather widely-diffused terrestrial element. Helium is a gas, and it ESCAPES INTO THE ATMOSPHERE'</p>
        <p>fix:Ml a ntimber of mineral springs. Yet helium . . is not a constituent of the atmosphere. In a similar way the considerable quantities of hydro- gen which escape into the air can scarcely be do- teeted. It was these facts which led Dr. John- stone Stoney to his remarkable theory that these gases escape from tli.3 earth's atmosphere into outer space. Rising by their 'lightness to tilt upper layers, the• velocity of the path of their atoms is so great, that they are able to escape the control of gravity, and wander off through solar or stellar space until attracted by some larger body. Thus, particles of helium end hydrogen from the earth may finally reach the sun. The helium whose PRESENCE IN THE SUN caused the appearance of the unknown lines in the originally have come from, selrretht7cilie tlien"Zirrent view of the relations between, radinn; and helium be correct,. then the existence of the latter in the aim implies also the presence of the former. The radium of the sun Is apparently changing, into helium. But is it in so doing, augmenting the heat-giving powers of the luminary? Th,se who have eagerly seized upon the radium of the sun as a means of m- imic-6'l-m. its life ir "^nrs"riPrv'e demands have hitherto been 6ontent with the moferbeioflaly t of its presence, they have not stopped to inquire whether the vast stores of energy de- yelened by the change is transformed into heat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>eir, Saturday, March 10, 1906.</p>
        <p>accept a general condemnation of the tree planting scheme until the former resolution of the Council has been rescinded.</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley: Trees have been planted since our last meeting without his sanction. Mr. Woodward: That being •so the trees can be removed.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker k I would like to know whether or not it is a fact that trees have been planted in positions in which they are dangerous. Mr. Swinton: At one time there were but they have been removed to my satisfaction. In answer to Mr. Woodward Mr. Swinton said he did not approve of the recommendation of the Parochial Committee, as he did not like trees to be planted where houses abutted on the footpaths. Mr. Woodward: Then in your opinion these are not fit places for trees to be planted? Mr. Swinton: That is so. I refused to allow them to be planted but the Parochial Committee took the matter out of in hands. lam against the trees being planted there. (Mr. Whitley : "Hear, hear.") Mr. Linaker pointed out that the District Council was liable for any trees which had been planted, and ought to satisfy themselves that they were not allow- ing anything will&amp; was objectionable. He moved that I the Highways Committee take the matter in hand and that the question be deferred until the Committee pre- sented a report. Mr. Hallows said he was going to propose that a special Committee be appointed. He wanted an unbiassed Committee and he would certainly object to the Highways Committee as at present constituted. He did not want to state any reasons but he thought that if a specia Committee wereappointed it would meet all objections. He did not propose at that time to refute the statements which had been made some of which were confounded lies.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker : I think the Highways Committee has the complete confidence of the Council. (Hear, hear.) Mr.-Baxter thought the Highways Committee would be more acceptable than any special Committee which was appointed. Mr. Whitley: Can trees be planted opposite a persons window?</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker i I object to a continuance of the dis- cussion on those lines.</p>
        <p>Mr. Linaker's resolution was then put to the meeting and approved. The Highways Committee is constituted as follows: Messrs. Woodward, Pilling, Wright, Baxter, Linaker, Harrison, Antwiss, Jones and Whitley. Starved by lin2ernia</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
