      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>Fortnightly Meeting.</p>
        <p>, The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Bari o Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Mr. A. R. Norman (presiding), Niessrs. C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Byes, P. Mountleld, T. Baxter, H. M. Davies, C. E. Livaker, J. H. Davies, J. Smith, T. Wright, S. Woodward, W.Harrison, G.Pilling, S. Horton, J. L. Jones, C. Horton, J. Fryer, J. Spruce. W. M. Morris, P. Collins, T. Wood, J. Douglas, J. W. Temperley, J. Rutter, J. Hallows, J.Whitley. R.Billing- ton, C. E. Linaker, jun., W. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, T, Antwis, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk),De Prez, Cook and Pearson (relieving officers), and J. Cunnah (workhouse master).</p>
        <p>THE COST OF VACCINATION. The St. Giles Camberwell Board of Guardians for- warded the following resolution and asked thatithe Board should support it: That the Local Government Board be requested to take steps at an early date to promote legislation for the repeal of compulsory vaccination failing which that they be asked to take steps to promote legislation to place vaccination officers under the entire control of the Guardians, and thereon that such officers do institute proceedings for non- compliance with the Vaccination Acts only with the consent of the Guardians. That payment of both public vaccinators and vaccination officers be by salary, and that the fee system be abolished. That longer time be given to conscientious objectors in whicljio apply for exemption certificates, and that the means of obtaining such certificates be simplified by providing for the con- scientious objectors making a statutory declaration before a justice of the peace, and forwarding the same to the vaccination officer.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. Antwis thought the resolution should be "sup- ported. It was decided to refer the question to the Parlia- mentary Committee.</p>
        <p>BOARDING-OUT OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the hon. secretary of the State Children's Association stating that the Associa- tion was interested in the promotion of the boarding out of Poor Law Children belonging to the Union and offering to supply the Board with a list of vacancies in the event of their deciding to board-out their children. Mr. Norman suggested that the matter should be deferred until the Board had decided whether they would board-out the children.</p>
        <p>Linaker : Will it ever he settled? Mr. Ashton: I was instructed to call a meeting on a day when there was no other business. Mr. Spruce moved that a meeting of the committee appointed to consider the question (consisting of the whole of the Board) be called to go into questions at ten o'clock on the next Board Day. If the matter was mach longer delayed the country members of the Board would be very busy and could not conveniently attend the meeting. Mr. Spruce's motion was carried. MR. ASHTON'S SALARY. Mr. Baxter moved that Mr. Ashton he paid £lOO for his services during the year as clerk to the Assessment Committee. This sum was the usual amount paid to Mr. Ashton. Mr. Woodward seconded and the resolution was I carried.</p>
        <p>COST OF OUT-RELIEF. The Finance Committee reported the payment for out-relief to Mr. Cook £7O, Mr. de Prez £3O, andfMr. Pearson £24.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce reported that there were at present 151 persons in the House as compared with 143 twelve months ago.</p>
        <p>THE PARLIAMENT COMMITTEE. "CONVENIENT FOR SHELVING MATTERS." Mr. Morris asked when the Parliament Committee met. He had never heard a report of a meeting of the Committee nod he rather thought that to refer matters to that committee was an easy way of shelving subjects which they did not want to deal with.</p>
        <p>Mr. Norman said be took it the Clerk used his dis- cretion as to when the Committee should meet. Mr. Ashton: Reports have been given, but certainly not very often. Mr. Morris : Men I want a question shelved I move that it be referred to the Parliamentary Committee. Mr. Ashton explained that be waited until business had accumulated to such an extent as to justify tho ,calling of the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <p>TREE PLANTING AT STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL TAKES ACTION.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Mr. Hallows and "Confounded Lies."</head>
        <p>Mr. Hallows and "Confounded Lies."</p>
        <p>The question of tree planting at Stockton Heath again gave rise to considerable discussion at tbe meet- ing of the Runcorn Rural District Council on Tuesday. There is now, however, every prospect of this much vexed local question being finally settled as the High- ways Committee of the Council was instructed to visit Stockton Heath and inspect the trees with a view to ascertaining whether or not they constitute obstructions. At a special meeting of the Stockton Heath Parochial Committee held on February 2lth the request of the Tree Planting Committee to Le allowed to plant trees in Heath-street, Chapel-lane, Dandonald Avenue and Gaskell-street was considered and it was resolved to recommend the District Council to allow the trees to be planted in the streets named. Mr. Woodward said that before the Council considered that minute he thought they should hear read another letter which he had received from Mr. Hough, White- field-road, Stockton • Heath, upon the question. Mr. Hough said : '1 feel exceedingly sorry it is necessary again to call your attention to the reckless way in which the Highways Acts are being defied in Stockton Heath, by the tree planting " faddists." Since my previous letter, calling your attention to the above, two trees have been placed upon the footpath within a short dis- tance from the gate to the house of where I reside and are both obstructions to pedestrians and ultituatelv would obstruct the light and view from the house if allowed to remain. Upon objecting to the placing of these obstructions to the party placing them there, I was informed your Surveyor had given them authority to do so. I now respectfully ask you to give instructions to your surveyor to have them removed forthwith and save me the unpleasantness of issuing summons for compulsory removal. May I respectfully suggest that your Council supply your surveyor with a bona-fide tape "so that all reports as to measurements with regard to obstructions and widths of footpaths may be correct."</p>
        <p>Mr. Pilling moved the adoption of the Parochial Committee's recommendation. The footpaths in the street named were better than those in the main roads and he could not see that the trees:would be obstructions. Mr. Hallows seconded.</p>
        <p>..... —.— Mr. Roylance said he had been in Stockton Heath the previous day and found that in the streets where permission was asked to plant trees the trees , had already been planted and had been there some time. He spoke with respet,t to Heath-street. Mr. Pilling : None had been planted prior to Saturday week. Mr. Roylance : They are in to-day and here we are asked to give permission for them to be planted. Mr. Pilling : They were planted because the Parochial Committee gave their consent. Mr. Whitley rose to move an amendment. He main- tained that it was the right and duty of the District Council to protect their public roads, and to stop or prevent the obstruction of any road where such obstruc- tion was likely to be detrimental to the interest of the district. It was, he contended, the duty of the district Council to prevent any unlawful or unnecessary encroach- ment upon any road or footpath within the District. He took it that was their duty under the Local Government Highway Act which gave them the power , to direct legal proceedings and to take any steps which 1 were necessary to carry the Act into effect. When a right of way over any road or footpath,which the public had enjoyed, was obstructed, it was their duty to take proceeding to have the obstruction removed. To do that they had three courses open to them. They could (1) direct their surveyor to remove the obstruction, , (2) indict or charge with crime any person causing the obstruction, or (3) proceed by way of action in the name of the Attorney-General. Where the public right of way was so clear as it was in the case before them he believed the Council should, without any delay, direct their Surveyor to remove the obstructions, or—and this would probably be the best action—give due notice to the persons caus- ing the obstructions calling upon them to remove them. If that was not done their Surveyor could remove the trees and the persons planting them could bring an action for trespass against the District Council if they questioned their right. In the terms of Mr. Hough 's letter any individual had a perfect right to abate any nuisance or obstruction which, as in the ease of trees with their objectionable guards and most dangerous spikes, prevented him passing freely along the highway' In that matter a great responsibility rested upon the District Council and a legal liability for accidents caused by the trees also rested upon them. Acci- dents had happened and were of almost daily occur- rence. On Saturday, Sunday, Monday, and that morning he had had complaints of accidents due to the presence of the trees. The trees ought to be removed from the footpaths in Fairfield- road. Gaskell-street, Walton-road,Whitelleld-road, Dun- donald Avenue, Heath-street, Kimberley Drive, Chapel- lane and other nariew places. If the 3 were allowed to remain at least one lamp should be placed near every tree, lighted from one hour after sunset until one hour before Sunrise so as to prevent people from damag- ing themselves and their property,the cost of the light- ing to be borne by the persons planting the trees. If any damage was done the Council was liable to be pre- ceeded against for such damege and it might turn out 1 to be a terribly costly and serious matter. Mr. Swinton rightly and wisely objected to the trees being planted upon narrow footpaths and refused , to take any responsibility upon himself. Trees were being 1 planted without Lis` authority: persons near whose houses the trees were planted did not want them; and expensive footpoths, put down at the cost of the ratepayers and property owners were being torn up and , spoiled and the material wasted and lost. He moved "That , no trees be allowed on any footpath in Stockton Heath 1 less than eight feet wide, and not nearer together than 20 yards. That trees so placed without Oa authwity of the Highways Committee be immediately' removed, and that where trees are planted the footpaths be made good again to the satisfaction of the Surveyor, the expense to be borne by the persons who caused such trees to be planted." 1 Mr. Woodward: This District Council is already committed to a certain degree with respect to the exist- 1 ing trees. Mr. Whitley: On reasonably wide footpaths. Mr. Woodward: We have already instructed the Tree Planting Committee that they may plant trees in situa- tions approved by our Surveyor. Your amendment is not consistent with the action which the District Coun- cil has already taken, and before you can move it you must rescind the former resolution of the Council. _</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitley : Trees are being planted to-day. Mr. Woodward I cannot help that. The trees have to be planted in positions approved by our Sur- veyor. Bring cases where trees have not been so planted, and the trees must be removed. Otherwise the Council is committed to the scheme. Mr. Whitley : The Surveyor told us at our last meeting that trees were being planted without his consent. Mr. Woodward: That may be but you must bring individual cases and the trees can be removed. I cannot</p>
      </div>
