      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>LANB PRO3LEI.</head>
        <p>THE LANB PRO3LEI.</p>
        <p>Address By Captain Crasfield.</p>
        <p>The-taxation of land values formed the subject for discussion at the meeting of the Warrington Junior Liberal Association held at Gregory's Cafe on Monday. The proceedings were uader the presidency of Mr. E. Osborne, and were well attended. Captain CEtOSFIELD, in the course of his address, said the land question was as old as the hills. It Was the riddle which the Sphinx demanded civilization to solve and those nations who failed to grapple with the pro- blem would be swept away by the Nemesis of cause and effect even like the mighty empires of old. Moses, the great law-giver of the Jews, saw that the real cause of the enslavement of the masses of Egypt was the posses- sion by a class of the land upon which and by which the whole of the comitunlty must live. He said, " The land is the Lord's and you are only pilgrims on earth," and he not only provided for the fair division of the land among the people, and for making it fallow and common every seventh year, but by the institution of the jubilee he provided for a redistribution of the land every fifty Years thus making monopoly impossible. Tiberious Gracchus, the great Roman Tribune, likewise recognized the importance of the land question. In one of his patriotic speeches he exclaimed,"tiomans you call yourselves masters of the world, but you do not own a foot wide of its soil. The foxes have their holes,but you have not where to lay your head."(Applause.)These warn- ings,however,were utterly neglected and very largely in consequence of this the Roman Empire, the mightiest Empire the world had ever seen, crumbled and fell to the ground. The German people who succeeded to the debris did not recognize private property in land, but were soon introduced to the idea by the Church, which, while promising the people a kingdom in heaven, took care to grab their little all on earth. This land ques- tion had played a great part in history, had overthrown dynasties and had caused revolutions. They would be purblind fools if they refused to learn from history. They imagined, however, that they were more prosper- ous than ever. They quoted their exports and their income tax returns with the pride that smacked of a shopkeeper nation. They thought of the railroads they had built, the commerce they had created, the beautiful mansions they had erected. They thought of their glori- ous freedom which allowed them to do what they liked and to say what they liked and did not trouble their heads as to whether what they were doing was worth doing or what they were saying was worth saying. In short they were blinded by the magnificence of their successes, and failed to see the canker which was gnawing away at their very vitals—town life, slum existnce, the herding together of men, women and children, in con- ditions far worse than those of many a savage; 80 per cent of the nation now lived in their towns, 30 per cent in what might be called slums. (Applause.) Well, then, what was the remedy? "Back to the land," some people said, and forthwith cried for protection to make that possible. Well,if they could possibly get the great bulk!baek to the landjt might be xvorth whi I e considering even such a severe remedy as the one proposed, but as that was manifestly impracticable they must seek another solution, and that was to make their towns decent places to live in, places that would not cause the physique and the morale of the nation to degenerate. How could they do that? By the prevention of overcrowding, by making land cheaper and reducing the rates. And how could they do that?—by the taxation of land values. He put that remedy first as it was the subject of his lecture, but they must not imagine that that remedy would alone suffice. The people must be taught how to live in a town.' a very different matter to living in the country. They must be taught the value of hygiene and cleanliness. They must learn more self-restraint and above all a truer patriotism must arise in their midst; they must work more each for all and all for each. If that spirit was more abroad they would have manufacturers less callous and corporations less care- less about the black volumes of smoke that made day hideous. How then were these lessons to be taught? At school? Yes, by all means, but the impressions there obtainad were too fleeting, they must be taught in the great:national school of a national militia aswas advocated by thet National Service League. He brought that in because importas,t as was the land question he con - sideredthe question of universal training of far greater importance both because it was an insurance against war, and because of its good effects physically and morally. (Applause.) He hoped, therefore, they would give him an opportunity, of putting his views on this subject before them at an early date. (Hear, hear, and applause). Let them return to the land question. What they wanted was a share of the value which had been given to the land by the community. The result of that would be that people who now held up land in order to get a huge price for it at some later date, would have no inducement to do so because of the high taxes they would have to pay. Land round their cities would come cheaply into the market, rents would be lowered, large open spaces could be bought by our Town Councils and the abominable over- crowding would gradually be abolished, Further, the income from the taxes on land values would make : possible the reduction of rates and taxes. Of ,• course,there were people who would object to anything,l some people because they liked to be cantankerous and others because their pockets would be touched. The landowner said "Why should you wish to tax land ' when it ghee up in value? If you do this you : should tax interest on capital." That was an argument which was not difficult to answer. Speculation in stocks and shares did not necessarily adversely affect 1 the community,but speculation in land was the cause of much of the suffering of our slams. as Henry George I said in a powerful passage, "of our women who by the thousands are bending over their needles or sewing machines, thirteen, fourteen and sixteen hours a diy ; of these widows' straining and striving 'to bring up the little ones deprived of their natural bread winner, the children that are growing up in their squalor, and wretchedness, under-clothed iunder-fed, and uncter-educated." (Applause.) What they aimed to do, therefore, was to check speculation inland, and in doing that they were convinced that they would prevent much of the wretchedness which prevailed in their midst. As n result of theat measure it was evident that houses would be more plentiful, better and cheaper, that more employment would be provided in the build- ing and allied trades, that it would be easier to estab- lish factories in new positions where work could be carried on more economically, that the mining industry would be stimulated so that they should get cheaper coal and iron and stone. Many other advantages would follow which were succintly put forward in the pamph- lets which the audience had in their hands. That then was what they, as Libel-els, had got to push. They had something here solid and tangible, something which if brought about would make life brighter and happier for thousands of poor wretches. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Mr. OSBORNE said the land qnestion was certainly one with which the present G ..vernment would have to deal—and deal with in an adequate manner. Personally be believed in land nationalization but he was not such a pur-blind fool as not to accept something in the mean- time—(hear, hear)—and the taxation of site values was something to be going on with. U •der the English constitution land was held either for public purposes or to be cultivated and improved. That was not done 1 because the holders of the land had nothing to pay if they allowed the land to be idle. Moreover if the tenant improved the land it was the landowner and not the tenant who reaped the benefit. That would have to be changed not only for the individual but for the municipality and the State, so that if land improved in value those responsible for the improvement should reap the benefit. The taxation of land values would help forward the better housing accommodation for the poor and would help to remove a great many of the social evils which existed. (Hear, her and applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. OKEL said he was plelsed to hear that Mr. Osborne was in favour of the nationalization of the land. The system of the single tax would operate in that direction as if land values were taxed the land would virtually be the property of the nation.</p>
        <p>An interesting discussion ensued in which many members took part. At the close Captain Cros9eld and Mr. Okel were heartily thanked for their attendance. Captain CROSFLF,LD in replying accepted the invita- tion of the President to address the Association on " Universal Military Training."</p>
