        <p>MARCH 10th 1906.</p>
        <p>In reply to a question pat THE ALIENS ACT by Mr. RAMSAY MAC- AND ITS nois-ALD,the Home Secretary</p>
        <p>WORKING. . - stated that eighty-nine aliens were refused permission to land in this country during the month of January, that being apparently the latest month for which the figures are complete. There had, said Mr. GLADSTONE, only been three appeals, and he added that only two of the rejected aliens came from Russian ports. These last two statements,we take leave to think, are of no value whatever. The alien who is turned back at the ports does not in general know .that he has a right to appeal, and as the official who turns him back is, as it appears, in most instances, ignorant of his language, there is really no means of conveying to him any idea of his privilege under the unfortunate Act which was passed by the late Government. What is remarkable, therefore, is not that there should only have been three appeals, but that there should have been any appeals at all. Then, as to the rejected aliens who come from Russian ports, it may reasonably be assumed that a Russian port is almost the last place from which a political refugee is likely to come. He will be secretly con- veyed from one place to another before he is able to take ship for a land where the political refugee has hitherto found the ports opan to him and safety ahead.</p>
        <p>The fact that only 89 aliens were turned back in the month of January strikingly exposed the futility of a piece of legislation, the professed object of which was to turn back the flood of alien immigration altogether. The object of the Act is now explained as being to turn back the diseased, the criminal, and the pauper, but the Aliens Bill was produced in response to an outcry against_the competition of foreign labour. Certaiß districts in the East End of London, it was said, were being filled up by Russians and foteign Jews, who worked at such cheap rates that they took the bread out of the mouths of the English workman. It was to appease those frum whom this complaint arose that the late Government brought in its Aliens Bill, and though it found that it could not, without revert- ing to such acts of injustice as were never per- petrated in the country since the persecution of the Jews in the Middle Ages, do even a tithe of what was demanded of it, it did not cease to pre- tend that the Act which it eventually succeeded in passing against all good advance,was a measure of protection for the English workman. What now appears is that even if such protection Were advisable, the Aliens Act does not afford it. The exclusion of 89 persons is comparable only to Mrs. PARTINGTON'S efforts to sweep out the Atlantic with a broom. On the other hand, whilst producing no real benefits for anybody the Act deprives this country of one of its proudest and most honourable distinctions. There is no doubt that patriotic men, fleeing from persecution at home, have been turned back from our shores perhaps, because the new bureaucracy which we have established is too dull or too unedu- cated to understand its own duties, but with results that are lamentable to. contemplate. In driving back political refugees who have not £5 in their pockets,and who do notunderstand that they have a right of appeal, we are in all probability handing them back to death or a cruel and laborious exile. This is what we are prepared to I do for the poor privilege of turning hack 80 or 90 aliens monthly, many of whom are on their way to other countries. Englishmen,we are convinced,will not permit the continuance of a system like this,by which it is only possible to reap discredit and national shame. The Act does not keep out the criminal, if he should have the proceeds of a suc- cessful robbery in his pocket. It does not keep out those who come here for the purpose of sub- sisting on vice, if they can show, as they can, of course, that they are possessed of more than £5; but it does with an almost fiendish cleverness, accentuated by bad administration, keep out those whom we should formerly have welcomed with open arms. This must not continue a moment after the time at which it is possible to provide a remedy. The Government must at once do what it has the power to do administratively, and must then turn its attention to such an amendment of the Act as experience has already shown to be necessary. Should it become known that even one political refugee has been executed or sent to Siberia because we have closed the doors of mercy in his or her face, the Government will be confronted by such a condemnatory outcry as it will not find it easy to appease.</p>
        <p>The smoke of the battle THE VICTORY AND is clearing. The whinings WHAT NEXT, of the Tories about rni3' representation, bad luck, or faulty organisation are dying away. Their linen ha 3 been washed in the sight of us all, and the unqualified surrender of Mr. Balfour to Mr. Chamberlain has arrayed the whole of the Tory party under the banner of Protection. It is a bold policy for the Tories, in face of the country's decision so emphatically pronounced in favour of ' Free Trade thus to burn their boats, and exclude</p>
        <p>themselves from power until they have converted the nation to belief in taxed food and restricted trade. but in this hour of triumph, Free Traders must bear in miad that the campaign is only open- ing. The first pitched battle has been fought„ and the Protectionists have been routed, horse and foot. The Tory general, Mr. Chamberlain, however, is not dismayed. He is rallying his routed forces, and announces his intention to carry on the war with unabated vigour. He has annexed Mr. Balfour, and has driven the small band of Tory Free Traders out of his ranks. The Tory party is therefore now Protectionist to a roan, and it must not be forgotten that if three-fifths of the voters of Great Britain at the recent election voted for Free Trade, two-fifths supported the Protectionist policy. It therefore behoves Free Traders to walk warily. A Government, especially an energetic and conscientious Government, in the course of five years will arouse many enmities, must inevit- ably commit mistakes, and, as we know from long and unvarying experience, will wane in popularity. The nation may be tired of the Government with- out abondoning Free Trade, and a swing of the pendulum may give the Tory Protectionists an opportunity of which they will not be slow to avail themselves. The Liberal Govern- ment and the Liberal party hold the sacred cause of Free Trade in trust, for it is for them to take care that it is not imperilled. It is, above all, essential to the cans of Free Trade that finance should b 3 re-established on a sound principle. Financial difficulty is Mr. Chamberlain's opportunity. Growing expenditure is his interest. If a permanent deficiency of revenue should mark the closing year of the pre- sent Parliament, and if, in order to establish equilibrium, an alternative could be put before the country between increased taxation on articles of necessity and duties on foreign manufactures, impossible though the latter may be as a financial resource, Protection might have a chance. An expensive army is the force by which Mr. Chamberlain might hope to carry the fortress of a general tariff. It is for a Liberal and Free Trade Government and a Liberal and Free Trade Parlia- ment to see that such opportunities are not offered.</p>
        <p>Everybody knows that the Tits COST OF remedying of the serious NEW SCHOOLS. want of school accommoda- tion in Warrington will be a lengthy and expensive project. It is, moreover, cne which cannot be postponed, or in any way shirked. The Board of Eclacation is insistent and Warrington has to build accordingly. Work has already commenced on the Beamont School in the Orford district, and the preliminaries are almost completed for the Bolton School at Latchford. In fact, plans and contract have been approved by the Education Committee and the Town Council, but a special meeting of the latter has been called by the economists to re-consider the matter. We understand that the cause of complaint is that the cost of the school does not conform with the basis of £lO 10s. per head which was laid down in the specifications guiding archi- tects and builder. If we are rightly informed the Bolton school will cost nearer £l5 per head than £lO, if erected to the accepted designs and con- tract. The builder's price alone is £11,418. It may be that the Education Committee thoroughly understands and approves of the necessity for such a big outlay at Latchford, but there is rni doubt that the Town Council, which is responsible for sanctioning the expenditure, is very hazy in its conception of the matter. More information is !requires, and there can be no harm in holding a special meeting to sea whether any economies can be effected'which will reduce the cost per head to a figure which will be generally accepted as reason- able. So many new schools will be reqnired in the future that it is imperative we should know exactly on what basis rigid economy can be combined with efficiency.</p>
        <p>Our Conservative contempo- Ma. CROSFIELD, M.P. rary made quite a commo- AND tion in its own circle last CHINESE LABOUR, week about the Member for Warrington. It was dis- covered that Mr. Crosfield had not been in his place when an important division on Chinese Labour was taken in the House of Commons, and nearly two newspaper columns were taken up in emphasising the new Member's perfidy. " Why." says our contemporary, "he should so soon in his Parliamentary career have flouted the electors is inexplicable.' It certainly would be if it were true, for, as 5,599 electors so well know, the last thing Mr. A. H. Crosfie!d is likely to do is to "flout " them, or in any way be careless of their interests. Possibly the "Observer's" six headlines and crushing indictment of the cruel wrong it had conjured up suited the tastes of those readers who in true Tory fashion _open their mouths and swallow gratefully all that lis put therein whether good, bad, or indifferent. Some few may have thought it odd that a responsible newspaper did not inquire at the ' fountain head why Mr. Crocfield was not in his place when this division took place. A satisfactory explanation was easily obtainable. Mr. Crosfield was busy "flouting " the electors on is back. Illness confined him to his hotel during that part of the week in which the debate on Chinese Labour concluded and the division was taken, or we doubt not that he would have been as ready as ever to cast his personal influence against the system of slavery under the British flag which he rightly abhors and has always condemned. We are not sorry to uote:that in championing the poor, "flouted " elector— which must be a novel experi. ence for the "Observer "—our contemporary alludes to the Chinese Labour question as the One upon which Mr. Crosfield was mainly returned. This strengthens the mandate he has received to do all in his power to end the indentured slavery\ in the Transvaal.</p>
        <p>The question of religious- TIM BIBLE IN teaching in the day schools</p>
        <p>rio SCHOOL. is week"by week coming to engage more attention. The Free Church section and the Socialists ask for no religious teaching at all, Mr. Crooks, on the other hand, has declared that if the Bible is kept out of the schools the Labour party will rise against any proceedings so iniquitous ; while Lady Wimborne is asking for madenominational religious teaching. Indeed, she calls all Evangelical Churchmen to a rally beneath this banner. The powers of Rome are looming darkly on one flank, while the forces of secularism are gathering on the other. The quickening influence of this discussion is to he welcomed. The writer of a hook just published, with the title " How to Teach the Bible," advises bevinning with "parts of Scripture which deal with Nature.' God speaking to man" is the next theme. The historical phase of the Old Testament is not insisted on; but justice in the home is to be shown through the patriarchs and in the State through the prophets. The idea of a Church is next to be defined upon generous lines. Then the child is to learn something of the teaching of the New Testament in regard to the affections and will, and to "view the beauty and strength of the single soul in whom dwelleth ' righteousness." Lady Wimborne, in the new Nineteenth Century, seeks to make Evangelical Churchmen realise that their teaching is not so ' far removed from undenominationalism as it is from that form which is known as"definite Church teaching" ; and that nothing is so vital as the maintenance of Protestantism and the freedom it secures. A significant point is, are all those secondary schools to be made familiar with snch a series of books as the Cambridge Bible for Schools and Colleges ?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WIDNES CHURCH IMPROVEMENT.</head>
        <p>WIDNES CHURCH IMPROVEMENT.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Consistory Court on Wednesday permission was asked by the authorities of the Church of St. Paul, Widnes, to complete the tower on the church and to erect therein a peal of eight bells and also a four-dial clock.—lt was explained that the total cost of the work would be £1,950. The cost of the clock will be defrayed by Mr. and Mrs.F. H. Gossage, of Wooltou,and the tower and bells will be paid for by voluntary contributions.</p>
