        <p>A ha. shown East</p>
        <p>Afri</p>
        <p>quaint three</p>
        <p>* * * rington has certainly come to the fore in the LI sense of late years and the success of local at various eisteddfodau has been a matter for Aulation. Some notable victories were achieved Earlestown festival on Saturday and the cator spoke most highly of the Warrington its.</p>
        <p>aditl-dit /oCalist</p>
        <p>have it on the authority of Alderman John that the painting of the market was never so well done as it is being done now. This is a valuable testimonial for the system of employing skilled labour direct, and Councillor Stott must feel gratified at the 84CCeS8 of his scheme. It has found employment for 14ie unemployed and given satisfaction to the employer * * " You are a sanguinary perverter of the truth," was an expression used at a meeting of the Normanton (Yorks) Urban Council. This is distinctly unparlia- mentary language. The recollection of the way an ordinary labourer would interpret the expression, sug- gests that the speaker must have been brought up in a tainted atmosphere. * * * Ladies as well as gentlemen will have an opportunity Crosfield of attending the complimentary dinner to Mr. A. H. on Saturda ' M-P., and Miss Crosfield at the Lion Hotel Y and next week. As a rule the members of the fair sex have to remain at home while the " lord and master " enjoys himself at these festive gatherin her rights, and gilisisbitt4tawnoimndainesitsioroapoifdlty cohefmseitng Does it bring woman's suffrage any nearer, think you fair reader?</p>
        <p>)bertsa'</p>
        <p>,41 mflSicaJ talent a s':)llgral at thr</p>
        <p>* * On Oolitic'llor Evans proposing the minete3 of the Health Committee several members wanted some information respecting aesophagostomiasis, the new desease which the Medical Officer of Health has had brought to his notice. Councillor Evans refused to be drawn, however, into either pronouncing the word or describing the disease. "If you want to know" said he "ask the Doctor." It was a loss to the meet- jug that no one took the advice, for the Doctor looked qUite willing to enlarge upon the subject if invited. *4* A small but effective illustration of the benefits of Free Trade is afforded by the match industry in Prance. Here the making of matches is a Govern- Irient monopoly, and all imported matches are heavily taxed. Worse still, the Government matches are Often of a very poor quality. Imagine an inhabitant !Pf our own free country having to endure the irritat- 'N experience of a certain M. Beauge, who recently 'ought a box of 100 matches and struck 44 of them Znsecutively without succeeding in getting a light. ihe remaining 50 he took to the offices of an enter- Prising morning newspaper, and in less than no time these also lay on the floor, sti nck but not kindled. s** Warrington is the home of many industries and riot the least important is the manufacture of gas stoves and meters. In face of all we have heard about trade depression, it was distinctly gratifying to hear at the annual meeting of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., on Thursday that the past , year had been an exceptionally busy one, in fact the difficulty had been to cope with the orders. The firm are finding it necessary to make use of land which they have acquired at Grappenhall alld here foundries and workshops will shortly be erected. The prospects are indeed encouraging and W e join in hoping for a continuance of the prosperity of an industry which finds employment for aby no means inconsiderable number of townspeople. * * * The Town Council loves administering a quiet snub to the Education Committee—that influential body which can increase the rates and is yet not directly ?nswerable to the ratepayers. Though the Committee has existed for three years without having complied with the law in regard to appointing one lady member, the Council became suddenly and virtuously indignant Po Tuesday and referred several appointments back in order that the Education Committee might nominate lt,to a woman as required by the Act. We have heard it 4.1? aitl that there are one or two old women on the corn- 1. 6 mittee already, but perhaps they do not fill the bill, so to speak. Anyhow, the Committee will have to cast about and find a member of the fair sex who can ktingly be nominated in the interests of education ," generally, and the large number of female teachers in Darticular. th..r * * • • As the now House is called "a hereditary chamber," ,atay be of interest to give a list of fathers and sons 'ae assist one another's deliberations. FATHER. SON. Chamberlain. A. Chamberlain. r Geo. Newnes F. H. Newnes. "' W. Be• n W. W. Benn. I. T '3 T. runner. J. F. L. Brunner. )t Chas. Maclaren. H. D. Maclaren. 04155511 Rea. Walter Russell Rea. 11. Schwann. C. D. Schwann. They are seven, and all save the Chamberlains are °ll the Liberal side of the House. * * * )3Tjae puke of Devonshire will have none of Mr. , %'rour s " if's " and " and's." Sneaking of the rernier's attitude on the Fiscal Question as die- in his famous letter to Mr. Chamberlain, the 4,111te said on Tuesday: "The human brain reeled ti‘othe effort to discover whether the main , proposi- -11:18 or the qualifications were of the most importance. ilitosaedvised them to use their own judgment. For th .11 the qualifications did not substantially alter A:2,14 tote 'lew which he had been compelled to entertain if orne time that Mr. Balfour was in substantial— t°at 9omplete—agreement with Mr. Chamberlain on lalect of Tariff Reform." rr.</p>
