      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE TRANSPORTER BRIDGE. The directors of the Widnes and Runcorn Transporter Bridge Company report that for the past half-year ended December 31st, the takings amounted to .£604 45. 10d. and there was a profit on the working of £ll2 Gs. 7d. SUCCESSFUL BAZAAR AT WIDNES. The Wesleyans Widnes of connected with the new and handsome church at Albert-road held a three days' bazaar last week, on Wednesday, Thursday, and Friday, to'reduce the debt on the now church and schools,which were opened a little over a year ago, owing to the destruction of the Albert-road Sunday school by fire in 1902. The total cost has been, including site, £6,713 17s. id. Of this, £2,619 115. 10d. has been raised. There is also a grant of £5OO to be claimed from the Twentieth Century Fund when the debt has been brought down to a certain figure. In order to claim this grant and fulfil the conditions of the Chapel Com- mittee, the bazaar has been held, and this, with generous donations received from Messrs. Gossage and Timmis and others, will about enable the friends to realise their ideal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>RUNCORN LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATES AND ABSENTEE LANDLORDS. On Monday the adjourned licensing sessions for Runcorn and district were held, Alderman Norman pre- siding over the bench. On an application being made for the renewal of the license of tke Rutter's Vaults in Brunswick-street, the magistrates' clerk (Mr. F. Johnson) said the licensee, Henry Shepherd, was fined Li a week ago, and the justices requested him to call attention to the fact that this man was a seafaring man, and was away from home for long periods and did not seem to have the proper supervision a tenant should have. Mr. Broady, representing Messrs. Greenall, Whitley, and Co., brewers, Warrington, said the firm intended to make a change as early as possible, and he gave an undertaking that the new tenant resided on the premises. The Chairman asked if the brewers had many cases like this, where the licensee performed other duties and was not able to attend properly to the house.—Mr. Broady said he did not think they had. They had no idea that this man did not reside on the premises until this case came up.—Superintendent Ennion said there were a good many licensees in Runcorn who followed other occupa- tions. The Chairman said he knew there were a number, and there was a feeling on the bench that these cases should be done ILsay with. Messrs. Greenall would make a note of it.—Mr. Little (a magistrate) said the:cases mentioned by the superintendent were not similar to the one under consideration. They followed other employment, and were away in the daytime, but lived at home. Shepherd was away from home for weeks.—The Chairman said in every case it would be well that the licensee should be on the premises. The licence was renewed on the undertaking that Messrs Greenall would find a new tenant.—Matthew Macdonald was granted the transfer of the licence of the Royal Hotel from George Curzon to himself,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATHS AT ST. HELENS. Ellen Kelly, a widow, of 53, Albert-street, St. Helens was found dead on the sofa at ten o'clock on Sunday morning by her son after, it is alleged, a drinking bout. Ben Jones, of 6, Prospect-road, St. Helens, a labourer, aged forty years, died very suddenly on Sunday morn- ing in his house. A PROFESSIONAL CADGER. Charles. Woods, aged seven of 11, Parr-street, was chargedjat St. Helens on Monday with begging, and the boy's uncle, John, was charged with begging and using the boy for the purpose, and William Woods, the father of the boy, was charged with conducing to the offence. The charge against John Woods was altered to " causing and procuring " under the Children's Act, and he was sent to goal for three months' hard labour. William Woods, the father, was sent to prison for two mouths and the boy was discharged. A TERRIBLE DEATH.</p>
        <p>I Mrs. Kate Argib.aid,-;;Tdf t-3;,--diedi; the Providence Hospital, St. Helens, on Tuesday, under very sad circumstances. Mrs. Archibald lived at 50, Lswe-street, and on Saturday evening, February 24th, about half- past eleven, when she was going upstairs, carrying a paraffin lamp, her clog slipped off one of the steps and she fell, breaking the lamp and setting herself on fire. Relatives did all that was possible for her until nest morning, when a doctor was obtained, and he hail the h poor woman taken to the hospital, where she died after I much suffering. MEMORIAL TO TPIE LATE MR. J. ROBINSON. There was a large congregation at Sunday morning' service at the Rainford Parish Church, St. Idelens,when the Bishop of Liverpool unveiled a new west window I given by Sir Gilbert Greened! and Messrs. Gceenall, Whitley and Co., to the memory of Mr. Joseph Robin- SOD, of Crank, St. Helens, who was a generous bene- factor to the Church. In the arrangement of the window regard has been given to the great height of the lights, and the principal portion has been devoted to four large single figures chosen from the Old Testament, whilst the space below the figures contains a particular subject in the life of each. The figures are those of Moses, Samuel, Elijah, and Daniel. At the foot of the window is the inscription :—" To the glory of God and in memory of Joseph Robinson, of Fairfield House, Crank, in this parish, who died August 29th, 1905, aged 75 years." The Bishop in his sermon paid a high tribute to the life of the late benefactor. ST. HELENS MAN'S MISCONDUCT. In the Divorce Court, Mr. Justice Barg ,ve Deane had before him the undefended petition of Mrs. Sarah Pownall for a judicial separation by reason of the mis- conduct of her husband, Mr. Robert Pownall, a watch- maker. The petitioner, of St. Helens, Lancashire, married the respondent in 1882 at the Congregational Chapel, Rock Ferry, Cheshire. They lived together in Cheshire, and afterwards at Westfield-street, St. Helens. He had a branch shop at Widnes, attached to which was a local post office. There a young woman was employed, and the respondent visited this woman at Liverpool. Afterwards he made theacquaintance of a young woman named Norbury, and he used to visit her from time to time at Coronation-terrace. He there passed by the name of Brown, and Miss Norbury as Mrs. Brown. A decree of judicial separation was granted witk costs and custody of the children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>ALLEGED FATAL DENTISTRY.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED FATAL DENTISTRY.</p>
        <p>An inquest was opened at St. Helens on-111o.nday night on the body of Joseph Johnson (8), who died in hospital under peculiar circumstances on Sunday.—Coroner Brighouse, reading the police report, said that on January 19th the boy, after suffering a fort- night's toothache, was taken by his father to St. Helens Market-place, where a man named William Mitchinson, residing in North-road, St. Helens, extracted two teeth free of charge.— "I am not responsible for what the police say in their report " said the Coroner, "but they refer to Mitchinson as a quack doctor, and as one who acted as a lightning teeth extractor-"—" I want that correcting," exclaimed Mitchinson, who was present in court, "I am not a quack doctor."—Continuing, the Coroner explained that, after the operation, tile boy suffered great pain. His mouth became ulcerated, and the doctor ordered his removal to the hospital, where he died.—The inquiry was adjourned till Monday, so that Mitchinson should have legal advice. EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>LADY NEWTON'S DANCE. Most of the prettiest young girls in society were present at Lady Newton's dance in Belgrave-square, London, on Monday. The party was given in honour of the eldest daughter of the house, Miss Lattice Legh who (according to a writer in the " Daily Chronicle ")' looked exeeedindly dainty in a dress of gleaming silver tissue. Lady Newton wore mauve, threaded with silver with a high diamond collar, with a small tiara in her hair. Two dozen young people were entertained to dinner beforehand, and Lord Newton's two sons came up from Eton for the occasion. " IGNORING " THE HOUSE OF LORDS. LORD NEWTON'S PROTEST. In the House of Lords, on Monday, Loyd Newton asked the Lord Privy Seal whether, in view of the appointment of a Committee on Parliamentary Pro- cedure in another place, steps would be taken b:F the Government to secure due consideration for the rights and privileges of this House. He said their existence i seemed to be so far entirely forgotten n i regard to this matter. That was not surprising, considering that n the last year the existence of this House had been almost entirely ignored. They had gradually allowed them- ; selves to be elbowed out of all legislative work, and they really seemed to be approaching a posi- tion which an unfriendly critic might describe as a sort of compromise between a high-class debating society and a registry Office. They were practically obliged last year to confine them- selves to purely registry work, and the climax was reached last session when, on the occasion of the intro- duction of the Aliens Bill, time was so limited that the representative of the Government was actually afraid to move his own amendments. (Laughter.) The Unemployed Bill was introduced and rushed through its pages, and, if he was not mistaken, two days after- wards the House rose. Everybody would agree that that was a thoroughly unsatisfactory state of things. He appealed to the Government to assert the honour and the dignity of this House in regard to legislative work. —Lord Tweedmouth, in the absence of the Marquis of Ripon, replied, and said he could assure the noble lord that the Government were not less anxious to maintain the dignity of the house and to secure its privileges than their predecessors in office. His idea was that the principal reasons why the House of Lords had been a house of registry for the greater part of the last twenty years was that for seventeen of those years the great majority of that house had been in sympathy with the great majority of the other house, and, there- fore, was only too glad to pass the measures that came from it (Ministerial laughter.) He suggested that it was altogether previous to raise this question at this moment, but should there be any danger to the•privi- leges of that house they would be only to glad to take consultation with noble lords opposite to secure a proper arrangement for the benefit of the House.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
