      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Coming Events It will be seen from our advertising columns that Mr. T. Brough's well-known Juvenile Choir are giving a performance in the Parr Hall on Monday evening next. This choir is undoubtedly a very fine combina- tion and the programme submitted for this special event is of a most novel and attractive order and is certain to delight everyone present. The purity of tone, balance and blend of voices is truly exceptional, and all who have not yet heard this clever little com- pany of talented singers should take this opportunity of doing so. An exceptionally attractive programme has been arranged for the concert which will be held at the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening next in aid of St. Mary's (Buttermarket-street) Working Girls' Club. The artistes will be Miss Marie Houghton, Miss Lily Fairhurst, Mrs. John Dolan, Miss Sexton, Mr. J. Rabbitt, Mr. J. W. Bibby, Mr. R. H. Pearson (violin), and Mr. Matt Delacey (humorist). A meeting to further the interests of the Beautiful Warrington Society in St. Austin's Ward will be held in Cairo-street schools on Tuesday evening. At a meeting of the Junior Liberal Association on Monday evening Councillor Ball will give an address on " William Morris." At Cairo-street schools next Thursday evening the Rev. Charles Hargrove M.A., will lecture on The first three Gospels and their origin." The penny popular concert will be held in Bold- street school on Saturday evening as usual. A concert in aid of the Poor Children's Summer Camp will be held in the Wesleyan school, Padgate, on Saturday evening, March 17th. The first annual show of bulbs promoted by the , Stockton Heath Garden Society will be held in the Hall Cafe on Thursday next. Our readers will see from our advertisement column that the Rev. Herbert Dunnico is announced to com- mence a series of special sermons on Sunday next. In the morning Mr. Dunnico will deliver the first of five sermons " Religion and Belief," and in the evening the first of five sermons on "Religion and Practice."</p>
        <p>The L G.B. and the New Street. We learn that at a private meeting of the Council on Tuesday it was agreed that Councillor James Evans and the Deputy Town Clerk should interview the Local Government Board and explain the position of the Corporation in regard to the withdrawal of Messrs. Griffiths and Messrs. Greeuall's property from the schedule of properties to be purchased in making the new street from Sankey-street to Rylands-street.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good Boos.—Advt. Smart Sentence on a Negligent Father. A Warrington painter named Jesse Astall (living in Vere-street) was charged at the Police Court on Thursday with neglecting his children and also obtaining 6d. by false. pretences. Oilly in October last, it was stated, prisoner got a I-Audi for family neglect, and he had done practically nothing since, although he Was an excellent workman. He was always soaked in drink. During the last ten weeks he had only given his wife, who was scrupulously clean in the house, 50s. As regards the false pre- tences prisoner met a man named Hatch. He went to Hatch's wife and obtained 6d. from her, saying that her husband had sent him. This trick he had performed in several other instances. Prisoner was sentenced to nine months' hard labour.</p>
        <p>Lorne House School Concert. There was a crowded audience at the Parr Hall on Thursday evening on the occasion of a concert given by the pupils of Lorne House School. An excellent pro- gramme was given by the children who acquitted them- selves in a manner which reflected the greatest cfedit upon those who had undertaken the work of preparing them for the event. The proceedings which were under the Chairmanship of Air. W. Roberts. opened with a chorus which was admirably sung. The infants gave a pretty action song " Riding in the tram " in an effective manner. They met equal success in another action song "When granny cornea home from the fair" in which they were joined by Miss E. Chrimes. An admirable miscellaneous programme was submitted to which vocal items we' e coniributed by the Misses E. Pickup, E. Birch, N. Mullins, G. Danks, D. Perdue, A. Southern, A. Fairhurst, and E. Winstanley. Miss E. Cook contributed a well played clarionet solo, while the school orchestra played with effect. The outstanding feature of the entertainment con- sisted of two cantatas " Princess Tiny Tot " and " Meadowsweet " which were excellently given. The characters in the first were : Princess Tiny Tot Miss C. Warburton ; Majordomo, Miss A Titchett ;, Mother Redcap, Miss J. Birchall, whilst the junior girls represented a number of nursery characters. The title role in " Meadowsweet " was taken by Miss C. Cook, the other characters being : Caterpillar, Miss A. Whalley ; witch, Miss H. Howell, and Bridesmaids, 11 the Misses C. Chrimes, J. Brunt, E. Winstanley, W. 1 Stubbs, S. Clatworthy and C. West. Miss Roscollor and Miss L. Everton acted as accompanists - -</p>
        <p>Borough of Warrington Building Society. The thirty-first annual meeting of the Borough of Warrington Incorporated Permanent Building Society was held at the manager's office, Legh-street, on Wed- nesday evening. Mr. J. Andrews presided and there were also present :—Messrs. G. Holford, T. H. A. Pilkington, H. Floughton, J. IT Clarke, W. D. Jeans, jun., E. Lockett, J, G. Chappell and 0. Plinston (auditors)—The report of the directors stated that including .£6 198. 6d. brought forward at 31st January 1905, the nett balance of profits, after providing £l6 19s. 6d. for working expenses legal charges, and income tax :was £B9 is. id. To this sum the Directors recommended that X,95 be trans- ferred from the reserve fund, and that a dividend of 4 per cent, per annum free of income tax be paid to the shareholders, leaving 3s. Id. to be carried to the next account. The number of members on the books was 48, of whom 11 were borrowers. The Chairman moved and Mr. Clarke seconded the adoption of the report which was agreed to. The whole of the directors retired but were re-elected as follows : Messrs. John Andrews (chairman), G. Holford, T. 11. A. Pilkington, W. Roberts, H. Houghton, and S. F. Welsby. The auditors (Messrs. T. G. Chappell and C. Plinston) and the surveyor (Mr. Thomas A. Sutton) were re-elected. The pay- ment of a dividend of 4 per cent, was sanctioned. A vote of thanks was accorded to the directors and managers for their services during the past year. Mr. J. H. Clarke who proposed it expressed the hope that the Society would continue to flourish and that business would be brisker during the ensuing twelve months. Concert In the Parr Hall.</p>
        <p>With the very commendable object of aiding the , funds of the Railway Benevolent Institution a concert ' was held in the Parr Hall on Tuesday evening. There was a large audience and the event was in every- way a success. The programme was a highly inter eating one, the major portion being sustained by the Chester Cathedral Quartette (Messrs. Dawson, Green- wood, Buxton and Ditchburn). The voices of this talented party blended most effectively in several well- chosen items,most of which were of a light, humorous character. " Patent medicines " and "Little Tommy" were particularly good examples of this type and proved extremely popular with the audience. The individual members of the quartette acquitted them- selves in a:manner which called forth warm encomiums. Mr. J. H. Ditchbum is a favourite with Warrington audiences and he was very happy in his selection of "Three or Jack" and "Old Happy-go-lucky " songs which went with a capital swing and won the heartiest approval. Mr. J. S. Buxton, although suffering from slight huskiness, gave an artistic interpretation of" By the fountain" and Mr. A. Greenwood infused the necessary feeling and expres- sion into his rendering of " Lorna." The other parts of the entertainment were equally pleasing and enjoy- able. Mrs. A. Booth was warmly greeted and enhanced her reputation by her admirable vocalisation of " Jewel Song " and " Annie Laurie," in which her rich and flexible voice found full scope. Responding to an encore for the latter item she gave " Love's garden " with equal success. Miss Alice Woodward, another local artiste, sang " There's a land " and " Roses " in an effective way, and gained her quota of the audience's plaudits. The humorous element was entrusted to Mr. Loui Parry (Chester), who was eminently successful in his efforts to amuse, and kept the audience in great merriment. The duties of accompanist were ably discharged by Mr. W. H. ray- on, ilus. Bac.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Index to Inside Pages.</head>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages.</p>
        <p>PAGE.</p>
        <p>_ 2. Serial: " A Cruel Temptation." Women's Chat and Fashions. 3. Tree-planting at Stockton Heath : Discussion at the Rural Council. Football. Captain Crosfielcl on " The Land Problem." Science Notes. 6. Catholics and the Education Question. Town Council. District News. 7. "Are we spoiling our Children," by W. Pett Ridge. Children's Corner. Liberalism In Stockton Heath. A well attended meeting of Stockton Heath Liberals was held in the Providence Schoolroom on Thursday evening, under the chairmanship of Mr. W. H. Sherburn. On the motion of Mr. H. Roberts, seconded by Mr. J. Longland the following resolution was unanimously approved :—" That in view of the increasing population of Stockcon Heath, and of the growth of Liberal sentiment (as indicated by the result of the recent general election) the time is oppor- tune for fuller devolopment of the work of the Liberal Association iii social as well as political directions, and this meeting hereby pledges itself to support any well considered scheme by which the same may be realized." A strong committee, with Mr. E. Lloyd Jones as hon. seoretary, was appointed to make I arrangements for the organizing meeting. During the proceedings, refreshments were served and enjoyable musical items contributed. Judging by the enthusiasm displayed there is every prospect of the formation of a strong and active Liberal Association to supple- ment the existing body. Sequel to an Accident. At the Warrington County Curt on Thursday ; Frederick Hodson, green grocer, 33, Priestly-street, brought an action against John Emmess, coal dealer, , 2, Parr-street, for £2O damages alleged to have been I sustained through the negligence of defendant's servant. Plaintiff's case was that on July 15th, 1905, he was in Nicholson-street with his pony and cart. The defendant's driver, who was in charge of a horse and lurry, and who had he alleged been thrashing the! horse approached at an unreasonable speed and in turning a corner the lurry collided with a wall. The impact broke the shafts of the lurry, and the horse bolted and jumped on the top of plaintiffs pony and cart doing considerable damage.—Defendants sub- mitted that the affair was purely an accident. The cause of the collision with the wall he said was the ,skidding of a wheel of the lurry in the channel.—His Honour. Judge Bowen Rowlands, K.C., held that the accident was due to the negligence of the defendant's driver and •gave judgment for the plaintiff for £8 18s. 6d. with costs. Plaintiff was represented by Mr. T. S, Steel, Mr. A. Browne appearing for the defen- dant. Potroleum Oil in barrels and drums delivered free.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, bridge at., Warring- ton.—Advt. Board of Guardians—Dr. Braide's Position. A. long discussion took place at the meeting of the Board of Guardians on Wednesday on a recommenda- tion of the House Committee that consideration of the appointment of a resident medical officer be deferred and 'that the proper treatment of the sick indoor poor necessitates a daily attendance of at least two hours duration, and therefore he (Dr. Braide) be called upon forthwith to pay daily visits of not less than two hours such visits to commence not earlier than 9 o'clock or not later than 11 o'clock in the forenoon, these visits not to be deemed to affect the medical officer's res- ponsibility to attend when requested for urgent cases." Dr. Braids wrote suggesting the appointment of an independent arbitrator to go into the whole question. —Mr. Ashcroft contended that the cause of the com- mittee's action was an " old sore " and they wanted to interfere with Dr. Braide's private practice.—This statement was strongly denied by Mr. Jolley and Others. On a vote being taken it was decided to refer the matter back to the committee for reconsideration. It was resolved that the salaries of the Master and Matron (Mr. and Mrs. J. F. Awbery) be increased by £2O and £l5 respectively, making the joint salaries £2lO. Mr. Ashcroft contended that the increases were too large especially in view of the enormous expendi- ture on salaries &amp;c., of late years. , Local Successes at Earlestown Eisteddfod. 4Saturday was quite a day of successes for our local talent at the Earlestown Eisteddfod. The well-known Lyric Quartette Party (Misses A. Woodland, L. Fair- hurst, and Messrs. R. Cook and P. George) added another to their list of victories, this making the sixth first prize they have won. The adjudicator (Mr. Wilfred Jones, R.A.M.) remarked that this was an excellent quartette party, the voices blending admir- ably, and they sang the test piece, " When hands meet," by Pozsuiti, unaccompanied right through without a flaw and ended in perfect pitch. Their achievement was the more creditable as they defeated that splendid quartette party from St. Helens which won first prize in the National Eisteddfod. Miss Lily Fairhurst was awarded first prize out of eleven com- petitors in the ladies' challenge solo competition. The adjudicator remarked that she had an excellent con- tralto voice and sang her very difficult solo, " The Enchantress," in a manner which reflected great credit on her music and voice trainer. The chief choral competition prize of £l2 and gold medal also came to Warrington, the Apollo Prize Choir winning for the second year in succession. Four choirs competed— Warrington Apollo, Alakerfield, Leigh and Wigan. The adjudicator in his remarks on the Apollo Choir's rendering said the choir had got excellent voices, especially the second basses. He never heard better. The first basses were also of a very fine quality and sang well. The tenors although rather weak were well produced and sang well, but not the open tone that they beard in the other choirs. The test piece, " Spartan heroes," was such that it required plenty of dramatic feeling and this they got from the Apollo Choir. The rendering was just what he liked and really an excellent performance. All the successful local talest are under the training of Mr. Harry Berrey I of St. Helens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ADJOURNED BREWSTER SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>ADJOURNED BREWSTER SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>Objections to Four Licences.</p>
        <p>The adjourned licensing sessions for the Borough were opened at the Police Court this (Friday) morning. Notice of objections to the renewal of the licences of the Rose and Crown, Bridge-street, Rope and Anchor, Buttermarket-street, Boars Head, Pig Hill, and the Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street, had been entered by the Chief Constable, and the proceedings therefore ((roused considerable attention, the Court being crowded by persons interested in the trade. Mr. John Fairclough presided over a full Bench. WARNING TO LICHNSEES.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said he would like to call atten- tion to the action which publicans had taken with regard to music licences. Two years ago, withtwo exceptions, all music licences were taken away but several publicans had recently been guilty of consider- able laxity. ' Mr. Fairclough expressed the hope that the Chief Constable should bring before the Court anyone who departed from the orders of the Bench.</p>
        <p>LICENCES RENEWED. All licences, with the exception of those objected to by the Chief Constable, were renewed. OBJECTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable's grounds for objecting to the renewal of the licences mentioned were that they were not required to meet the wants of the neighbourhoods in which they were situated and that they were doing very little business. Mr. F. E. Smitb, M.P., (instructed by Messrs. A. Brown and Co.) appeared on behalf of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., the owners of the "Rose and Crown " and the" Rope and Anchor," and asked for the renewal of the licences. It would, be said, be a disastrous tiling if the impression got abroad that a house, if it was to survive, must be a big one with glaring buildings and enormous takings. Both houses, he contended, were required as proved by the takings. The average weekly takings of the" Rose and Crown "were £l6 12s. and of the" Rope and Anchor " £1.7. The objections to all four licences were upheld by the Bench and the question of compensation was referred to the compensation authority.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S '`IMAYFA I R" BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>A NEW BISCUIT FOR AFTERNOON TEA.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."]</p>
        <p>! The only interest Warrington have now in the Northern Union Cup is in handing it over to its new holders when the Competition has been, decided. Local aspirations were rudely shattered at Mount Pleasant, where Batley, for the third time in Cup-tie history, put Warrington "to sleep." Only one point separated the teams at the'close of a hard struggle, and it was the general opinion that the Cap-holders were unlucky to lose. Except in the mind of the most rabid of home partizans there could be no doubt on this point. Warrington had nearly all the game, were the only team to penetrate the opposing defence, and yet they lost. Of course, it was not pure bad luck that caused Warrington to lose. There were other considerations, one of which was bad management both on the field and prior to the game. But of this, anon. The one thing that Warrington can be con- gratulated upon is in making the best fight they have yet made against the " gallant youths " in a cup-tie.</p>
        <p>The fact remains, however, that Warrington should certainly have won, notwithstanding the handicap which injuries to players imposed on the Club. It is upon the reasons why they failed that I now propose to dwell, and to apportion the blame where I think it is deserved. Primarily Warrington lost because they failed to take their chances, while the home side made the most of their few opportunities. But I must go a little further back to get at the root causes. It will be remembered that the selected team containedßrooks, Isherwood, Dickinson and Taylor at threequarters and Hockenhall, Lees or Lloyd at ball-back. Not very much fault could be urged against this selection, except that Lloyd had never shown any form in the best company that justified his choice for a cup-tie with Batley. When the team left, it was found that Lees was unfit, and Lloyd went. Between the time of departure and the appearance on the field those members of the Committee who were in charge of the team decided to play Brooks at half, and Lloyd on the wing. lam willing to admit that they were in a difficulty, but I feel bound to record my opinion —and it is that of eveiy supporter of the club I have met— that a very grave error of judgment was corn- mitted. In fact the course taken was so remarkable that I could hardly believe the report at Batley until the team turned out. Lloyd has never before, to my knowledge, played wing three.quarter in a game of the slightest importance, and to put him in this important position against Batley was the counsel of panic, neither more or less. That he would be a failure was a foregone conclusion—and he was, an awful failure. I don't blame Lloyd. Had he been played at half back, where he has bad some experience, he would probably have done as well as either of the Batley pair, or as well as Brooks. But on the wing he was purely a passenger, and a hindrance to his centre, Isherwood. The mistake was in selecting him at all. If Kenyon and Widdeson were objected to, then the man for the job was certainly Jordan, but any or the three would have made a considerable difference to the course of the game.</p>
        <p>I have been asked whether I thought the playing of Lloyd lost Warrington the match. My answer is " Yes" and" No." I say "Yes " because Isherwood made two openings in the first half which Lloyd was quite unable to take advantage of, and which Jordan, Kenyon, or Widdeson would almost certainly have turned to account. I say "No," because Warrington should have won, even as matters turned out. They threw points away in the game itself. Before turning to the play I feel bound to say that bad management had as much to do with the defeat at the hands of lßatley on Saturday, as it had in 1901, when the same teams were opponents in the final at Leeds.</p>
        <p>With regard to the play itself there is no doubt that luck was against the Warrington men all through, but they had their chance! The turning point of the game came when Hockenhall scored his brilliant try. The position was of the easiest for adding a goal, but that was no reason why the scorer, blown with a tricky run, should have taken the kick. It was not as if Hockenhall was a reputed goal-kicker. The one man who has shown conspicuous skill in this line, and wha has made a study of the art, is Preston and if he had taken this particular shot, as he should have done, I think Warrington would have won. Missing the goal was two paints absolutely thrown away. I suppose the blame rests with the captain—whoever he was— and certainly there was never any sign of intelligent captaincy on behalf of Warrington throughout the I struggle. For many years now, Warrington have been badly led on the field of play, and this has undoubtedly told on the results. Batley were con- spicuously well-generalled by Oakland and Davies,and circumvented much of Warrington's cleverness by old- headedness—a feature which has always been conspic- uous in their cup-tie football. It is no use crying over spilt milk now, but there was an uncomfortable feeling in the breasts of Warrington's supporters that those two points would be wanted before the end,and they were !</p>
        <p>The game opened sensationally, for Wattle Davies put his side ahead in the first five minutes with a penalty goal from near touch. There was no chopping and changing with the place-kicks on the Batley side ! After Ilockenhall's try, engineered by Isherwood, Davies kicked another goal from the same position, and Just before the interval Oakland dropped a third ' from the base of the scrum. I am told that on this occasion Thomas was in the thre,equarter-line, bat I did not see him myself. If so, then this was another instance of adopting a policy which has previously proved disastrous. Up to half time, though playing against wind and slope, Warrington 'were much the cleverer side. The four inside men combined beauti- fully and with any support on the left wing, a couple of tries should have resulted.</p>
        <p>On resuming, three points in arrears, the odds were on Warrington. But I feared Batley's trickery, and events justified the fear. They contrived, by loose packing and other devices, to waste about half the actual football time. Once, Turton was doctored for an injured leg on the field of play, and the referee allowed the game to be delayed for quite five minutes. Herein he was wrong, and very unfair to Warring- ton, for he stopped the game on the very second that the 40 minutes was up Warrington quite failed to get going, or even to reproduce the form of the first half. Hockenhall and Brooks passed badly at critical moments, and only once did the centres get on the run. Then, Isherwood dashed over finely with what seemed a perfect try, but Mr. Priestley ruled that he had fallen short. Isherwood tells me that he was well over the line, and other members of the team support him. But it's all in the game. And so things went on—Batley defend- ing stubbornly and Warrington attacking in half- hearted fashion until the end. At the last moment there was a glorious chance to pull the game out of the fire. Preston had landed one penalty goal, and a Batley man got clearly off-side again. The burly forward took every care with the kick, which was not difficult, but the elusive oval passed only inches on the wrong side of the post. What a shout there was, and a groan from Warrington ! It was all over, and Batley had won.</p>
        <p>The honours on the Warrington side go to Isher- wood, Dickinson, and Berry. These three stood out above all the rest, and gave of their best. Berry was magnificent, and in him Warrington have the full back of the future. The forwards missed Boardman and Shugars badly, and never quite got the upper hand of the Batley eight. The latter were the best part of the home team. In the back division, Oakland and Davies, while far below the form of old, were the best of a second-rate lot. In open play they would have been easily beaten, but Batley saw to it that there was no open play after half-time. They were ever a wily lot.</p>
        <p>Owing to Dewsbury having taken their game to Hull there was a fine crowd at Mount Pleasant, and the gate money realised £l3B. A half share of this is Warrington's only consolation. The second round draw leaves Batley at home again to York. Lucky Batley !</p>
        <p>The draw has worked out as follows : Batley v. York. Brighouse Rangers v. Hull Kingston Roverl 111111 V. Keighley. Oldham v. Halifax. Bradford v Leigh. Salford v. Egerton. Leeds v. Broughton Rangers. Featherstone Rovers v. Widnes or Victoria Rangers.</p>
        <p>There should be plenty of interest in the match at W!lderspool to-day notwithstanding Cup-tie disap- potntments. Like Warrington,Wigan lost by a solitary point in Yorkshire, York doing the trick. Wigan are always popular visitors to Warrington, and given a fine day there should be crowd such as will delight the heart of the club treasurer. The match will count both in the North Union League and the South West Lancashire Competition of which Wigan are the leaders. A big effort is sure to be made •by our visitors and the Warrington crowd ought to enjoy the first strenous struggle they have witnessed since Swinton were here on January 20th.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will, unfortunately, still be handicapped by injuries to players. Neither Hocken- hall, Boardrnan,Shugars,llirria nor Fish are available. It will be noted, however, that Widdeson is brought to partner Isherwood It is a pity he did not do so last week. Lees has recovered and will join Brooks at hall-back, while the three centres complete the three- quarter line. In all probability Lambert would have got promotion,but the important Combination game at Wigan has to be remembered.</p>
        <p>The full team will be : Ilerry,full back ; Widdeson. Isherwood, Dickenson and Taylor, three-quarters ; Lees and Brooks, half backs; Preston, Belton,Elarmer, Thomas, Swift, Jolley, Morrison and Heath, forwards,</p>
        <p>The Wigan team has been chosen as follows Mason; Levtham, Jenkins, Price, Lowe ; Anderson, Thomas ; Hilton, Whittaker, Brookes, Moores, Barton, Watkins, T. McCabe, and Collier.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>referee for the match is Mr. W. M'Catcheon.</head>
        <p>The referee for the match is Mr. W. M'Catcheon.</p>
        <p>As above indicated there will be a rare duel at Wigan between the "A " teams. Whatever the result of the match Warrington should still retain the championsht but they are anxious to repeat last year's perfoiffiance and beat Wigan home and away. The following strong team has been chosen: Knight, full back ; Jordan, Cartwright, Jones, and Kenyon, threequarters ; Lambert and Davies, half backs ; Forster, Lunt, Fairhurst, Bradshaw and Ashton, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Preachers for Sunday.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyam Church, 10.30, Rev. A, C. Parker ; 6.30, Red. J. Manning. Annual congregational tea and public meeting on Tuesday evening. Bewsey-road Wesleyan Churck, 10.30. Rev. W.Mellor; 6.30, Rev. Sidney Pitt. Golborne-street Baptist Church, 10.30. Rev. H. Dunnico (" Religion and theology ") ; 6.30, Rev. H. Minnie° ("Religion and daily life.") Latchford Baptist Church, 10.30, Rev. J. F.Matthews (" Old Testament Studies; Joseph and personal purity ") ; 6.30, Rev. J. F.Matthews ("The omniscience of divine love.") Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church—choir anni- versary, 10.30 and 6.30, a student from Manchester College. Afternoon at 3 musical service including selections from Mendelssohn's " Hymn of Praise.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday morning the preacher was Mr. R. J. Moss. In the evening the Rev. Sidney Pitt spoke on the words" And Jesus said, Somebody bath 'touched me: for I perceive that virtue has gone out of me."—There were good congregations. tis</p>
        <p>Latchford Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The United Bible classes held their open meeting on Sunday, March 3rd. Mr. James Hampton presided over a good attendance, and Mr. G. Davey-Smith delivered a splendid address. Miss Howell sang "The Better Land," and Mr. J. Clayton rendered 4 The Sailor's Grave " and" The Holy City." Mr. W. H. Ford pre- sided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Wycliffe Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Church.</p>
        <p>At this Church on Sunday the Rev. John Yonge preached to large congregations. In the morning he delivered a Lenten sermon on the words : " And rend vourtheart and not your garments and turn unto the Lord your God : for Re is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil." At the evening service his text was "The Son of Man came eating and drinking and they say behold a man gluttonous, and a winebibber, a friend of publicans and sinners. But wisdom is justified of her children." _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>There were good congregations at this Church on Sunday. The Rev. Sidney Pitt preached at the morning service his text being: "Then coineth Jesus from Galilee to Jordan unto John, to be baptized of him. But John forbad Him, saying, I have need to be baptized of Thee, and cornest Thou to me? And Jesus answering said unto him, Suffer it to be so now, for thus it becometh us to fulfil:all righteousness. Then he suffered Him." In the afternoon the choir rendered a service of song entitled "A Child of Jesus." The music was most effectively interpreted, Mr. B. Wadsworth presiding at the organ. Mrs. Harnpson gave the con- nective readings. The offertory was on behalf of the fund for the redecoration of the Lecture Hall.—At the evening service the Rev. J. Manning preached. The Wesley Guild in connection with this church is in a very flourishing state. Last week an interesting debate took place between the ladies and gentlernen,,the subject being" Should women take public positions?" The ladies, of course, took the aMrrnative and carried their point by a majority of nine. On Monday evening a devotional meeting was held, when Mr. Sales, of Liverpool, read a paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Baptist Church, Colborne Street.</head>
        <p>Baptist Church, Colborne Street.</p>
        <p>In the absence of the titstor who wis preaching at Toxteth Tabernacle Liverpool, the pulpit at the above place of worship was occupied by the Rev. R. Black of Wigan: There was a fairly larg.e congregation in the morning when the rev. gentleman delivered a thought- ful discourse on the words "The eyes of a wise man are in his head;the eyes of a fool are in the ends of earth " Provs. 170. 24v. lie said the principal of the test illustrated a very common kind of folly whose manifestations were familiar to all. There was the folly of the parson who was discontented with the sphere in which Providence had placed him. He imagined that if he occupied a different position and woked under different conditions he would be successful and happy. The poor were persuaded that all would be well with them if their lot was different ; while the rich sighed for the happiness that was thought to be associated with rustic simplicity of life. The student vexing his mind with Greek syntax longed for active ' ministry : and the minister of some years experience longed for his student days when it was 1 easier to master a problem in Euclid than the problem of Church division. There was such a thing however as a man making a wrong choice in life. The round man lin the square hole was common enough. There was 1 many a man wearing the vestments of ecclesiasticism and making martyrs of a long-suffering congregation by his inane drivel, who ought to be wearing the habili- ments of the ploughman and cracking a whip over the ears of a pair of sleepy horses. God did not mean them to take root just where they happened to be dropped. Ambition was not incompatible with duty. But it was folly to fix their eyes on a different position to that which they occupied and allow themselves to become so disheartened as to neglect the duties of their present position. The text also had a bearing on that form of folly which habitu- ally looked for large opportunities and neglected smaller ones. By thinking and speaking disparagingly of" the trivial round, the common task," they missed the daily and hourly opportunities of pouring forth their lesser selves in deeds of helpfulness and self- sacrificing love on behalf of men. Faithfulness in the discharge of the smallest duties was the real test of character ; and the giving a cup of sold water in the name of a disciple" was as honourable as organ- ising great reforms. The text further touched the folly which was always harking oack to the good old times that were, and also very nearly touched the folly of the busybody in other men's matters—that person who interfered with everybody's affairs and neglected his owl:IL who set him- self to reform everybody save himself. It was an easy thing to go out as a kind of moral knight-errant—a 1 modern edition of Don Quixote—tilting at the imag- inary wrongs in other men's lives, in comparison with going into the presence of a holy God and dealing with Him about their own sins which were real. Reform the reformer, and the reformer would reform the world. ! —The church was well filled in the evening when an , eloquent sermon wss preached on " Zscchzeus." P.S.A. Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The thirteenth anniversary of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society was celebrated on Sunday, when a special ser- vice was held in the Congregational Church, Bewsey- street. The proceedings were attended by a crowded congregation, over which Mr. George Walker presided. ! In a brief speech the CHAIRMAN said that while the large attendance bore testimony to the efforts put forth by the P.S.A. Society, he did not take it as the complete measure of their success, because he believed their influence for good was very far-reaching. "Brief, bright and brotherly " their meetings did not appeal to the intellect or the senses by any particular form of dogma, but by sympathy reached the hearts of the people and ' created a higher ideal of usefulness and responsibility. They might have great reverence for the older methods, but at the same time they had a greater admiration for the later methods. It was only by appealing to their ; humanity and sympathies that the social movements for ' the uplifting of the people could be successful. As members of the P.S.A. they believed in the power of music to uplift and he also believed that they could exert agreat influence by means of music incases where language would fail them. The music did not need to ' be classical, in fact, he thought the simpler the music the more heart reaching was its °fleet. They knew and</p>
        <p>all were bound to recognize that frequently the noblest truths in the gospel of Christ were borne home in song. The Wycliffe P.S.A. was playing an important part in the uplifting of the people of Warrington and many who were leading happier and more useful lives were thankful that they had been brought within the sphere of its influence. (Applause.) The PRESIDENT, the Rev. John Yonge, delivered an address during the course of which he said that a year ago he took a survey of twelve years of the P.S.A. brotherhood, recalling some of the facts of its early days. He also tried to help them to look onward to a prosperous, aggressive, and progressive future. He wished he could say the same that day—that the year just ended had proved as aggressive and progressive as could have been desired. He believed the actual num- ber of members was smaller. They had not aggressed, and so they had not progressed. Permit him to say that fluctuation and declension of that kind, while it should not unduly discourage, should be courageously striven against. They were all delighted to have another visit from CrosCield's Prize Band. They rejoiced in their continued and growing success, and were glad that they were willing to lend a helping hand to their men's meeting. There were good people to be found who seriously questioned the propriety of a place of wor- ship being made to resound with the music of such a band as theirs. One hardly knew, indeed, how the objection came in, or what its reason was. He supposed the real root of the objection that was urged by some was that it was a very sensational proceeding, and they did not like anything "sensa- tional "in religion—or in religious worship, or religious meetings. They liked things done quietly, without noise, " decently, and in order." without anything at all eccentric, or out of the way. It was no use Christian people standing on the thresholds of their churches, and looking shocked and disappointed at the people who passed by and did not come in, yet did nothing to get them to come in. Religion and the Church of every name whose prime work it was to promote religion, needed to become similarly " violent," as a band and similarly aggressive upon she attention, and upon the interest of men and women because people were apt to be so apathetic and lethargic and careless about religion. They needed to be made to think about religion, and about their need of it,and to he convinced that religious people cared about their unconcern and indifference, that religious people were exerting themselves, at cost of toil and outlay and tirne,tnawaken their interest and arouse their endeavour after a better life. There were people who could only be reached by some violent,some sensational, some unusual methods. But by such, it had been proved possible to reach them. They had been reached and they had been saved. The P.S.A. movement itself had been a "violent " thing. Unless, however, they took care, he feared it would become as stereotyped and formal as the previous existing Church services. When it did that it would lose the popularity it had hitherto enjoyed ; and cease to succeed. He wanted their Wycliffe P.S.A., starting that day on its 14th year, to gather fresh and augmented violence in the shape of new self-assertiveness, and per- sistent self-advertisement, and resolute and united and individual endeavour to arouse and enlist the working- men of the town. The P.S.A. Society conspicuously stood for the militant activity of the Christian Church. The P.S.A. was an aggressive force against the forces of sin, and irreligion and indifferentism. It had spoken from that platform strong but kind words about the evils of intemperance, and of gambling, and of impure and profane speech. Nor had it spoken in vain ! He might be wrong, yet he hardly thought he was ; but he had an idea that the influence of that and other P.S.A.'s in town, and also of the men's Bible classes connected with Anglican Churches, many of which sprang to life and usefulness by the stimulus of the P.S.A. movement, had something to do with the vastly improved state of the town on the recent election day. It was said to have been the soberest polling day ever known. The Chief Constable bore witness to that fact. Was it too much to claim that the influence of the men's meetings had told considerably in that direction? He drew encouragement from the fact. There had been an uplift of opinion, and a reform of what was the too prevalent habit. If political and municipal elections could become conspicuously sober, it said a great deal for the moral tone of the town. Be that as it might, these meetings of men on religious lines on Sundays must be beneficial ! And they must make them more so, by becoming more and more militant in their spirit, and in their enterprise. (Applause). During the afternoon a number of excellent items were contributed by Messrs. Crosfleld's band.</p>
        <p>The New Testament. The first of a series of three lectures on the New Testament was delivered in the Cairo-street Schools on Thursday evening by the Rev. Charles Hargrove, M.A., of Leeds. Mr. F. Monks presided over a good attend- ance. Mr. Hargrove took. for his subject" The New Testament How it came to be a book." He clearly traced how the writings which compose the New Testament were collected and put into book form at the latter end of the second century of the Christian era. The New Testament did not become a distinct book until long after the time of the Apostles because the early Christians valued oral traditions above all writings. The New Testament made no claim to be infallible or to be inspired. It was the book about Jesus, his life, his apostles, his work and his worth. It was an inestimable treasure to mankind, no matter what was the conclusien of modern criticism as to its composition. It might not be as old as people would have it to be, its psrts, or some of them, might have no , business to be within it, but it contained all that was ' known to them of the life, and the teaching and the influence upon His own generation of the Son of Man. (Applause.) The Lecturenwas heartily thanked on the motion of Mr. F. Mosxs, seconded by Air. A. J. jOLLEY.</p>
        <p>Bishop Thornton on " Social Delusions." The first of a serious of Lenten 'addresses to men arranged by theWarrington Church Society was delivered at the Parish Church on Friday evening by the Right Rev Bishop Thornton, of Blackburn, whose subject was "Some social delusions" There was a very large attendance. Dealing with the present social conditions the Bishop said there seemed to be something like a breakdown. There was a tremendous social muddle— an euonomic muddle. Much of that was caused by the very unequal distribution of wealth. He could not help feeling that when the suffering of centuries fought for its rights, there was likely to be wild talk and there might also be wild work done. There were some appalling facts to be givsn of this country. The destitutes in England including 1,500,000 paupers, were equal to the entire population of Australia. The death rate among them was 28 per 1,000. Thirty per cent, of the people of this country were ill fed, and eighteen per cent, had no margin in their income to meet the con- tingencies of life. On the other hand those who owned four fifths of the country could be assembled in a single chamber and addressed by a single speaker. There were 150 people who owned half England. At the West End of London there was a population who simply spent time in an amazing fashion. They shot grouse at a guinea a bracet stalked stags at £5O apiece, and the rest of the season simply "sat down to eat and drink and rose up to play." At the other end of London, there were hopeless women underfed, engaged in mak- ing match boxes, having to find their own paste and receiving 2icl. a gross. They worked 14 hours a day to keep body and soul together. Of course it was some- times said that this class deserved their lot because they were drunken, indolent and reckless, but there were a great many of that description among the upper class and no such dismal penalty was exacted at their hands. One tenth of the English people drudged all their lives and one-seventh were buried either as paupers or criminals at the public expense. In London there were 200,000 one roomed tenements, in some cases with eight residents. Eight hundred cubio feet of air space was now reckoned the proper amount for each person and yet the average of the country was only 300. Twenty-five, persons per acre was the proper proportion of population and yet in some parts there were 350. It was impossible for an imperial race to be raised under such conditions and it was high treason to God to imagine that religion contemplated such human misery. Continuing the Bishop said it was an utter delusion to think that the Church was opposed to the poor, or that they were not welcome there. That was one of the greatest delusions which could possibly exist. The Church was anxious to do her utmost on behalf of the down trodden people of tUis country. His lordship declared himself a strong Christian Socialist. He believed that the individual should be subordinate to the community in every important respect, and that every individual in the country should have a chance to live under decent conditions. But they must look upon moral conditions as well as study political economy. He personally was in favour of land ownership being regarded as simply trusteeship, of the abolition of entail a graduated income tax, measures dealing with the whole system of bequests and also the giving to municipalities of more power over the drink and housing questions. But don't let them think that these could be attained by a violent bursting up of the pre-ont conditions of Society. It was a great social delusion to believe that, as it would only result in a great deluge of bloodshed, followed most probably by the country being com- pletely ruined. It would be no panacea to bring about enormous changes by violent means. Socialism of the class advocated by Karl Marx had been tried but it never succeeded. The spiritual factor was the impor- tant thing in the business. The most successful solution of the social problem would be the realisation of the saying of the Great Teacher," Love thy neighbour as thyself." This (Friday) evening the Venerable Archdeacon ' Madden will preach on" Temperance and Maishood."</p>
      </div>
