      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>GARDEN ALLOTMENTS.</head>
        <p>GARDEN ALLOTMENTS.</p>
        <p>; ence, on behalf of the tiorough of Warriniton, on the : occasion of the death of his Majesty King Christian of The monthly meeting of the Warrington Town Denmark, had been laid before their Majestie , and had Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday, when been graciously received. there were present :—The Mayor (Alderman Bolton), ; PAINTING OF THE MARKET . the deputy-Mayor ( Alderman Fairclongh), Aldermen : Councillor Wilson attended a recent meeting of the John Evans, R. W. FrancomlOW. Pierpoint, J. Roberts, Markets Committee and requested that as there was J. Smethurst. D. Tinhion ; Conncillors T. H. Ashcroft, still great distress among painters in the town the J. Ball, T. Barton, W. Benson, W. Brough, T. Chantlerf number employed on the Markets at one time should W. Clegg, J. G. Craik,.J. Dickenson, James Evans, L. be increased. It was resolved to leave the matter in Ellison, W. J. Forshaw, Dr. Joseph, J. Lord. A. Peacock, the hands of the Borough Surveyor. J. Pemberton, J. Sankey, J. Skelland, J. F. Smith, F. , Alderman ROBERTS in moving the confirmation of Stott, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson i Messrs. A. T. the minutes said that toe work on the Markets had Hallway (deputy town clerk). J. Fairhurst (borough never been so well done since there was a market. treasurer), I'. Longdin (borough surveyor), J. Deas , (water engineer), W GARDEN ALLOTMENTS.. S. Haddock (gas engineer), J. G. i Gornall (medical officer), J. M. Murray (director of ' The Gas Committee recommended "that the Engineer education). be instructed to let the waste land round Longford Gas VOTES OF CONDOLENCE. i Works for the purpose of garden allotments." ! Councillor STOTT remarked that some months ago The MAYOR said he again had the sad duty to per- several applications were made by ratepayers under the form of reporting the deaths of two gentlemen who at Garden Allotments Act for plots at Latchford, but the one time sat as members of this Council—Mr. Robert ' Council had heard nothing of the negotiations. If it Dobson, sen., and Mr. Henry Brown White, and in i was the intention to let this land at Longford for that doing so he was sure he would express the sorrow of , purpose he was afraid the original applicant s,who were the members of the Council at the loss of two worthy 1 from the Latchford end, would not find it of much use townsmen. Mr. Dobson, though a man who had not ! m it would be extremely inconvenient to walk from been before the public for many years, was one for Latchford to Winwick-toad, to avail themselves of the whom they had the greatest respect. He proposed a vote off. of condolence to the family. In regard to Mr. White. 1 er - ' The MAYOR : I understand the Chairman of the said his Worship, his connection with us was of more I 1 Allotments Sub-committee will report at the next recent date, as up to 1902 he held the responsihle meeting. position of Magistrates' Clerk for the borough. In the i Alderman FAIRCLOUOII : This land near the Gas performance of the many onerous duties which it fell to ' Works was offered to our own men for gardens, but they his lot to perform Mr. White acted impartially and fear- • would not have it. You had better lay a tramway to lessly, and he could only add that it is with regret Chatmoss so that men can be taken to the allotments they had Lo mourn his loss. He moved a similar vote , there. There is no possibility of getting land for of condolence. !allotments in Warrington for 60,000 people. You can only The resolutions were approved.' apply it to about half a dozen. EDUCATION COMMITTEE REPRESENTATIVES. j Counpillor ELLISON said the Allotments Committee 1 A WOMAN WANTED. . I had had several interviews with applicants, but the . . . . .. . .. .</p>
        <p>The Council then i;r7oCe-e-iad'i-O-ap-e—oin't 14 members of the Council as representative members of the Educa- tion Committee. The MAYou!rnoved that the 14 retiring members of the Council be re-elected as representative members of the Education Committee as follows :—Alderman Bennett, Pierpoint, Bolton, Fairclough. Smethurst, Tinnion and Roberts ; Councillors Brough, Craikt Ellison, Evans, Forshaw, Hulse and Wilkinson. Alderman PIERPOENT protested against his name havin.,e been left on the list. He had said before that he would retire. That was his intention, and nothing they might try to do would prevent him from carrying it out. Councillors Clegg, Skelland, Peacock and Dr. Joseph were nominated for the vacancy caused by the retire- ment of Alderman Pierpoint, and upon a vote being taken Councillor Dr. Joseph was elected. The remain- ing 13 members were re-elected. The next business was the appointment of two selected members of the Education Committee on the recommendation of the Committee, and nine co-optative members of the Education Committee on the recom- mendation of the following bodies :—Two by the Association of Church of England Elementary Schools within the borough and one each by the Association of Roman Catholic Elementary Schools within the borough the Association of Unsectarian Elementary Schools within the borough, the governors of the Warrington Grammar School, the Council of the Warrington Diocesan Training College, the Warrington branch of the National Union of Teachers, the Working Men's Committee of the Warrington Infirmary, and the War- rineton Traces and Labour Council. Alderman PIERPOINT asked if it was not part of the scheme that a woman should be a member of the Education Committee. lhe Mayon said that was so. it was hoped that the ' Training Colleze vroulcl send s representative, but they sent the Rev. Canon Morley Stevenson. The scheme did not seem to be workable and they would ask the Board of Education to give them a scheme which would be more practicable. Alderman SMETHURST proposed that Messrs. A. J. Jolley and T. May be appointed selected members of I the Education Committee. Alderman PIERPOINT pointed out that nine-tenths of i the elementary school teachers were girls and women, and should they not have a woman adviser on the com- mittee ? ~ Councillor PEACOCK said that he would propose that a lady should be appointed. The MEYOR. : We have not the power according to the Act. The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK said the suggested appoint- , ments could only be confirmed or sent back. Councillor PEACOCK moved that the recommendation be referred back,and upon a vote being taken the Mayor declared the amendment to be carried. Councillor WILKINSON proposed that the list of nine co-optative members be referred back to the Education Committee in order that a lady should be one of the selected members. Councillor LORD asked if they were bound to adopt the nominees ? The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK: No ? You can refer any back. Councillor LORD accordingly moved that the recom- mendation be referred back. Alderman TINNION pointed out that if the amendment was carried the Education Committee would be in a difficulty. Before the next meeting of the Council :the committee hoped to deal with the scheme and he did not see that any harm would therefore be done by appointing the gentlemen recommended until the scheme I had been revised. After further discussion names of the co-optative members were approved as submitted. REPRESENTATIVE MANAGERS. The Council then appointed Me. Bibby and Councillor Ellison representative managers of St. James School in place of the late Mr. R. G. Henshall and the late Alderman Hutchinson. THE EDUCATION ESTIMATES. DECREASE IN THE COST OF THE "BOLTON" SCHOOL. Alderman PieuporYr (in the absence of Alderman ' Bennett), moved the approval of the Education Com- mittees estimates. He said it would have been , pleasanter tohave presented estimates showing that the cost of education was going to be less, but under pre- ! sent conditions it was impossible to do that. The ' estimates would increase from time to time. The com- mittee were trying to be as economical as possible in' the matter of administration. The estimate for the ; "Bolton" School had been reduced by £9OO, which ' showed that the committee were taking steps in the right direction. (Hear, hear.) The increased educa- tion deficiency was £2,889, and that would require an extra rate of 3d. Councillor Eursov seconded. The MAYOR explained that the reduced cost of the " Bolton " school had been arrived at by doing away with certain elaborations originally contemplated, and , by substituting terracotta for stone. Altogether the reductions amourited to £963. £429 was practically for work which would belong to the next school which was built. Alderman PIERPO[NT quoted Birkenhead and ten other Comity Boroughs which. he said, were similar to War- rington, and in which the average cost of education was £1 ss. 6d. per head as against 139. 10d. in Warrington. The Council would be pleased to hear that there was a difference of lls. Bd. per head in favour of Warrington. (Hear, bear.) Councillor EVANS said that the comparisons of the average cost per hear? for education which Alderman Pierpoint had made did not altogether, apply. Liverpool and some of the other places'f had had their Board schools built when the new educa- tion authority came into vogue. They had to build their schools in Warrington yet. In answer to Councillor Lord Alderman PIERPOINT said the alterations had still to receive the sanction of the Education Committee. Councillor PEMBERTON moved that the minutes be ; roferred back. Instead of the committee being economical they were just the reverse. If the corn- I mittee considered the matter again they might make further reductions. If they had eight or nine more 1, schools to build the cost to the ratepayers would be enormous. Warrington was in a far different position , to other places as she had been magnificently served with a systena of private education, and the ratepayers ; had not been called upon to pay anything for many I years. The commits° were spending too fast—not</p>
        <p>negotiations had all failed. He mentioned the matter to the Gas Committee, and they said they had some land which might be used for the purpose, hence the ' resolution that night. Councillor PEMBERTON suggested that allotments might be made in Victoria Park. The system was a paying one as had been found by the Altrincham District Council, who made a good revenue out of it. The DEPUTY TOWN CLERK said these garden allot- , iaents had nothing to do with the Allotment Act. I The matter then dropped and the minutes were con- firmed. , THE MAYOR AND THE WATER COMMITTEE. The Water Committee had decided that the Mayor , should be appointed a member of the committee in place of the late Alderman Hutchinson. Councillor LORD said he wished to move an amend- ment to that as the Mayor was an ex-officio member of the committee and there was no necessity for him to be appointed separately. Was that the means of keeping another man off the committee? Councillor PEMBERTON seconded, and said as the Mayor was an ex-officiornember,another member should be appointed because the committee would need plenty ofistrong members. I The MAYOR explained that he was previously a main- her of the Water committee, but he gave way to Corm- I cillor Stott. Now a vacancy had occurred he wished to Ibe again put on the committee, as otherwise he would cease to be a member of it when his terra of office as Mayor expired. After further discussion, Councillor LORD withdrew the amendment, and the minutes were approved. A RESIGN ATION. At a meeting of the Electricity and Tramways Com- mittee a letter was read from Mr. Alderman Tinnion resigning his seat on this committee, and it was resolved that it be accepted. Alderman SMETHURST, without comment, moved the confirmation of the minutes of this committee, Councillor WILKINSON seconded. Councillor SToTr asked whether he would be in order in proposing that Councillor Ball be elected to fill the vacancy which had been occasioned by the retirement of Alderman Tinnion. Councillor LORD : It would not be in order. The MAYOR: All you can do is to send the matter back. Bring it before the next committee meeting. Councillor STOTT : I understand the Council have power to fill any vacancy on a committee. The MAYOR : You are out of order in discussing the matter according to Standing Order 38 which reads.— "Every vacancy on any committee of the Council shall be notified to the Council at their meeting next after such vacancy, and the Chairman, or some other member of the committee, upon notice of motion, or upon the recommendation of the committee which shall be speci- ally mentioned in the Council summons, may move to I appoint a member of the Council whose name shall be I mentioned in the notice of motion or recommendation to supply the vacancy." Councillor STOTT lam satisfied. I don't want the Council to act in an inconsistent manner. IThe minutes were then approved. TO AMEND THE EDUCATION SCHEME. The committee have resolved that the following I members be appointed a sub-committee to consider and report upon the advisability of amending the education scheme :—Alderman Bennett, Smethurst and Tinnion ; Councillors Evans, Fulton-Smith and Pemberton. FRIENDrX SOCIETIES' REPRESENTATIVE. It was resolved. "That the friendly societies be requested to send delegates (in the proportion of one delegate to every 250 members) to a meeting at the Town Hall in order that they may select their repre- sentative on the Education Committee for recommenda- tion to the Council." STANDING ORDERS. It was resolved, "That a committee consisting of all the members of the Council be appointed to consider and report upon the revision of Standing Orders." On the motion of the MAYOR, seconded by the DEPUTY-MAYOR, the minutes were agreed to without comment. MAYOR AND MAYORESS'S SILVER WEDDING. The MAYOR at the conclusion of the meeting said : It was my silver wedding yesterday, and I shall be Pleased if all the members will drink my health. (Hear, hear, and applause). Alderman SMETFIURST said he had great pleasure in proposing long life and happiness to the Mayor and Mayoress. ( Applause). The members then retired into the large committee room, when the health or the Mayor and Mayoress was enthusiastically drunk.</p>
        <p>Cadbury's is abso• lutely pure cocoa. It is more than a drink—an excellent food and its regular use is conducive to good health and bodily vigour.</p>
