      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 10, 1906.</p>
        <p>plete control of the schools of this country. The Lloyd George's, the Birrell's, the men of individualistic tem- peraments and, if they liked, of self-conceited intellects, 1 who thought they could control a question of that kind and get the people of England to give them unlimited licence to do so were very readily and easily , dealt with. They would notice how 1 Mr. Birrell had now come down frhm , his high horse, how Mr. Lloyd George would leave a I semblance of the Bible in the schools, and that even Dr. Macnamara would keep the Bible there. Did they think for a moment that if the Liberal Government felt itself strong enough that it would be able to carry an ; Education Bill against the conseusus of the religious people of this country? (" No.") In that House of I 670 members there were 70 Liberals who were committed i to the Catholic electors demanding pledges from them. lln addition they had the support of 163 Conservative members and 85 Irish members when he had before , described as the watchdogs and sentinels of I the Faith, and there were in the Labour party 20 who dare not oppose the Catholic deman4s. He defied the Liberal party to prodele a majority of 20 or 30 over th it combined elf )rt, and if that I were so they must not be afraid of the result. They were apt to forget in the discussion of political matters that when questions were to be carried safely I through the [louse of Commons with large majorities, there was even then the ultimate resort of the House of ' Lords to see that the liberties and the religion of the ; people were protected! Though he was not usually an ; advocate or a defender of the House of Lords, still he ' I was ready in a matter of religion even to make the devil ; quote scripture and use it as his owe. (Laiighter.) The Coustitution of the country was such that the House of , ! Lords had never been allowed for any length of time ; to resist the often and overwhelmingly expressed wish of I I the House of Cornmons,but if, the House of Lords found 1 that a Bill for the destruction of the voluntary schools 1 I had been carried by a majority of only twenty or thirty, lit ever its existence as a correcting body was justified it ; would be in those circernstances. There was much in the I Education Act which ought to be amended even from the Catholic and the Anglican point of view and if there had been proper organization, if the Catholic !people had been roused three years ago as they were being roused now,the present popular control that they had in the administration of the Act would never have been introduced—they would never have been subject to the intelloctuel government, criticism and super- vision of a Council like the Warrington Town Council ;or that of any other town. (Laughter.) He wished to speak with all respect of the Town Council of Warring- ton, but it was adding insult to injury to say that the teacher of a religion like the Catholic Church whoss antiquity was lost in the mists of time was to ba suddenly addressed to the supervision of a lot of elevated grocers for a short time. (Laughter.) There might legitimately be a difference amongst their higher-placed ecclesiastics and even amongst their priests and the people themselves as to what was the policy to be pursued. There were some who believed in a separate and entirely complete treatment of the Catholics by themselves while others believed in the epirit of the resolution that the control of the schools should be in the hands of Catholic teachers under Catholic management. For his part he preferred the policy of a separate treatment. Education, as far as the State was concerned, was easily fulfilled by the con- ditions laid down as to the attainment of a speci- fied standard in secular subjects. Supposing there were a million Catholic children attending schools and the cost was .£1 per head, why in the name of Heaven should not the authorities give them the £1 per head and keep their prying noses out of the place? (Applause.) There was no \mistaking what Catholics were and there was no division of sects among them. In the Protestant Church—in which he included both the Anglicans and the Nonconformists—the ,contrary was the case. If Nonconformists liked to say to those who represented the Established Church : "We don't want you to put tests on us," by all means let them as it was right and logical. But they must not let them come to Catholics who knew what they were about and could satisfy the srequirements of the State with any standard they named. (Applause.) Many Noncon- formists were with them on the question. Outside the seventy members pledged in the House of Commons there was a vast amount of Nonconformist feeling which was ready to deal with a final aettlement of the Catholic question. He would like to correct the impression which some Catholic parents held—that they were the first and final source with regard to the con- trol and educp.tioa of their children. That was not so. The Divine command was given to the Church to- "'go and teach all nations" and the Church had never surrendered it. Someone had said something that night about "Rome on the rates." She had a right to be on the rates for it would be a long time before she got back her own. (Applause.) Mr. Madden referred to the great self-sacrifice which the building of the Catholic schools had entailed, and said that not one school had yet been surrendered. (Applause.) Their Anglican friends had surrendered 1,000 so that there was evidently a Spirit of compromise amongst them. Catholics did not intend to give up anything, and to preserve their schools they would fight ' to the finish. (Applause.) They wanted everyone out of the Catholic school except the Catholic. They would take Protestant money if the authorities liked --(laughter)—and they would in return furnish the State with the finished article. (Applause.) The Church aonf dßwomheenmßaodnelenohcaodmspprookmeinsetahveitrhe i:vaaean ao with timee case. (Applause.) In this matter it would not be, as I was said by a patriarchal canon of the Catholics Church in Manchester—Canon Lynch--the pantomime of passive resistance. It would not be a sorry procession of gentlemen led by their ministers going down to the Police Court and trying to get off the payment of a rate while someone sneaked behind their backs and paid it for them. It, would not be that pantomime--it would be one which touched the reason of their life and their existence. All politics faded beside the grandeur of the Catholic question. (Applause.) The glory of the empire was nothing beside the price of the soul of one Catholic child. Let the Government be seduced and led away by its Nonconformist friends into an attempt to destroy Catholic/schools and the result would be that on the following Monday morning not one Catholic child in I the land would darken a school door. (Loud Applause). They were accused of an attempt to evangelise England and to bring it back to the days of Augustine. By all means they wished to. (Applause). Continuing 1 Mr. Madden saw they had to put back-bone into some ' of the hesitating Anglicans who stood trembling on the brink. He believed the large percentage of the Protestant ministers of to-day had been convinced long ago of the truth of the faith of Peter. They hesitated because they were so mixed up with society and ha twhiev ecsata hn ad mlic ci hl iaersa fho. r Iwhomf tthey yt the saw was the owpaein ultgherine would be such an Anglican movement as would show the Nonconformist body what was in store for them. It , wns'a matters well andt wbhea havoenrestweaantddatraalgthhatfcoarwthaardliasinotEhaegse. I I land would not go down (Loud Applause). , Mr. G. N. VERNON seconded, and said that on that primary question Catholics of all political parties were united. He contended that under the Acts of 1870 and 1902 Nonconformists had derived more advantages than any other body in the country. The Board schools were attended for the greater part by Nonconformists and the Act of 1870 was practically an endowment of Nonconformist teaching. Under the necv Act Nonconformists had nothing to complain about, ' one point being that the provided schools built by the ratepayers were acceptable to them. The passive resisters had made themselves the laughing- stock of the country. Nonconformists had not done their duty in the cause of education, and Catholics were not now going to stand by and see their children robbed of their faith without a struggle. If the Government, which had been helped into power to a great extent by Catholic votes, dared to pass any measure which would take away their schools and the rights of Catholic parents to have their children taught tue Catholic religion by Catholic teachers, the day, would come when there would be another election, and he did not think there was a Catholic who would give a Liberal half a vote if he bad a thousand. (Applause.) They were not asking for separate treatitent. They wanted their rights as Catholic citizens, and they did not wish to deny those rights to members of any other denomination. (Applause),</p>
        <p>s 'Hie resolution was passed with enthusiasm. Mr. P. DELANEY moved : "That the foregoing resolutions be signed on our behalf by the chairman of the meeting, and by the secretary, and that copies of the same together with the number of people present at the meeting, and the number of Catholic children attending our schools, be forwarded without delay to the Prime Minister, the Minister of Education,our own Member of Parliament, the Archbishop of Westminster, the Bishops of the Liverpool and Shrewsbury, and the Catyolic Council of Education."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. McAvov seconded and said he did not think he was betraying any secret when he said that the Borough Member was already taking some steps to enforce their views on the Government. (Applause.) The Rev. Father Wir,spNslapported: It had,ho said,been dinned into his ears for a long time that the agitation 'of Catholics about their schools was the work of the priests only. That, however,was discontinued entirely in 1902 because then the boot found itself upon the other foot and the agitators were not Catholic priests but other reverend gentlemen—(laughter)—who took pattin the passive resistance movement. That meet-</p>
