        <p>Notice. The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday afternoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Tn-day after- noon without extra charge. Address all conamunica, tions to" Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington WEDDING FResearrs.—Large selection of Useful and Ornamental Articles.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. . 1 1 The Parr Hall was crowded on Suneay evening when Coming Events a meeting of Roman Catholics was held to consider the It will be seen from our advertising columns that Education question. At the conclusion of the services Mr. T. Brough's well-known Juvenile Choir are giving at the Churches many members of the congregations a performance in the Parr Hall on Monday evening a most in precession to the hall and the meeting was t enthusiastic one. Mr. Owen Toole president of next. This choir is undoubtedly a very fine combine-the n&amp;atholic Young Men's Society, occupied the chair tion and the programme submitted for this special and WM, supported on the platform by most of the local event is of a most novel and attractive order and is ' priests and many prominent laymen. The proceedings certain to delight everyone present. The purity of opened with the stirring hymn" Arm for deadly fight", tone, balance and blend of voices is truly exceptional, - Mr. J. B. Caldwell presiding at the organ. and all who hhve not yet heard this clever little corn- I The CHAIRMAN, having read a letter of apology for . non-attendance from Mr. George Bartley, said he would pany of talented singers should take this opportunity , like it to be miaerstood that that meeting had not been of doing so. I called to in any way offer opposition to any of their J, R. WOOD, Piano Dealer and Tuner,Friars-Gate, : fellow-townspeople as to the religious views they held. and 47, Old London-road., Stockton Heath.—Advt. I They were present with the object of protecting their , own Catholic religion. It had been rumoured in the Personal. I town that they were getting up a kind of missionary Mr. C. E. Parker (president) and the Rev. J. F. ; meeting, and he had therefore offered a few words of Mathews (senior hon. secretary of the local Free explanation so that there should be no misunderstand- ing.: Whatever alteration of the Education Act might Church Council) have been the Warrington delegates 1 be made which would affect Catholics in the slightest to the National Council meeting held in Birmingham ,' degree, they would resist to the utmost any change this week. 1 which would take away from them the rights which they A marriage has been arranged, and will take place 'at present enjoyed. (Applause.) Mr. Birrell had at Helsby in April, between Dr. Peter Thompson, ' complained of the apathy of parents on the Professor of Anatomy, Kings College, London, and question, but surely he did not intend his remarks Nessv, the eldest daughter of Mr. G. L. Davies, J.P. t!'° apply to Catholics, because there was a the Old Hall, Helsby, Cheshire. ' striking monument to their interest in education I in the shape of the 1,100 schools which they had erected ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL 1 (Applause). Those schools were built with one object WATERS.—Advt. , and that was to provide their children first of all with a , Catholic education. (Applause). They knew the The Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron ' sacrifices which Catholics had to make in Order to build Company Limited. , those schools and thus to do what they considered their callutotyraftoioratheir was trieeli:aizeo.moHoef the cry The Directors have decided to pay an Interim Dividend at the rate of four per cent, per annum, free previous to the general election of "Rome on the of income tax, on the ordinary shares, and the usual rates." He thought that was one ,of the greate