      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>BIG MEETING OF ROMAN CATHOLICS AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BIG MEETING OF ROMAN CATHOLICS AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Strong Resolutions Passel.</p>
        <p>THREATENED WITHDRAWAL OF SMODL CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S C9UGHS. A WHOLE FAMILY CURED BY VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. DEXTER, Wholesale Fish Merchant, Lowes- toft, writes :—" My children have suffered from bronchitis and bad colds at this time of the year for several years, but thanks to VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE they are much better and we very seldom hear them cough now. I might also say I had my little boy, aged three years, bad with croup, but thanks to your Cough Cure he soon recovered. It is pleasant to kno.v there is something to cure troublesome coughs and croup in children."</p>
        <p>VENO'S LboxTNING Couott CURE is absolutely the most efficient remedy procurable for children's coughs. It cures whooping cough and croup rapidly, strengthens the lungs, and makes children less susceptible to colds. , Sold everywhere by Chemists at 9A., is, Ed. and 2s. 9d.</p>
        <p>I ing was evidence if any were needed that when 1 Mr. Birrell asked for the opinion of parents in the case lof Catholics as distinct from the priests, he was guilty lof an inwertinence. He would be made aware of the ' fact that the priests had been speaking with the same voice ad their people, and he would probably get very little relief from that. He confessed they had only 1 voiced but too feebly, timidly, and often too tardily I what they had felt so deeply and endured so silently for so long, but now Mr. Birrell would find no discord among them. It was their time to speak and, he hoped, to act. In appealing to parents Mr. Birrell would no doubt realise that he had appealed from the frying pan to the fire.(Applause.)They must not be over-sanguine and think the battle was over. Mr. Birrell had said that the election had vindicated and established the principles that public money carried with it complete public con- trol and that no tests as to the religious beliefs of the teachers mist be imposed in publicly-maintained schools He added that if the Government built on that ground they were safe. His (Father Wilson's) opinion was that if Mr. Birrell built on that ground and applied those principles he would find he had been building on a volcano. (Applause). The mountain was already in labour. There had been rumblings and angry sotinds issuing from it for two or three weeks in all parts of the country end especially in Lancashire—(applause)— and ho would find that what came forth from the mountain would not be the ridiculous mouse of passive resistance. (Laughter and hpplause). As Mr. Madden had said that if the Act were altered in the direction indicated, on a certain Monday morning the schools would be empty. (Applause), Father Wilson spoke of the self-sacrifice of both parents and teachers the former in building the schools and the teachers in producing quite as good results as their fellow teachers in the Board schools at one half the salary. (Applause.) They claimed, he continued, that the question muse be so settled that every Catholic parent would be able to find in the elementary schools the opportunity of having his child educated in entire and complete conformity with his religious convictions. Under the Act of ,1902 they surrendered to a large extent their freedom of the schools and they had been hampered in many ways. It was impossible that they Mould return to the days of starvation grants and of sath.ries that were not a living wage. What they most demand and go any length in securing, and block the way entirely until they did secure it, was the opportunity of having their children educated in accordance with their religious beliefs - (Applause.) The Rev. FATHER WHITTLE also urged the audience to hand on the precious jewel of the faith, which had come to them through years of tribnlation.The Noncon- formists offered them a luke warm and milk and water religion but they would have none of it. (Applause.) On the motion of Mr. EL RIGBY seconded by Mr. A. MARSH a vote of thanks was accorded to the speakers. The Chairman having been thanked the meeting closed with the singing of" Faith of our Fathers."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ENCOURAGEMENT FROM ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ENCOURAGEMENT FROM ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>For those who are - sick it is like a peep into Ilbe garden of hope to receive news which gives them assur- ance of permanent recovery. Many of us will read with delight this encouraging news from St. Helens, " have no objection to the facts of my cure by Doan's backache kidney pills being published," says Mrs. E. Astles, 14, Crook-street, St. Efelens, because they have done me so much good, and I think it is only right that I should let others know how reliable the medicine is."</p>
        <p>" For three years I was a great sufferer from weak kidneys. I had dull, gnawing pains in the small of my back. I could scarcely get up and down stairs, and to raise myself 'after sitting was a difficult matter. Some- times my back would crick and seemed as if it was giving out. I often had to take hold of something for support or I should have fallen; bending with me was quite out of the question. I had medicine from t'ne doctor, and I had my back rubbed with oils, but I received no benefit.</p>
        <p>"When-I began- with Doan's backache kidney pills and found they were doing me good, I kept lon with them. Now that I have used four boxes of the pills I am cured. Doan's pills are excellent, and I cannot praise them too highly. (Signed). Emily Astles." - Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box (six boxes for thirteen shillings and ninepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Be sure you get exactly the same kind of pills that Mrs. Astles had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>. xes, ne says flea give ms ate ror me.- Madge "Take him. His life's insured for twenty thousand. pounds."</p>
        <p>SOB: "'Are you superstitious about the number thirteen ?" He: "No, indeed. If a girl refused me thirteen times I'd know I was in great luck not to marry such aa obstinate, hard-headed girl." "Then eggs are older than history," whispered the sweet singer, as she broke the third shell. "Per- haps they are the Lays of Ancient Rome,'" said the comedian boarder, who reads classics during the winter months.</p>
        <p>FARMER SKINFLINT (reading sign): " Eyesight Tested Free uv Charge.' By Gosh! Mandy, in I go an' find out if it's hurtin' my eyesight tew read the paper." Mandy: "An' if it is, are yew goin tew squander good money on spectacles ?" Farmer Skinflint "No, sir-ree; lw goin' tew give up the paper." Mee. HENRY PECK "First we get horseless carriages and then wireless telegraphy. I wonder what next ?" Her Husband (meekly): "Wifeless matrimony, perhaps." LITTLE ETHEL (at breakfast): "Mv egg is quite cold; is yours?" Little Tommy: " tes. I wonder why it is ?" Little Ethel: "I expect cook made a mistake and boiled them in cold water."</p>
        <p>A PHILOSOP/lE-R-88;;;:li-a -;;;;11 ..c-Zia see the girl he loves best eat breakfast alone, when nobody was watching her, we would have:many more old bachelors!</p>
        <p>Miss RICKETTS : "It is said that the contact of lips in the dark evolves a visible spark." Mr. Spudds : "That is very interesting. I had never heard of it! but we'll turn down the gas and do some experiment- ing !" " THAT mob scene was handled with splendid effect," said the critic. "Oh, yes," replied the manager. "You see, we engage the villain's creditors to go on in that scene."</p>
        <p>- "I SAY, Pat, did you count au those pigs ?" "Every mother's son of them except one." "And why did you not count him ?" " Arrah, then, sir, but he was so frisky, and ran so much about, I couldn't do it at all at all." EVEN the most industrious Izaker seems to encour- age loafing. TINE saved may be money earned, but banks do not compute interest on that principal. " I DEPEND upon you," said the chandelier to the ceiling. "All right," was the reply. "Hang on for all you are worth." HEWITT : "You knew my wife before I did, I believe ?" Jewett: " Yes ; she once did me a great favour." "How was that ?" " She refused to marry me !" . .</p>
        <p>GLIDDEN : "Your lawn always looks in fine shape. How do you manage it ?" Wartner: 'Easy enough, I have two growing boys, and I tell them every day that they mustn't meddle with the lawn-mower or the garden hose. The result is that my lawn is well shorn and well watered all the time l"</p>
        <p>..- " Tun fact is," said the fat man, "I married because I was lonely as much as for any other reason. To put it tersely, I married for sympathy." " said the lean man, "you have mine." AUSTERE LADY " Madam, your child is annoying me with its attention! I wish you would keep it to yourself." Mother (suppressing her anger): "Oh I you must excuse him. He thinks you are his grand- mother, and he is very fond of her." Tun more, you see of some people the less you are satisfied.</p>
        <p>MAGISTRATE: "Where did you get that scar?' Binks : "My wife, your honour." "Ah ! she must be a very energetic woman." " Yes, she always strikes while the iron is hot." "YES, indeed. Brown is a very sick man." "When was he taken ill ?" " Yesterday morning I was called to attend him." " Ah! that accounts for it." And now those two physicians pass each other as perfect</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>RESCUED BY BILE BEANS.</head>
        <p>RESCUED BY BILE BEANS.</p>
        <p>Collier Crippled by Rheumatism and Indigestion.</p>
        <p>The value of Bile Beans in the treatment of maladies peculiar to miners and all out-door workers is proved by the experience of Mr. H. C. Strongroom, of Bear Park Colliery, Durham, who says : " I have worked in this district for 28 years. I never had a day's illness in my life until I was seized with rheumatism. I bad been working for a year or two in wet seams, but felt no worse for it until I suddenly changed to dry work for a few days. The change seemed to leave me with a weary feeling, and one morning I woke up with a hot pain in my right ankle and had to creep downstairs. As I had never had such a pain before I didn't know •what to make of it. The doctor thought I must have injured the bone or sprained my ankle, but on the third day he said it was rheumatism. The pain got worse and worse until I had to be lifted in and out of bed. My appetite com- pletely failed, and the little food I managed to eat was not sufficient for a child. I sank so rapidly that the neighbours thought I was going to die. "Ode day a friend told me about Bile Beans, and I decided to try them, although many so-called remedies I had tried had failed altogether to bring we relief. I soon began to fc3el the benefit of Bile Beans. Before I had finished the first box I could eat as well as ever, and the rheumatism was much better. Before the second box was ended I was able to get back to work again. That was two years ago, and 1 have been well ever since .Bile Beaas were the first and only medicine that put my stomach right, and cured the rheumatic pains." Mr. Strangroom's trouble arose from digestive dis- orders. It may seem to you a far cry from acidity of the stomach to rheumatism, but doctors agree that rheumatism is caused by an excess of acid in the blood. Exposure to cold and wet weather does not in itself cause rheumatism ; but helps to crystallize the acid in the muscles and joints, thus causing stiffness, swelling and hot pains. Bile Beans cure rheumatism by correcting the digestive disorders, cleansing the blood, and getting rid of the accumulated acid crystals. Bile Beans are purely vegetable, and invaluable for regular household use, because they always do good. Sold only in sealed boxes of all chemists, or post free from the Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 4, ltedcross Street, London, LC., at is. IA. and 2s. 9d. (2s. 9d. size contains three times as many beans as the is. size).</p>
      </div>
