      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>_Dear Uncle Ned,-1 hope you are quite well. 'loll must not think lam not taking any interest in the "Children's Corner."</p>
        <p>I have not written to you lately, but I look forward to Thursday, the day the paper comes, and at breakfast-time I never fail to say, "Has the paper come, mother?" I always read with great in- terest the contents of the " Children's Corner."</p>
        <p>With regard to the competition for doll- dressing, I am sending you a doll which I have dressed, but its shoes and stock- ings I bought, and I hope the little girl who gets the doll will like it. I also enclose a contribution to the "Children's Corner," which I am sure you will like. It is a piece of poetry called "Visitors." I wish we had had more snow this winter, for I enjoy playing with it very much.</p>
        <p>The other day my cousin Nel,lie came to tea; and she 7played, twelve games of draughts with my mother.</p>
        <p>Hoping you ani all my cousins are well and happy, I remain, your loving little niece, MARGARET MARY PRESS.</p>
        <p>VISITORS. When visitors come, we sit in a row, As prim as prim can be; Our faces are washed, our hairs are brushed, And,miserable things are we. When visitors come, our cook is cross, And nurse is cross as well; And Mary is always so busy, Because she answers the visitors' bell.</p>
        <p>The ladies ask how old we are, And how we get on;ait school; Father says we're all very good, But he doesn't think that, as a rule.</p>
        <p>They tell us all to be quiet and good, Like china dogs on the shelves; But we always think it seems rather absurd, When they talk so much themselves.</p>
        <p>But we try to be good, for mother's sake, Whatever the visitors say;</p>
        <p>MEN</p>
        <p>flc Iway glvU6 trie rest or cake - When the party has gone away.</p>
        <p>- : : A CONTRIBUTION FROM MAY</p>
        <p>, . AVERY. WERE J THE SUN. I'd always shine on holidays, Were I the sun;</p>
        <p>On sleepy heads I'd never gaze, 'But focus, all my morning rays On busy folks of bustling ways, Were I the sun. I would not melt a ,Fledding snow,</p>
        <p>, Were I the sun;- ' Nor spoil the ice where skaters go; Nor help those useless weeds to grow; But 'hurry melons on, you know,</p>
        <p>W' ere I the min. I'd warm the swimming pool just right,</p>
        <p>Were I the sun-; _ On school days I would hide my light, "Half term" I'd always give you bright, Nor set so soon on Christmas night,</p>
        <p>Were I the sun. I would not heed such paltry toys,</p>
        <p>Were I the 'sun.; ; Such works as grown-up men employs; But I would favour solid joys; In short, I'd run the world for ;boys,. Were I the sun.</p>
        <p>ANSWERS TO PUZZLES. Buried Cities.—Paris, Madrid, Berlin, Naples. Buried Flowers.—Pink, Aster, Lily, Lobelia. Buried Girls' Names.—lda, Nora, Mabel. Puzzle.—A Book.</p>
