      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>I TJNIXED BAND OF HOPE INIEETiNG.—There was a large 'attendance at the meeting of the Hill Clifle And Stockton Heath Band of Hope which was held in the Providence Schoolroom on Monday evening. Unusual interest was taken in the proceedings, it being "the children's night,"and the meeting was entirely conducted by the younger members of the society. After the open- ing hymn and prayer, the Rev. .1. S. Hughes introduced Miss'Edith Hamblett, who presided over the first part of the programme. The young lady speaker was IMiss Lily Powell whose maiden speech was delivered in a very clear and audible voice, her expression being very good indeed. The second part of the programme Was in the hands of the boys. Master Arthur Hughes, who also delivered a little speech, discharged the duties of chairman in a very pleasing manner, and Master Thomas I Richmond delivered a capital little speech upon" Why Ihe belonged to the Band of Hope." During the even- ing songs were creditably given by Edith finches. Carrie Cain, Elsie Whitfield, Elsie Martin, Sarah Bate, Mary Earl and May Isherwood. Master A. Dutton con- tributed a humorous song which was much enjoyed. The action songs " Little tambourines "and "Washing day" were Wf 11 given by a number of girls, as were also the dialogues "Teetotal workers " by G. Hughes, M. Richmond, J. Forsyth, A. Forsyth, T. Bennett and E. Bennett," A birthday present "by Ada Mounfleld and Edith Julian, " Bridget,s tale" by Emma Hamblett, M. Earle, M. Mounfteld, R. Arnold, and May • Isherwood, the boys' dialogues being "Fishing " by A. Whitfield and H. Isherwood, "I would not be a publican " by H. Earle, H. Jones, E.' Musson and A. Whitfield. Recitations were well given I by G. Cliffe. E. Grounds, E. Whitfield, A. Chile, E. , 'Hughes, T. Pearce and A. Isherwood, whilst Master J. Whitfield contributed selections on the gramophone. At the close of the proceedings the Rev. J. S. Hughes expressed his delight with the manner the youthful per- formers had acquitted thFmselves, and presented eight hooks (which had been given by a lady present) to those who had taken their parts the be,tt on the evening's per- formance. Master G. A. Whitfield acted as accom- panist.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLURES.—According to " Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of Commercial Failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, March 2nd, was 176, viz. :—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette, 98 ; Deeds of Arrangement,registeLed,7B. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were : Bankruptcies, 97 ; Deeds of Arrangement, 92—total 189 ; being a decrease of 13. The total num- ber of Cornmerciel Failures recorded during the 9 weeks of the present year is 1,505 •, the total number recorded in the corresponding 9 weeks of last year was 1,042, showing a decrease of 137. One might well have some respect for a woman who could tell a good black lie ; one has none for a woman everlastingly telling half-truths.—Miss C. A. Barnicoat in the " Fortnightly."</p>
        <p>LALL lituriss liEstmvED.f</p>
        <p>ARE WE SPOILING 0U R CHILDREN?</p>
        <p>W. PETT RIDGE (Author of `! Nord Ent' ly," ic.).</p>
        <p>A bachelOr can approach this question with an unbiassed ' mind ; be may be wrong but he is at any rate impartial. Moreover the judgment given here is not Enid a Court of Appeal exists in every household and there my de- cision can be reversed or upheld. Let me say at once that in my opinion the methods of parents are greatly improved, and the spoiling of children becomes less common as the years go on. 'Whilst there is • not BO 1111101 ,of the " Go and see what baby is doing and tell her she mustn't " ; on the other hand the sentimental mother no longer permits her ten year old boy to Wear the long curls which some of us used to pull in school day's with a cry of , " Shop !" Let us argue the matter and see whether we are at one in regard to reasons. Three judges some time since had to hear a case, and they gave their decisions as usual in order of seniority ; Henn Collins coming second said " I agree !" Romer, the third, remarked • " I agree also !" " Pardon me," said Henn Collins, " I wish to give my reasons."' And having stated these at considerable length, he bowed to his colleague to intimate that he had finished.</p>
        <p>....... " I still agree," said Lord Justice Romer. 'There was a time when children called their. fathers " sir !" ; what the' fathers called the children who thus proclaimed servility can be imagined. Those were days. when no house was completely furnished unless it possessd a birch ; when governesses used a ruler mainly for application to little knuckles ; it • was con- sidered right and indeed indispensable that a child should be shaken regularly ; nurses with the tact and intelligence of hens told their charges grisly stories that came back to terrified young minds in hours of sleep. Girls were brought up to be Clinging, and • dependent, with a nice taste in fainting ; boys were cuffed into a show of reverence for their elders. I see ho good reason why the child of 1904 should envy the child of 1854.</p>
        <p>" The good of olden times let others state I think it lucky I was born so late !" THE RELATIONS BETWEEN FATTIER AND SON.</p>
        <p>What is the general spirit existing now between father and son So far as I can see it is one of increased comradeship ; fathers are younger than ever and join in games in which sometimes the Old Boys wiu, sometimes the Old Boys lose; to the good sportsman this 'natters little or nothing. There are indoor recreations now-a- days in which all the femily can join and sons, who are growing ,up and approaching the age when they have the inclination to be-desperate , blades and make their mark somewhere reck- lessly, can be induced (given 'discretion ,to ! parents) to see that home has the attractions possessed by fully licensed premises, without some of the drawbacks to be found there. No better way exists of training children to be good-tempered ; once a boy can lose even a mere game of bagatelle with equanimity, he has been brought far on the road that leads to a sane disposition. The father generally takes charge of his boy .at ten, that being the age when the lad brings home serious tasks from school in regard to • which the mother, goaded by appeals for advice and assistance, generally replies that children who bother mothers about subjects Nvhich mothers learnt years ago at school but have since forgotten are debarred, by a special regulation, from going to Heaven. This is where the wise father who knows his own children, conies in. If he can gain the boy's affection anti respect at this stage, he will never really lose then % The boy may call him " the guv'nor " or " the old 'tin "' or " the pater ; the names will not disguise the fact that he is proud to be his father's son. The greatest error that a father can make is to omit to note the year that is at the top of the current almanack and to forget that his sons grow older each year. There comes a time when they feel they have the right to smoke indoors, to take a glass of claret with their meals, to go on their own account to the play. Happy the father who knows the right moment for acceding to these applications ; for him who unwisely tries to preserve the view that his boy of eighteen ought still to be wearing a sailor-hat with the words " H. M. S. Obediept " there is in store an astonishing disillusion. " Beg pardon, father," says the lad, who wishes to break news gently, "but do you mind , if I bring a friend in to supper this evening r " I'll think it over, my boy !" " For a littleMusic.'!</p>
        <p>" -Does your friZdsa;thg F" " Has a beautiful voice," says the lad, enthusiastically. " Like a nightingale." " Oh," with incredulity. " What age f" " About my own age, lather." " Well, you can bring your friend, but mind it's not to be made a precedent. I don't want the house over-run with them. And no staying late."</p>
        <p>" She'll go bank to her mother's at ten." " She ?" " It's a young lady I've been fond of for some time, father," blurts out the lad, " and we are going to get married soon !" TAX MODERN TREATMENT OF CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The mothers' boys are of the age when they can be placed in corners if their behaviour comes short of absolute perfection, whence they are released on giving their parole. I suppose some little children are still punished severely, but the general trend is certainly in the way of milder correction ; for my own part I would as soon think of whipping a lamb. Even the fiendish parent is now fearful of public opinion, and of the very excellent Society which prose- cutes in such cases ; I like to think that we hear of the instances of cruelty that do exist. There is a pillory for every parent who allows temper to get out of control, and who in this condition maltreats children, and plenty of wil- ling hands are ready to buy eggs and take a straight aim. It is good to know that better care is taken of infants than ever. 'the rate of mortality is still terribly high, and many a mite who has but just left its Maker goes back to him tired of a world for which it was not fully qualified. Thi,s will continue to be the case until mothers of the poorest class are persuaded by competent persons that their methods are inaccurate. I recollect that I once ventured to remonstrate with a mother in lloxton, who was giving a few months old baby some fat meat and a sip of beer, and suggested that boiled milk—. " Excuse me, sir," she interrupted, with the air of one reproving an amateur, " the dear little thing always has what we 'aye !"</p>
        <p>FOOD AND DRESS.</p>
        <p>But mothers of a better kind do seem to take trouble now-a-d to get the right kind of Food for their babes, and most of the infants whom I am able to number amongst my ac- quaintances. 'exhibit less of that fixed stare of repletion than babes did in the years of my youth. lam told they are dressed with something less of barbarity and that common- sense has entered a domain where one had de- cided not to expect it. Children of a slightly larger.growth are certainly apparelled with none of the old eccentricity. I have recollections (which I have tried in vain to efface), of being in my early youth brought up soberly in lient, but of suddenly • astonishing the village and creating something that amounted to the pro- portions of a scandal, by appearing suddenly one Sunday morning as a preposterous High- lander with bare knees and a tartan of some hitherto unknown clan, and having the satis- faction of knowing that every parent beside my own was desolate with envy, impervious to the tcorhings .11,,1 s,.r.r.aoa *Later 4 arueared</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 10, 1906.</p>
        <p>(rather as though -1 were a member' -Of -ft theatrical stock company) variously, as a shivering infant in petticoats, an unhappy French oillt er, a midshipman, an expelled Tut- roman witli fez enmnlete.. Children are not from the river, or tank, or dam at liana, Iwo- bles • out the Spring cart / horse, puts out the , blankets and the crockery, and odds and ends of the camp, and if the drover allows himself the luxury of a tent in case of wet nights, this also must be erected. Then the cook prepares sup- per, and by the time he has everything ready it Is sundown, and the sheep are being yarded into the break, a disagreeable job in very dry weather, as the dust created by five thousand covers everything in the camp. The men get off their horses and tie them up, and moire the sheep slowly into the yard on foot. Then the heavy boughs are drawn across the gateway, and the work of the shepherds is done for the day. They, take the saddles off the horses, and put on the hobbles and bells let them go, in some cases, however, travelli first some distance with them to put them good grass.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>TYPICAL CAMPING , GROUND.</head>
        <p>A TYPICAL CAMPING , GROUND.</p>
        <p>To-night their camp is in a corner of a large paddock, upon the boundary 'fence of a new station. The grass in its vicinity is good short feed for sheep, but of little use for horses, so the drover gives orders for them to be put • through the gate on to the next station, where the tall bluegrass waves among the ,bushes. This of course is, morally speaking, thieving grass, but the drover who will not steal grass for his horses is looked upon with contempt, and indeed will have difficnlty in proceeding far on his way, for the legitimate stock-road is of course eaten bare by the many thousands c‘on- timidly passing along it. The road allowed is—in open country—half a mile in extent ; but this in many places is fenced as narrow as a couple of chains, forming the dreaded "lanes" of the drover, in which there is never grass or water' and some of which extend for many miles at a stretch. The men respond gladly to the cook's sum- mons to supper, and the drover, who has left the saddle on his horse, joins them at the meal. Then he mounts his hungry steed once more, and rides off to the station ahead, five miles .away, to "report," or give notice of his coming on the run to the station manager. After a long canter he reaches the white roofs of the home- stead, glittering in the moonlight. He rides up to the garden fence, and calls, " Anyone in ?" " Hulloh I" comes back from the vine-shaded verandah. "Is the boss there P" A tall man walks across the garden. " I have come," says the drover, "to let you know I will be on your run at daylight to-morrow morning with five thousand wetbers from Merrvburra travelling to Goul burn ."</p>
        <p>"Who is in charge ?" " I am—Duncan McLeod." " All right ! I will send up a man in the morning to clear the station sheep out of your way. liy the way, you can put your horses in that horse , paddock of mine to-night, close to your camp.' " Thank you," says the drover, chuckling softly to himself, for he knows that his hags are already up to their knees in the long lush station grass. " Good-night _ _ _</p>
        <p>" Good-nrght !" and the drover turns his horse, and canters away. Riding briskly back to camp, he does not fail to take notice of the main features of the road which he will have to traverse in the morning ; marking in his mind where the best grass is, which side of a ridge to travel, where to find water for his dinner-camp. and other details. Incidentally he observes that the feed is good all the way along, and he kriows that, if the boundary rider sent out in the morn- ing is not too officious, he will be able to move his sheep slowly, and give them the opportunity of a good graze. The duty of the station man, however, is to keep the drover within the im- aginary line that marks the half mile road, and to keep him travelling forward at the minimum rate of six miles a day—which is required of him by law. Between these classes of men there is much friction, and many a heavy-handed battle is waged upon the plains between the harassed drover and the conscientious servant of the squatter. Arrived at the five mile gate the drover pulls the saddle and bridle off his horse, takes the hobbles from the saddle and puts them on him, and lets him go with the rest. Then he carries his riding gear to the camp, unrolls his blankets lays his head upon the saddle, and lights his pipe under the stars for half an hour's smoke before turning in. • And that i s the Western drover's day.</p>
      </div>
