      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>8</p>
        <p>A PROSPEROUS LOCAL INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Richmond Gas</p>
        <p>Stove and Meter Co., Ltd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>PROPOSED NEW WORKS AT GRAPPEN HALL.</head>
        <p>PROPOSED NEW WORKS AT GRAPPEN HALL.</p>
        <p>A prosperous year was under review at the fifteenth annual general meeting of the shareholders of the Richmond Gas Stove and Meter Co., Ltd., which was held at the offices of the company, Academy-street,War- rington, on Thursday. Mr. Thomas Dwerryhouse (chairman) presided, and there were also present : Messrs. Horace M. Thornton (vice-chairman and manag- ing director), John Coson, J. F. Atkins, M. 8., John A. Ransome (managing director), and J. W. Glover, directors ; Alderman Smethurst, Messrs. Booth, F. .Howard, D. Garnett, C. T. Whitley, E. J. Hall, and Daft, shareholders ; Louis A. Voisey (auditor), and James W. Slack (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>REPORT.</head>
        <p>THE REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Directors reported that the balance standing to the credit of profit and loss account (after deducting debenture interest), including the amount brought forward as per balance sheet, was £33,537 is. 5d., which the directors recommended be appropriated as follows : £33,537 1 5 Depreciation on Buildings, Patterns, Plant, Showroom</p>
        <p>Fixtures Sto ' £5313 410 Dividend on Pre!. Capital at</p>
        <p>the rate of 6 per cent per annum for the year ended December 31st, 1905 Dividend (free of Income Tax) at the rate of 10 per cent per annum on the Ordinary Capital for the year ended December 31st, 1905 Bonus (free of Income Tax) at the rate of 21 per cent per annum 2009 3 0 Reserve Fund (bringing up this fund to £35,000) 27,936 14 7</p>
        <p>Balance to next Account subject to Directors' remuneration</p>
        <p>The business of the Company continued to be satis- factory, and it was gratifying to the directors that they were able, after placing entire cost of advertising as in former years against revenue, to again recommend a dividend at the rate of 10 per cent, and a bonus of 2i per cent in addition. The Company's works and various branches were in good condition and had been maintained Out of revenue. During the year the directors had invested a further sum of £5,079 15s. 9d. out of the Reserve Fund. The retiring directors were Mr. J. Coiron and Mr. J. W. Glover. Their re-election by the Board was reported in accordance with the Company's Articles of Association. The Auditor, Mr. L. A. Voisey, chartered accountant, retired, and was recommended for re-appointment. THE YEAR'S TRADE. PROPOSED NEW WORKS. _</p>
        <p>The CH AIRMAN said he thought they would all agree that the report was a very satisfactory one. The year had been uneventful, except as bringing with it more competition than ever they had had before, but they had dome very well. They had been full of orders and so busy that the working capaoity of the place had been taxed and it had been as much as they could do to turn out work to keep pace with the orders. With regard to the restriction iu the space available at the works, they would remember that some two years ago.he stated that they had taken over some land in Grappenhall which they intended to utilize some time. That time had now arrived. The works were too small altogether, and tbe directors proposed shortly to commence building on that land on a scale that would meet their requirements for some years to come. The land was about 9 acres in extent and they hoped to erect foundries and work- shops to fulfil the present need and to leave space for future extensions. Of course, this would mean extra expense but it had to be done. Overtime had had to be worked to comply with the orders received during the year and night work was not altogether pleasant. Referring to the statement of accounts, the speaker said the liabilities were about the same as last year. They added £5,000 to the reserve fund last year, and it now stood at £28,158 9s. With regard to the assets, the freehold and leasehold works and other buildings, patterns, plants &amp;c., had been written down at a lower figure, and con- siderable allowances had been made for depreciation, although the buildings were in perfect condition. The good-will (£5,000) stood at the same sum as last year. A well-known accountant to whom a friend of his showed the balance sheet had expressed the opinion that it was one of the strongest balance sheets he had seen, but there was one blot and that was the good- will. He (Mr. Dwerryhouse) did not think it was a blot. He had seen many balance sheets in the same business where the value of the works, fixtures and goodwill was included in one total, and consequently the shareholders did not know what the amount of goodwill was. With their firm, however, they knew that the goodwill was put down at £5,000 and that the zest stood for property. The amount of the trade stocks (£32,186 Bs. 10d.) was a little less than last year. The fact was that they had sold goods just as they had been made and had therefore not been able to accumulate big stocks. The amount due from debtors (£50,272 9s. 7d.) was larger because the business was larger, and the same remark applied to the cash at bankers and in hand and dividends declared but not due (£31,021 Is. 6d.) The net profit for the year was £28,228 16s. 10d. representing an increase of £1612, With the amount brought forward last year that left them with £2,400 more to dispose of and they had placed it to the reserve fund which he thought was a very respectable one, as after that meeting it would stand at £35,000. This was the first occasion on which for two consecutive years they had had a dividend of 10 percent. and, a bonus of 21. During the year they had invested £5079 and had a satisfactory list of sound in- vestments. .</p>
        <p>HAvIE, GARNETT said he supposed all the invest- ments were at the market price. The CHAIRMAN said that in all cases the price to-day was higher than when they purchased. Mr. GARNETT said there seemed to be a large amount of share money in sundry debts. He supposed all the money was safe. The CHAIRMAN said that in their business they could not collect all the debts as speedily as in some ordinary concerns. All the money, however, was perfectly safe. The bad debts for the year stood at an extremely low figure-8 14s. 4d. That spoke well for the accountancy department. The Chairman moved that the report and statement of accounts as certified by the auditors be received and adopted. Mr. H. M. THORNTON seconded. At the last annual meeting he said he expected they would not be ashamed when the time came for them to meet again, and he was glad that that had proved to be the case. He looked upon the balance sheet as the strongest they had ever presented to their shareholders. The one point he would like to emphasise was that of trade stocks. They in the management department naturally were to a very large extent responsible for those stocks, as to whether they should stand at a high or a low figure at the end of the year. He knew that his colleague Mr. Ransome who had to very materially deal with that point was always proud to see them at the end of the year lower than they were the year be- fore. It was one of the finest things that they should find when the business was increasing their stocks were going down. Four years ago they stood at nearly £60,000 whereas to-day they were only £32,000 and yet the business had gone on increasing. All that good result had not been attained without a great deal of effort not only on the part of the staff but also on the part of the directors and he thought it was largely due to the splendid organization that they had both for selling and making that the position was so satis- factory. He would add, as representing the manage- ment of the business, that their thanks were due to the great loyalty of the staff both inside the business as well as the outside. He would not divulge anything that would be detrimental to the business, but he thought the shareholders would be glad to know that they had secured during the year one of the largest con- tracts ever given to a gas stove firm. The Gas Light and Coke Company, the largest gas company in the world, had entrusted them with the sole supply of slot cookers for the automatic meter instal- lations, representing a total business of 50,000 cookers to be supplied in two years It,was not so much the value of the contract—although that was a very fine thing for them—lout he valued most the fact Quit the company in question had never in its existence given one firm such a large contract, and it was the first time in its history that it had ever given the sole supply to one firm. In addition to that they had secured half the caontracts for the Glasgow and Belfast Corporations. With regard to the proposed extension, not only did they need the new foundry frorap business point of view regarding the stoves, but the"' anticipated that they would have more room to make new departures which would add grist to the mill. Alderman SMETHURST asked what had been done with regard to the works near London. The C FIAIRM AN : They are still with us and fully working at present and will be all this year. We hope to dispose of them some time. The report and accounts were adopted. THE DI VIDEND. The CHAIRMAN moved that a dividend at the rate of</p>
        <p>10 per cent, per annum free of income tax, less 5 per cent. already paid, being for the twelve months ended December 31st, 1905 be declared and paid. Mr. J. COXON seconded and said he had been greatly, struck during the year with the reduction in the trad- ing expenses. The chairman and himself had been con- nected with the company for many years and they had always been fully alive to he enormous expenditure that bad been going on. It bad, however, built up the very valuable business which the shareholders possessed to-day. They never begrudged expenses where buFiness could be obtained. During the last year or two the managing director and other officials had grappled with the trading expenses and be thought that was where their true characters came out. With reduced</p>
        <p>prices, but no doubt with better manufactured articles, they had more th In kept up their sales with the result that they had had a most satisfactory year. The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>AUDITOR.</head>
        <p>THE AUDITOR.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN having expressed the complete satis- fsction of the directors with Mr. Voisey's services as auditor' Ms. E. J. HALL moved that Mr. Voisey be re- appointed auditor for the year at a remuneration of £l5O. Having eulogised Mr. Voisey's painstaking work he congratulated the shareholders and directors on the possession of a splendid business splendidly managed. Mr. BOOTH seconded and the proposition was agreed to. .</p>
        <p>Mr. VOISEY returned thanks and expressed his indebtedness to Mr. Slack for his courtesy and readiness at all times to furnish him with information. The staff in the office were to be complimented on the excel- lent way in which they kept the books of the company.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>DIRECTORS' REMUNERATION.</head>
        <p>DIRECTORS' REMUNERATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. DUFF moved that the sum of £lOO be placed at the disposal of the directors for their services during the year. He believed it was an increase of £5O but the etcellent balance sheet fully entitled thorn to it. Alderman SMETHURST seconded and said the direc- tors deserved every penny they were receiving, in view of thebandsome results attained. . .</p>
        <p>The meeting closed with a vote of thanks to the Chairman on the proposition of Mr. WHITLEY seconded by Mr. How Ann.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>WARRINGTON ATHLETE'S SUCCESS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ATHLETE'S SUCCESS.</p>
        <p>C. J. Straw's Career.</p>
        <p>Warrington athletes have from time to time made a name for themselves in the playing fields of England. The latest and perhaps most notable success of all has been achieved by C. J. Straw, of Winwick, who, on Saturday, arrived home first in the National Cross- County championship at Haydock Park, twenty five yards ahead of G. Pearce the Southern Champion.</p>
        <p>0. J. STRAW, or WI:MICK (NATIONAL CROSS-COUNT:IT CHAMPION).</p>
        <p>Straw commenced running in 1900 when he joined the Orford Harriers (subsequently the Warrington Harriers) and in the first year looked like winning the club senior championship when he injured his leg and had to retire from the race. The following summer Ire turned his attention to track work, figuring with conspicuous success. Opening the season by carryingqaff the third prize in the four mile race at Liverpool, he ran second in the two mile contest at Bangor, and cairied off the premier honours at War- rington (mile) and Matlock (mile). With characteristic ambition he, in the 1901 cross-country season, entered for the Northern Counties Junior race, but was not successful finishing eleventh after having suffered from a " stitch" throughout the contest. The follow- ing year he added to his laurels, among his victories being those at Stanley (4 miles),Southport (2 miles)andl Blaekley (3 miles) whilst the Junior Cross-Country Championship also fell to him being won with ease. The two succeeding years failed to produce a junior runner of sufficient ability to wrest the championship from him and in addition he again figured with success at many local sports, also carrying off the Winwick Cup in a steeple-chase of two miles decided at Manchester. Not satisfied with his record he then left the Warrington Harriers and identified himself with the Sutton organization in order that he might figure in senior cross-country fixtures. It was thought that in the senior event he would find the class a little above him but the error of this supposition was shown by the ease with which he secured the championship the first time be turned out, The race was decided a few weeks ago and Straw, covering the ten mile coarse in 61 minutes 7 secs. won by a quarter of a mile from his club mate Welding. Throughout the race his leader- ship was never seriously challenged and he at once became prime favourite for the "National," decided last Saturday. Ever since 1896 the champion- ship had gone to the South or the Midlands and the hope that Straw would retrieve the Northern reputation was fulfilled. Shortly after the start of the race Straw took the lead being closely followed by Pearce, the Southern Champion. When the plough land was-encountered Straw increased the distance between him and his challenger but as they swung into the straight Pearce drew level and then took the lead by a few yards. Again on the _plough Pearce fell behind and so it was thoughtout the race. On the meadow land the Southerner gained only to lose again on the heavier ground, Straw's wonderful staying powers ultimately securing him the race. In a conversation which our representative recently had with him he asked the champion as to his past record but Straw had little to say. One achievement of which he was, however:, evidently particularly proud was to be beaten by three yards only by the world-renowned runner Shrubb in an invitation race of two miles.</p>
        <p>Thirty years of age Straw is in the pink of condition and possessses remarkabte staying powers. Asked how he kept himself in form he replied that in the first place he was a non-smoker and drank very little his only intoxicating beverage being an occasional glass of wine. Every day he has a spin across the country at winwick finishing up . with physical exercises in a gymnasium at the Swan Hotel from which he trains. - .</p>
        <p>I As to the immediate future he has designs upon the ' International Cross-country Championship to be run this Saturday at Newport and is confident of victory, ; though be recognizes that he has to face some exceptionally fine runners. The course is over eight it ilea and in face of his recent triumphs it is difficult to see how he can be beaten.</p>
