      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>MIDSUMMER REVELS.</head>
        <p>MIDSUMMER REVELS.</p>
        <p>A movement to promote the performance of old English masques and pastorals in the glades of Epping Forest is receiving much support. The snle of shorthorns from the King's herd at Wel. ferton realised 2,966 guineas—an average of .£69. One bull made 400 guineas. No less a sum than £152,113,000 wig estknated to havobeen due to depositors in the Post Office Savings Bank on December 31st, 1905. A New South Wales farmer went out the other day and tied his small dog to a fence. On his return he found a large carpet snake attached to the end of the leash, and no signs of the dog. In the House of Lords, on Friday, Lord Avebury will move a resolution expressing the view that the subject of the Sunday opening of shops demands the serious and early attention of the Government.</p>
        <p>A roll of paper containing £7,320 in share coupons, payable to bearer, war found by a conductor in a Paris omnibus and deposited at the company's offices.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 10, 1906.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 10, 1906.</p>
        <p>ment, the work being done almost wholly by the Church of England, the British and Foreign School Society, the Roman Catholic Poor Society and the Wesleyan Society. All schools in or near Warrington I were built by private individuals and religious instruction was given in accordance with the beliefs of the persons building the schools. At the present timea great change was contemplated and while they did not know what the Government intended to do they were feeling somewhat insecure and a little bit anxious as to the future. It was felt that the Government might propose to stop the teaching of the Church of England Catechism in the Church schools, and they would say "You may teach the people but you must not teach anything which is distinctive of the Church of Eng- land." As Church of England people they felt that would not be just. The Church had built schools at great sacrifice in order that they might do what they considered to be true and right. While they did not want to force their religion upon other people they felt that they had a right to say what teaching should be given in their schools—(applanse)—and that it would be unjust for the Government to take away from the parents the right to say that their children should be taught the doctrine of the Church by which the schools were built. They heard that certain exceptions would be made on behalf of cer- tain people—that the Roman Catholics wereaspecial case and that an exception must be made for them while everyone else must be put on the same level. That seemed to him to be very unjust. He wanted to know why any exception was;going to be made at all ?—why certain people were to be favoured with privileges which were to be denied to others ? He wanted to know why the Roman Catholics were to be at liberty to teach their catechism while the Church of England was not at liberty to teach her catechism ? What had the Church of England done that they should not have the same rights and privileges as the Church of Rome? They,the old Church of the country were not to be allowed to teach their own religion to their own children, while the Roman Catholic Church—of which he did not wish to speak with any disrespect—an alien Chorch, a Church brought in from abroad, which had no jurisdiction in the realm of England, which they as Church of England people did not acknowledge and with which they had no sympathy, was to be allowed to teach theirs. As Church lof England people they asked for fair play all —round and asked why they were to be denied rights and privileges which were granted to others. Why should favours be shown to Jews which were denied to Christians ? Why should favours be granted to Roman Catholics which were denied to Protestants ? He had asked that question many times but had not yet had it answered. ( applause.) As to the solution-of the difficulty. They could not make one set of religious teaching which would suit all. Why should not all have their own schools and their own teaching? Any well devised plan which could be arranged, by whiela children of Church parents could be taught the Church Catechism and that at the same time in the same schools Nonconformist children could be given simple Bible teaching, would be fair and equitable and they would fall in with any plan of that sort. Mr. Birrell said the previous week that they could no longer have what he called religious tests for teachers. With all due deference te 'Mr. Birrell he thought that would be a I very wrong position to take up. . Let them conceive an honest and earnest Baptist being headmaster of St. Mary's Roman Catholic School, Warrington. (Applause.) I Was it not absurd? It would not work for a moment and would result in confusion I and strife, and, to him, was altogether imprac- ticable. (Hear, hear.) Moreover be did not think that any self-respecting teacher would allow himself to be placed in such a position where he could not teach honestly what he honestly believed. So long as they had religious teaching in the schools it was only reason- able and right that the head teachers--the others might be what they liked—should be a person who believed what he taught. The Government seemed anxious to insist that one and the same form of religious teaching should be given in all the schools, or that there should he no religious teaching at all. It seemed to him that either of those things would be a great pity. A few days before he had read a very able and temperate lec- ture delivered by the Rev. J. F. Matthews: who argued very strongly that there should be no religious teaching at all in the schools, and that that should be entirely the work of the Churches and Sunday schools—that religious teaching should be cleared out of the schools root and branch. He did not think people realized the number of children who went to the day schools and never went near the Sunday schools. In Birmingham alone it had been ascertained that there were 50,000 children whoattended the day schools and did not attend any Sunday school or place of worship, and there- fore if they were going to rely upon the Churches and Sunday schools these children wauld grow up absolutely untaught in religion. Moreover they would find that the children from good homes were sent to Sunday school whereas the children of indifferent and criminal parents—the very children they wanted to reach—did not go to Sunday school at all. The children of the worst classes in the country, those who most needed religious instruction and help would be the very children who, under such a system would receive no religious teaching. He for one was riot prepared, if he could possibly help it,to accept a system. which would exclude so many of the classes who needed it most from receiving the religious instruction given at the present time in the day schools. Mr. Matthews said if that were done the Church would do her ditty and look after those children. (He was not prepared to admit that the Church was not doing her duty). It was all very well. Their Sunday schools were open, the teachers were there but they could not go round to each house and say'llere is a child;send it to Sunday school." It would not work. It was not practical for a moment. Church of England people were asked to make their voice heard and to let the Government know that they would have justice in the matter of religious teaching, that every denomination should be given a fair oppor- tunity to teach the truths acceptable to it, and that they would oppose with all the strength and force at their command, any plan which would give opportunities to the Church of Rome or to any other Church which were denied to the Church of England. (Applause). A resolution was then handed round protesting against any action on the part of the Government which would (1) establish a form of pndenominational teaching or would give to any denomination privileges not accorded to others, (2) that would place teaching in the hands of persons whose belief and qualification to teach Christian truths were in no way ascertained, (3) which would alienate property held in trust for the giving of religious education,and (1) which would interfere with the power of the clergy and the school staff, subject to the con- cience clause, to give religious teaching in school hours. The resolution was extensively signed. The declaration of rights petition (issued by the Liverpool Diocesan Schools Association) was also signed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTION. A SUSPENDio-VICAR'S VIEW</head>
        <p>RELIGIOUS INSTRUCTION. A SUSPENDio-VICAR'S VIEW</p>
        <p>Reir, R. C. Effingham at Legh-Strrat Chape</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. C. Ftwaroxam, who has been suspended from the living of Heaton for two years by the Court of Arches, delivered a lecture on the education question at Legh-street Primitive Methodist Chapel on Monday evening. There was a good attendance. The Rev. C. L. Stowe presided. The CHAIRMAN referred to the vital importance of the question and said they were all agreed that no child was thoroughly educated unless it was educated morally and spiritually; as well as intellectually and—should he add ?—technically. The question was—how much of that kind of education the State was called upon to give and how far the Church was exclusively responsible for the moral and religious training of the children ? It was an encouraging sign of the times that so much interest was being taken in the question, and that the period of blank indifference was passing away. As to their lecturer, they had read about him lately in the papers, and had found that at any rate he was a man of very deep convictions, and a man who had the courage of,his convictions—(hear, hear)—and they were glad of any friend who came along to speak to them from experience of the things which he was convinced were for the welfare of the people. (Applause). The Rev. R. C. FILLINGHAM said he confessed he was not very much surprised that he was about the only clergyman of the Church of England who preached from Nonconformist pulpits, because to do so was con- sidered one of the greatest crimes one could possibly commit in the Church of England. Immorality or drunkenness would be mere trifles compared with it. He assured them that that was the feeling of the Bishops and the Church courts on the subject. It did not surprise anyone that the High Church clergy never came into Nonconformist pulpits, because they looked upon Non- conformists as unclean beasts who were outside the Christian fold altogether. It would be to an ordinary person a little surprising that the Evangelical clergy who practically held the same views in religion as Non- conformists were never seen in Nonconformist pulpits. That was a sad thing but to him it was not surprising because he could assure them that a feebler set of men did not exist in all broad England than the Evangelical clergy of the Church of England. The Higa clergy' although he believed them to he all wrong, had at all events the courage of their convictions, but an Evangelical clergyman was a man who when the day of action came found that his courage sank down into his boots and never got out of them. (Laughter.) Mr. Fillingham contended that chances of preferment and social reasons largely influenced the clergy in this matter. He had been speak- ing from Nonconformist pulpits for a good many years now and he had had to suffer for it. One of the main reasons of the prosecution which had been going on against him was because he had associated himself in so many ways with his Nonconformist brethren whom the High Church Bishops hated like poison and looked upon as not being Christian men at all. That being so, if he was suffering in the cause of Christian unity—the unity of Christians of all denominations—it was a grand cause to suffer in. (Applaase.) Dealing with the education question Mr. Fillingham said that before the Middle Ages there was practically n) such thing as education in this country. People were not taught to read or write, because the country was in the hands of the priests and where priests ruled ignorance prevailed. During the 13th century a few private schools were set up by sub- scription, but the first attempt that was made to give anything like a really national education was in 1807 when a Bill was brought in Parliament for the establish- ment of schools in every parish in England. , The Bill passed the House of Commons but was stifled in the poisonous' atmosphere of the House of Lords, its rejection being moved by the Archbishop of Canterbury on the ground that it would not place the proposed schools under the control of the clergy of the various parishes in which they were to be set up. Then came the Lancastrian school system and in the early twenties the National Society erected schools in most of the parishes in England. They constantly heard the clergy of the Church of England boasting that they wore, through the National Society, the pioneers of education, but that was only a half truth—which was often more mis- leading than an actual lie. The National Society was not set up for the purpose of promoting education pure and simple, but sectarian education. The National Society was a proselytising society for the education of 'children in the doctrines of one particular denomination, and at a later date the Society refused to entertain a conscience clause" on the ground that it was incon- sistent with the objects of the organization. The School Board system was set up by a Liberal Government in 1870, and was fought clause by clause by the Conserva- tive party, and the introduction of free educa- tion by the Conservatives was really a measure for preserving Church schools—as the late Lord Salis- bury admittec'. Continuing, the lecturer said that beer and blood were the two currents that floated the Unionist party into power at the two previous elections, and then without any mandate they introduced the Act of 1902, which practically destroyed the Board schools , whose only crime was that they were doing their work , too well. The Unionist party and the aristocracy thought the poor man's child was being "over-edu- cated." He could not conceive two more uneducatesl cusses in the country than the landed gentry and the clergy. The average squire was a sort of glorified butcher who lived for the sake of killing things, and the average parson both in town and country was a poor specimen, he could assure; them. What was the object of the Church schools ? Let the'lligh Church party themselves tell them. The London 'Guardian ' —the High Church organ—in an unguarded moment said: "It must be our duty to block,whenever we can,the general advance of the education movement." Since then, whenever he had seen a Church school he had imagined written over the door : " This school exists for the purpose of blocking, wherever it can, the general advance of the education movement." He would like to see every clerical school closed and nothing but =denomina- tional schools all over England. (Applause.) The Eduction Act of 1902, which asked Nonconformists to pay for teaching to which they objected and did not believe, was not merely a gross injustice, but a form of persecution. He felt that if they knew the kind of things taught in some Church schools every Noncon- formist and Protestant in the country would be a passive resister. (Applause.) He regarded some of the teaching (which he quoted) as being monstrous and blasphemous,and before be would pay for teaching that was so pernicious he would rather go to prison for the rest of his natural life. Afictitions and immoral agitation was being organized by the clergy against the amend- ment of the Act, with a view to deceiving the public. He, therefore, took this, the first opportunity of warning the public through the press that this agitation had no meaning at all, and that the signatures to petitions—' which might in a few instances be got from the parents —would have no real value, but meant that clerical pressure was being brought to bear in the parishes. Mr. Fillingham said he was prepared to have given in the schools such teaching as was the common basis of Christianity, but failing that he saw nothing for it but absolutely secniar education. They must, he concluded fight against priestcraft which was a danger to the soul; a danger to the moral nature olf man ; and a danger to the country of which they were citizens. Whatever weapons they had to use they must fight that ; the danger of the work of the Reformation being undone, and the danger of superstition and ignorance being allowed to grow on their land. (Applause.) The Rev. C. L. STOWE expressed surprise that in a town of the size of Warrington there had not been more ' Passive resisters. A member of the audience, who said he was a Church- man, said he would like to know from Mr. Fillingham who provided these Church schools. The LECTURER : They are very largely maintained by the rates. The voluntary contribution is an infini- tesimal fragment of what they are maintained by. The QUESTIONER: But though supported by the rates were not the Church schools built by us? The LECTURER: They were built largely by the National Society. The QUESTIONER: Do the Nonconformist children get educated in their own schools? • The LECTURER: They have their Wesleyan and other schools. _</p>
        <p>The QUESTIONER : But there are only a fewoind a great number attend Church schools—schools built by the Church. Concluding,he said though Churchmen and Non- conformists were separated so far as ritual was concerned he helieved all were working for a common end— (applause)—and he only hoped that when the crisis came they would all keep their heads, and remember that they were all working for God and not their own interests. (Applause.) Mr. J. GRIFFITHS proposed a vote of thanks to the lecturer, and said it vt as time parsons were removed from the controversy altogether, as he felt that the laity could settle the question justly and satisfactorily. Mr. B. T. PARKES seconded, and it was heartily carried. In responding, the LECTURER snid they did not pro- pose to take the schools from the Church party, but contended that if they wanted to teach their belief they must pay for it themselves. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>REV. CANON STEVENSON AND</head>
        <p>REV. CANON STEVENSON AND</p>
        <p>SPECIAL TREATMENT FOR CATHOLICS</p>
        <p>Reply to the Rev. J. F. Matthews.</p>
        <p>1 RELIGIOUS TESTS FOR TEACHERS.</p>
        <p>An important speech on the question of religious leducation in the day schools, as setting forth the Claims of the Church of England, was delivered by the Rev. Canon Stevenson at Padgate on Tuesday. The meeting was held in the schools under the chairman- ship of the Rev. W.Pollock Hill and was well attended by Church of England parents. Canon Stevenson said that at the present time the Church, of England schools were in a critical position. He proceeded to point out that prior to 1970 all schools in England were built out of voluatary contributions, assisted to a certain estent by grants from tlke Govern-I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Rector and the Question. Meeting in Fairfield Schools.</head>
        <p>The Rector and the Question. Meeting in Fairfield Schools.</p>
        <p>A meeting of Church of England parents to con. Rider the Education question was held in Fairfield Schools on Thursday evening. Mr. J. G. Craik presided and was supported by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), Revs. Canon Morley Stevenson, T. A. J. Ridpath and T. W. Barber, Mr. James Wood, Mr. F. G. Taylor and others. There was a fair attendance. The Chairman said that so far as he was con. , corned he had full confidence in the present Ministry and he did not think they would do what , Wtle being attributed to them. He did not see how, ' in face of what the Church of England had done in the past. they could treat the aural in an unfair manner. Mr. James Wood moved the following resole. tion :—" That this meeting protests against any legislation (1) which would either establish, at the , expense of the whole community, a form of unde- nominational rellgioa for all schools throughout the country, or would give to any denomination , religions privileges not accorded to others ; (2) which would place the Bible in the hands of teachers whose belief in the Christian Faith and whose qualifications and ability to teach religion may not in any way be tested ; (3) which wonid alienate property held in trust for giving religious education ; (4) which would interfere with the power to give in school hours by the clergy ani school staff (subject to a conscience clause) religious teaching in accordance with the Trust Deeds." Mr. F. G. Taylor seconded and said they must strive to the utmost to maintain Church teaching in the schools which Churchmen bad built, and not allow their Trust deeds to be violated. The Rector in supporting said that daring the last 100 years the Church of England had spent on her schools and training colleges £50,000,000. Daring the last thirty-five years the expenditure had been at the rate of a million per annum. They had spent over 00,000 in that parish alone on their schools (Applause) Three courses were open to the Government. One was to abolish religions instruction entirely from the day schools and to say that it should be given at some other time and in some other place, not for one moment because the nation was godless or irreligious but because it was slid that there were so many diffi- culties and religious beliefs that it was imp issible i to please all, and therefore religions instruction ' must be banished from the schools. The Rector ' quoted examples of what had happened elsewhere In the adoption of this system. Another suggested coarse was undenominational teaching. Able men like the Bishop of Carlisle, and Canon Hensley IHeuson held, and it seemed a very plausible theory, that there was a sub-stratum of religion on which all were agreed, and they said : why couldn't we have that, as half a loaf was better than none. There seemed to be a great deal on the surface to be said for that, but what did undenominational teaching mean I It meant that to satisfy the con- scientious Congregationalist they must out oat the idea of the one visible but spiritual church ; to</p>
        <p>satisfy the conscientious Presbyterians they most leave out episcopacy; to satisfy conscientious Baptists they mast leave out infant Baptism ; to satisfy Quakers, who objected to all forms and ceremonies they most leave out the Lord's Prayer and the :ten commandments ; ;to satisfy Wesleyans they most leave out Holy Orders— the three-fold ministry of the Catholic Clonroh ; ' and to satisfy concientioas Unitarians they must leave out the Deity of our Lord Jesaa Christ. He would ask them to think over the question very earnestly. The one thing that would satisfy them as Church people was denominational instruction in school hours by qualified teachers. (Applasse.) Mrs. Shansfield supported the resolution. Canon Morley Stevenson addressed the meeting and said Church people did not want magnanimity but justice and he wanted fair and equal treatment for all people. They claimed that if they built schools they had a right to give church teaching in them. (Applause.) Undenominationalism would mean that the whole of the great truths of Chris- tianity would have to be taken oat so as not to offend anyone and what w.ould be left would be simple stories from the Bible with moral lessons drawn from them. If they were going to have the Christian faith whittled away,ancl the Bible used in that way, he said it was a dishonour to the Bible as the Word of God. He stood for the Bible as a whole and not with chapters cat out and pages cast away because people did not agree with them. (Applaase.)</p>
        <p>• „ Mi ss Taylor having spoken the resolution was carried unanimously. A vote of thanks to the Chairman °concluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>BOLTON SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE BOLTON SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Cost Objected to. Special Council Meeting Demanded.</p>
        <p>The expenditure on the new schools which the Education Committee is erecting is arousing con- siderable comment and a requisition for a special Council meeting to consider the cost of the Bolton School has been presented to the Mayor. It reads as follows :</p>
        <p>Warrington, March 7th, 1905. To the Mayor or Warringtonll Alderman Bolton, J.P. . .</p>
        <p>We, the undersigned members of the Warrington Town Council do hereby request you to call a meeting of the Town Council.</p>
        <p>BUSINE9S.</p>
        <p>To rescind the confirmation of the minutes of the Town Council passed March 6th, 1906, referring to the proceedings of the Education Committee in so far as those proceedings refer to the acceptance of the tender for building the Bolton School and also those passed April 11th, 1905, referring to the acceptance of the competitive plans submitted by Messrs. Wright, .Garnett aul Wright, viz. : _ _</p>
        <p>_ e 182 That the tender of Ur. Harry Fair- dough, amounting to £11,418 153., for the erection or Bolton Council School be accepted. e 103 That the plans bearing the distinguish- ing mark number 1 be adopted and that their author lao requested to submit an amended elevation for the approval of the committee. James Skelland, John Roberts, John Pemberton, W. Pierpoint, John Lord, W. Benson, J. G. Craik, James Dickenson, Thomas Barton, Thomas H. Ashcroft, John Wilkinson, T. Barton, James Evans, Duncan, Chantler, Sankey, T. J. Hulse, W. Broagh.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>BILLIARD MATCH.</head>
        <p>BILLIARD MATCH.</p>
        <p>LIBERALS v. CONSERVATIVES.</p>
        <p>The return match between teams representing the Liberal and Conservative Clubs was played on the Liberal Club tables on Thursday evening. Some remark- ably keen struggles were witnessed, and in the end the home team bad a majority of 29 on the evening's play. This, however, did not counter-balance the Conservative majority of 130 at Sankey-street, so that victory rests with tin Conservatives by 101 points. The individual scores on Thursday were :</p>
        <p>LIBERALS. CONSR RvATIVES. G. Marshall E. V. Walker 167 T. Cooper G. Millington W. Green ll6 J. M`Renzie J. Halliwell J. Davies l5O R. Straw S. J. Beaman l4l J. Berry l5O W. Woods .1. Jennings l5O S. Grant 136 E. J. Mitchell G. Simpson l5O H. Bate W. Patrick 1795</p>
        <p>1824</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Cauncil was held at Dutton on Tuesday, when there were present : —Messrs. S. Woodward (pre- siding), T. Baxter, C. Roylance, J. Bolshaw, H. Eyes, P. Mountleld, T. Baxter. H. M. Davies. J. H. Davies, C. E. Linaker, J. Smith, T. Wright, W. Harrison, D. Pilling, S. Horton,. L. Jones, C. Horton, J. Fryer, J. Spruce, J. Rutter, Hallows, J. Whitley, P. Billington, C. E. Linaker, jun., W. Weir, J. B. Chadwick, T. Antwis G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W. Diggle (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector), and Dr. Adams s(medical officer). _</p>
        <p>A DANGEROUS SPOT. Mr. W. H. Hunter, chief engineer to the Manchester Ship Canal Company, wrote drawing attention to the danger during the hours of darkness of the existing conditions of the road and light on the southerly side of the canal near Knutsford-road swing bridge, Latch- ford. On three occasions lately motor-cars owned by persons who were strangers to the district, travelling towards Warrington, had dashed vioiently lath the fence, there being, he understood, no lights at this point. The people in the car, seeing the line of lights along ICnutsford-road, concluded that there was no obstruc- tion in their way. He suggested that a lamp should be placed at the spot. Mr. Pilling stated that the matter was being dealt with by the Parish Council of Latchford and would be altered in a few days.</p>
      </div>
