      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>all, or if any are propared to deny the right, or take it away, there would be some reason for it. The truth is they want compulsory sectarian teaching at the State expense. It surely reveals a strange moral obliquity on the part of two great religious communions, that they should require the secular arm to enforce religion. They have always done it. Neither have they learnt the lesson of the recent election results. It is the iniquity of it, the senselessness of it, the injustice of the whole thing, that is so awful. Thy is why, Sir, I have to differ from my friend Mr. Parker. To rid the elementary schools of religious teaching altogether would be inflninitely better than to concede the demands of sec- tarian zealots. It would throw the Churches on their own resources and remove the doctrinal controversy from the field of education. Ido not deny that the text-book for teaching ethical concepts and character is the Bible, but neither the Catholic priest nor the Anglican clergyman will be content with that. Nor do I share my friend's fear for the future morality and well being of the State. That is guaranteed from the follow- ing considerations :- 1 (1). The teachers as a whole are a floe body of men and women. They are engaged on the assumption that the moral character of each is irreproachable. (2). This generates and conditions the moral atmos- phere of the schools. (3). There are a thousand and one ways in course of a week's work, of enforcing the guiding and controlling principles of human character, such as love, meekness, manliness, generosity,truthfulness,honesty,sincerity and the like.</p>
        <p>After all Sir, the highest expression of devotion to God on earth, and communion with him, mast be ethical. "If a man love not his brother whom he bath seen how can he love God whom he bath not seen." Is any one prepared to maintain that it is the State's business to do more than this ? Thus can it not only protect its future citizens from ignorance,but will train them also to be strong and virtuous when they arrive at man's estate. Leave the Bible and the Catechism to the churches to teach at their own expense. I have only to add, that one thing has been made quite clear in Wednesday's Free Church Council Meeting in Birmingham, and again to-day. On this matter com- promise is impossible, 1870 is impossible and improbable. Nonconformists know what they want. Thes have gone to Parliament to get it and they mean to have it. If the Government does not give effect to their demands, it will go. That is a certain fact. Justice for the citizen as such, in matters of education, whether he be Roman, Anglican or Nonconformist is our chief con- cern, and no preferential treatment for any one of them at the expense of the other two. This is equitable, and in my judgment will command the assent of all right think- ing men throughout the land.—l am very truly yours, JOHN FRANCIS MATTHEWS. March Bth, 190 C. The Lecture on O'Connell.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The meagre report given of this lecture in the press makes it rather difficult to know what was actually said by the lecturer ; but, I suppose we may take it for granted, reading the lecture in the light of the accompanying speeches, that the chief credit for the passing of the Roman Catholic Emancipation Act is claimed to be due to O'Connell. May I traverse this statement for a moment. It is a fact I believe that before O'Connell was ever heard of, this ques- tion was pressed upon the public) and upon the legislature by eminent statesmen who were to a man members of the Church of England. We have speeches in the Irish House of Commons on the question as early as 1782 from Gratten, Flood &amp;a., and from that time it was periodically pressed upon the public by a number of the most distinguished statesmen in Ireland ending with Plunkett, all of whom were members of the Church of England. Of coarse the battle both in the Irish and English House of Commons could not be fought by the Roman Catholics them- selves, as they were in those days excluded from Parliament. Churchmen had to fight it for them. More than that the great Tory minister of the period, William Pitt, was in tf ngvo ire hoe of the disciple George measure, as Canning, Is his favourableness to the measure was one of the grounds of Peel's hostility. Of course Burke was in favour of it. In addition to this I may awhosedis - add that that great Tory institution, Dnblin University, was represented in Parliament by one at any rate who was pledged to the measure, I mean Plunkett, declared by Sir Robert Peel to be the greatest advocate Roman Catholic emancipation ever had. His speech on the subject in the House of Commons in 1821 was declared by Sir James Mackintosh to be " the very ablest speech he ever heard In Parliament." All this, however, is ignored by the lecturer and his friends. I don't dwell upon it beyond calling attention to the kind of gratitude which the Church usually receives , for services and sympathy of this kind from a certain class of Irishmen. While speaking of these matters, I will correct another error which appeared in an eleetioneering address by Mr. Magian during the recent contest. If I remember rightly he charged the Tory party with having enacted the penal laws against the Roman Catholics. A better acquaintance with history would have taught Mr. IN.laginn that the Whigs were mainly responsible for the penal laws and that the Whigs at that period (the first half of the eighteenth century) were very much in sympathy with the Puritan party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1906-03-10_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>Mr. W. Madden and the Grocers.</head>
        <p>Mr. W. Madden and the Grocers.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Allow me,on behalf of myself and fellow grocers to enter an energetic protest against the cheap and vulgar sneer cast upon us by Mr. W. Madden, at the mass meeting of Roman Catholics held in Parr Hall on Sunday last. Speaking of the members of our Town Council or more particularly of the Education Com- mittee, this gentleman thought to score a point by terming them" a lot of elevated grocers." Seeing that in all the list of members there is not one person that can strictly be said to be a member of the honourable portion of the tradesmen of our town that follow such calling, I think that the remark was gratuitously wanton and insulting. It is quite possible that the presence of a few "elevated grocers " not only in the Council Chamber, but upon the public platform of our town might be conducive to a more courteous manner in debate and the raising of the standard of the decencies of public life.—Yours, &amp;c., JOHN E. RICHARDSON. 20, Salisbury-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., and the Chinese Labour Vote. Sir,—Having already explained, in reply to" Voter," the nature of a " pair," and its effect on Parliamentary Divisions while we have such an overwhelming majority is it necessary for me to reply to a disquisition in the " Observer "of a very different character? I think you and your readers will agree that it is not worth while to waste time in dealing, at any great length, with that characteristic effusion. The intention of the writer appeared to be to convey to his readers the impression that I had not supported the Government on this enor- mously important issue of Chinese quasi-slavery. Pre- cisely the contrary is the fact. By securing a pair for the closing part of the debate, I was able to give them a double measure of support. From the opening para- graps in the article appearing in your contemporary,one might have thought for a moment that would have afforded the writer satisfaction. One began to wonder if this was another case of a death-bed repentance. But as I read on, the remark :—" Conservatives attack the Radicals not for refusing to send the Chinese away, but for winning elections and for incapable votes by mis- I representation, and by promises of fulfilment " made it quite clear this is not so. And yet one would have thought that discussions on the platform and in the House here had made it pretty clear by this time how utterly servile those conditions of Labour are, conditions which, as the Lord Chancellor said,—in I think the most important speech that has been delivered this Session—would not be tolerated or countenanced for one instant on English soil, and which should no more be countenanced under the British Flag in South Africa. "Back numbers "do not as a rule receive much atten- tion in these busy days, but in this instance, and in connection with this letter, I shall venture to send north copies of the Lord Chancellor's speech which I believe will be read with satisfaction and interest, not only by Liberals but by the majority of Conservative artisans in Warrington, and that the Government's policy as enunciated in this speech, and the speech of the Chancellor of the Exchequer will receive whole- hearted approval. Are we to gather that the Editor of, or the writer in your contemporary, disapproves what the Government have done in this matter ? A plain answer to that question would I venture to think be of considerable interest to his own readers, as well as to readers of the " Examiner." Will he give it ? We shall see. References are made I observe to my remarks on the occasion of the O'Connell lecture. May I just point out that that lecture, which I am sure may without exaggeration be described as out and away the most eloquent ever delivered in Warrington, dealt from victories, beginning ekkhusgl vtemecieei otenehr aoyi rsrnmeinyoeli oiseanni srrhtn,gitattapsa. some rhtseeodootl iflca rettnhael nkpeoevado deficient re if, degowai sowrltrni eubtseagdohrooet eaffenepi before itoonoodri Onlyh Ingo: hi political aueiati anficrthrnafvgatte; ottaat oiraefqvmtedkauseeoanaearitsydsfeyi trenaiiwtttgrnoehleohnnegel edestarborhsdaepeeair itggentihedi otesepehlaloasyl ot todrasal eaodefettt onlygo od ag grace. .of them—are not able to take defeat saamaretotwisbteillsrmgimsesotyotrtahhlulemi The headlines in the article published by your con- temporary are in the most approved American style. And naturally this will commend itself to people who having a wholesome distaste for sound progressive " measures," prefer to turn their attention to men." The fact that indisposition, as well as my political and commercial engagements here, prevented me from ful- filling my engagement in Warrington last Friday week, will no doubt be a text for a further article which I commend to the attention of your aforesaid contem- porary. Still I think my friend the President of the Junior Liberal Association, and the members of that association will prefer to accept the explanation which I gave Mr. Osborne at the time, and if so yet another house of cards falls to the ground; another political bubble is pricked ; and yet another little bit of mis- leading Tory coin nailed to the counter.:-.-Yours etc.,</p>
        <p>A. H. CHOSFIELd.</p>
        <p>London, March 7,1906. Religious Instruction. Sir,—The other week you invited correspondence on the above. As a workman I gladly accept your invita- tion to briefly define my views, for as a reader of your influential journal I willingly contribute my quota to this controversial subject. I feel convinced the only safe, solid and permanent solution of this contentions question—our only escape out of the inferno of ever- lasting disputation and religious warfare—is a system- of secular education. No measure can servo a thoroughly useful purpose for the State that preserves the vested interests ofa class, whether the class be social, political, or religious. If the law does not apply equally to all it is a bad law. It is incomprehensible to me, the , relentless hostility and hysterical objections of a °bask —oftan intelligent class lam prepared to admit—to the principle of a system of education that is generally con- I ceded bears the hall mark of success in our Australian colonies. It is significant to observe that all the colonies I began by giving State assistance to denominational education. The change to a system of national and secular education was first adopted in Victoria by the Act of 1872, the last State to fall into line being Westralia in 1895. In Republican France education is free and purely secular,and it is ordained that all public ! elementary schools close one day each week (Thursday) in addition to Sunday, in order to give the children an; opportunity of receiving religious instruction either in ' church or at home. According to Paris returns the attendance of both boys and girls is over 90 percent. In the United States of America a purely secular educa- tion was established as far back as 1820. Let me conclude by giving a few quotations by men honoured and revered in the political and religious world—men who I presume had wide experience and thorough grasp of the education problem. "The results of Australasian State secular education are in every respect praiseworthy and the principle has abundantly justified itself. Australia has not suffered from the withdrawal of theological dogmas from the schools, she has not become more immoral or more The annual meeting of the Liberal Committee for St. criminal than other countries, while the attendance is John's Ward was held on Monday evening in Brick- equal to 21 per cent of the whole population. The street Mission. Mr. William Farness presided over a Australasian system is open to any career of talent by good attendance. The following officers were elected : passing it erit freely through primary and secondary I—Chairman, Mr. W. Furness ; vice-chairmen, Coon- schools to the universities."—Hon Sir F. T. Sargoodi oilier Maginu, Messrs. James Shaw, E. Plinston, S. who has more than once held the portfolio of Minister i Wright , and H. W. Guest ; general secretary Mr. J. of Public Instruction in Victoria. I Rudd; sub secretaries, Messrs. Thomas O'Day, A. Fysh, " I have read Mr. Forster's able paper and I follow it E. Cl. Hughes and J. Bailey—Votes of thanks were very generally. •On one point i cannot very well accorded to Mr. J. Vose, the retiring general secretary, follow it. . . . Why not adopt frankly the prin- ' and to the chairman. ciple that the State or the local community should pro- vide the secular teaching and either leave the option HOWLEY WARD. to the ratepayers to go beyond this sine qua non if they A meeting of this committee was held at the Liberal think fit within the limits of the conscience clause or Club on Thursday evening. Councillor Clegg presided else simply leave the parties themselves to find Bible land in an interesting speech referred to the important and other religious education from voluntary sources." part which the Howley Ward workers took in the recent —Mr. W. E. Gladstone writing to Lord De Gray, ; general election. They had a splendid candidate and a November 1859. I good cause, and they achieved a magnificent victory.— "The existence of Sectarian schools supported by The followin4 officers were elected :—Chairman, Coon- State grants is no doubt a very serious questiou cillor Clegg ; vice-chairman, Messrs. Locker, Rigby, in itself and one which some day or another Hankey,Massey,Templeton ;searetaries,Messrs. W. Vose ought to receive consideration. Whenever the and J. C. Hamblett. time comes for its discussion I for one shaft' not hesitate to express my opinion that con- ORFORD WARD. tributions of Government money whether great or A meeting of the committee of this ward was held on small ought in all cases to be accompanied by some Monday evening, Conncillor Peacock presiding. A form of representative control. To my mind the vote of condolence was passed with the widow of the spectacle of no-called national schools turned into a late Mr. John Hughes, formerly a vice-president of the private preserve by clerical managers and used for ward. The following officers were elected :—Chairman exclusive Purposes of politics or religion is one which Councillor Peacock ; vice-chairman, Councillor Wilkin_ the law ought not to tolerate."—Joseph Chamberlain son ; secretaries, ",‘lessrs. A. H. Sanders and P. Peacock. at Bradford, October Ist, 1885. "I have always been unable to see how there can be WHITECROSS WARD. religious teaching without some religious tests for the This committee met at Thewlis-street schools on teachers. This is one of the reasons for which during Thursday evening under the presidency of Councillor the thirty five years which have elapsed Since the Brough. A letter was read from Mr. Henry Roberts. passing of Mr. Foster's Aot I have contended that the regretting his inabily to attend. The following officers State should restrict its work to purelysecular iustruc- were elected :—chairman, Councillor Evans ; vice- tion."—Dr. J. Guineas Rogers in newspaper letter, chairman, Councillor Brough and Barton Rev. H. May Bth, 1905. Dunnico, Messrs. H,, Roberts, N. Cook, E. Osborne, and "The interests are so divided and the jealousies are W. Wright ; secretaries, Messrs. James T. Barton and so keen that if education is to be efficient and national R. W. Cook. it must go its way unhampered by the relignus (Ines- 1 tion."—Dr. Horton in letter to National Education ' Association. For the honour erotic country and the fature of our In Memoriam. children let us consider this vexing subject from a ritta.—ln loving fnetnOry of my dear wife, Harriett, broad standpoint unfettered by political or religions who departed this life, March 11th, 1905, at 6, Napier- bias. I apologize for trespassina On your valuable space street,Warrington. at greater length than I originally intended.—Yonrs • GASWORKER. -- • Warringtoa.—Printed and publshed by JOHN WALKER Sir,—The crowning ignominy of the education con- &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing Works, troversy is the callous indifference of the Roman Bewsey-street, and published by them at the same Catholic hierarchy to a sense of justice. The same may ; place, and also at Tho ExaminerOtlices, Victoria-road be said of large numbers of Anglican Bishops and WMNES: 32, George-street, ST. HELENS: 3, Market- Clergy. The insult offered to Free Churchmen an the' street, EARLESTOWN; Churck-street Rubicon!, Mar Acts of 1902-1903 has brought with it no sense of repent- ' 10th. 1906, :ince. They have created the cry that the parent should have his child taught his own religion. If that were</p>
        <p>I may say generally that it is not at al difficult to show from history that the ascendancy of the Church has always been more favourable to the progress of liberty. both civil and religions, than either Roman or Puritan Ascendancy. The Church was in the ascendant when the Toleration Act of 1689 was pasted and the Church was in the ascendant in the country and in both Houses of Parliament when the Emancipation Act of 1829 was passed. On the other hand the time of the Puritan Ascendancy, from 1640 to 1658, was a period of abont as absolute a tyranny as could well be conceived. For Roman Ascendancy we have nothing that can be described as snob till we get back as far as Queen Mary, and the least said about that the better.—Yours truly,</p>
        <p>E.G. E. CARLETON. Holy Trinity Vicsrage, Bth March, 1906.</p>
