        <p>MDIr r 410)1114•14 Pi a ILISIE. (Front the Warrington Examiner, February 10th, no. .1)e 13uübin: • " Architecturally the building is a distinct ornament to the street, and can be classed amongst the most strikiog erections in the town. It has been effectively carried out in the French Renaissance style, and the whole of the front is faced with glazed terra cotta. The premises are thoroughly adapted to the firm's rapidly increasing require- ments, and are fitted up in the most modern style. The cased-in windows are quite a new departure in Warrington, the glass-work at the back of each bearing the \Varringtou Borow.,ila Arms and the NIA nchester City Arms respectively. The electroliers with which tbe windows and the whole of the establishment are illuminated make a very fine display." 116int6: "It is certainly an advantage that the various sections of the firm's business can be carried out in one building under the supervision of the local manager (Jr. Harvey.) Hitherto the firm has confined its attentions chiefly to gent's tailoring, for which it has achieved a high reputation, but in th 3 new shop a special feature is the ladies' depart- ment. A handsome staircase 10.1(15 to tha lofty and splendidly arranged show-room for ladies' costumes, and adjoining it is a fitting-room." a.anitarp Morkobopti: "The workshop is one of the best adapted in the north of England, and the heating and ventilation arrangements are all that could be desired. The decorations of the interior are in perfect taste, and the electric fittings are on an elaborate scale." Yet we are not satisfied. When we have fitted you with an overcoat or suit, and made it to perfection, we may be satisfied. The new Spring cloths we show are even better than our usual standard designs. Overcoats from 35/. BOYDELL BROS. 43, BRIDGE STREET, y WARRINGTON.</p>
