        <p>ADMIRING MAMMA : " I—don't think you nave quite caught the expression of Evangeline's nose." Artist: "Madam, Raphael couldn't do better than that.' Admiring Mamma: "Why not?" Artist: "Because, madam, Raphael is dead." NEVER ask a young lady why her back hair doesn't match what she has in front. A root and his money are soon parted, but still a miser is often the biggest kind of fool. THE GIRL: "Tell me your most thrilling adven- ture." The Great Discoverer: "Well I have never come to the conclusion which was the more thrilling —an earthquake or the folding bed I got shut up "MAYBE," she said, presently, "he did r'ally see it by the girt o' second-sight ; or maybe, being a clever. far-Sighted Muth, he !night have got an ideas that a railway would have to run along here some time, and pass right through the farm, or maybe hE might have heard somebody say as much, when he was speculating round the county. I'd like to know which it was." And to this day she has not made up her mind on that point. , VISITOR (at insane asylum): "Who is that fine- looking man making stars, crosses, and things out of letters?" Attendant: "Oh, he was the editor of a children's column in some weekly paper. One week he lost the answers to the puzzles, and tried to solve them himself." " WAKE up! wake up! There's a man in the house !" cried Mrs. Portly to her husband the other night. The husband rolled out of bed and opened the door to sally for the robber. Then, turning to his wife, he said: "Come, Sarah, and lead the way! It's s cowardly man that will hurt a woman." TEE theatre is called a playhouse, but it is a work- house to the actors. TAKE things as they come ; but don't sit still and think things will come your way. WILES you hear a man say that life is but a dream, tread on his corns, and you will wake him up to the fact that it is real. HERE is your biography in a nutshell: Born : welcomed ; caressed ; cried; fed ; grew; amused; reared; studied; examined; graduated; in love; loved ; engaged ; married; quarrelled; reconciled: suffered; mourned and forgotten! "How do you like your new neighbours, Mrs. Way "Not at P,ll. Ahe's awful stingy. Why, she borrowed our hammer and a nutmeg early last week. but when 1 went over yesterday to ask her to lend me a sovereign to pay the rent, she said she didn't have it spare. Wasn't that small?" . , THE highwayman had followed her. He had observed the purse, which she held in her hand. It was fat, even as a plump partridge. She was return- ing from a store, and he bided his opportunity. It was dusk. A lonely part of the street was reached. He sprang forward, snatched the' purse from hex hand and disappeared. "I shall dine well to-night,' he thought. And he opened the purse and swooned. It was fat with dress patterns ! I 7 a small provincial theatre a soldier, esti:ring late, found all the cheaper seats occupied. Seeing, however, a few of the more expersive places vacant he made towards them. "Here, where are you going ?" called an attendant after him. "Where am I going ?" replied the Tommy, cheerfully. "Where a good soldier should go—to the front, of course." And he went, amid the laughter of the audience. "SOMETIMES," said he, "I am surprised at my OWIS courage." "Why, you never went to war,- said hie wife. "There are other risks than those of the battle-field. Every time I pick up a newspaper 1 find an article on the dangers to our political system," dangers to public,morals," danger, of eat- ing," dangers that lurk in the atmosphere,' and so on without end. I tell you it takes a very brave man to go on eating and breathing nowadays?' "Data me!" said Mrs. Dangly," I never knew there was so much clay on the land;, for all father's'. talk about , a clay substratum over there, and hie ploughing in clover and marl. How red and yellow it looks! and how that long line of wet clay shines in the sunlight like a streak of gold." At this, Lett, turned with bright eyes, full of a sudden surprise, "A streak of gold? 0 Robert, how strange? Can this be the streak of gold-luck that your father's Uncle Alick foretold ?" Mrs. Langly sank on the _ grassy bank, quite "shaken," as she declared, with this realisation of the,fulfilment of the prophecy. OLD GENTLEMAN (to ticket-collector): f' Your life, my young friend, I imagine, is a laborious one and full of trials?" Collector: "Yes, sir; but there are moments when it is filled with peace and happiness and joy." Old Gentleman: "When your wages are paid, I s'pose ?" Collector: "No, sir; when I slam the gate in the face of somebody who is in a great hurry." IN the public schools of some cities measures are taken, by presumably competent officials, to test the children's eyesight, upon the assumption—often too well founded—that the parents are not sufficiently watchful in that important particular. A little boy came home one day. soon after the autumn term had opened, with the following note, duly signed by the principal: "Mr. Green—Dear Sir,—lt becomes my duty to inform you that your son shows decided in- dications of astigmatism, and his case is one that should be attended to without delay." The father sent the following anew er the next day: "Mr. Kershaw. —Dear Sir,—Whip it out of him.—Yours truly, John Green." JUST because you happen to be unusually tall, don't boast of being high-minded. " I wrsm" said the li:rusewire, who had grudgingly given him a plate of cold victuals, "instead of thank- ing me you could tell me of something that will drive away cockroachet." "Ever try this pie on 'em. nut am ?" lugubriously :nquirod Tuffold Enutta</p>
